 The desktop shell frame on the real runtime -- H4's first rung. The
 guios desktop proved a 19-app shell in the emulator, but it leans on
 codex-vm devices no real machine has: a host-side triangle rasterizer
 at MMIO 0xBE000000 with doorbell ports 0x400-0x402, absolute mouse
 coordinates synthesized on ports 0xE1-0xE4, and frame pacing by
 port-224 VM exits. This chapter is the frame those apps will land on,
 built only from what the Option A runtime actually provides: the GOP
 framebuffer from the handoff cells, keyboard and mouse from the H2
 USB stack (or PS/2 through the same mailbox), the PIT tick cell for
 pacing, and the CMOS RTC for wall-clock time.

 There is no frame loop running at a made-up rate. The desktop is
 event-driven: the loop polls the keyboard mailbox and the mouse pump
 -- both allocation-free when idle -- and repaints exactly what an
 event dirtied. The clock redraws when the tick cell says a second
 has passed. A machine showing an idle desktop executes a poll loop
 that allocates nothing and writes nothing.

 We say:

Section: Palette and Layout

 Three of these are the Seattle Seahawks brand colours exactly, and the
 rest are a ramp of College Navy so nothing on the desktop is a blue that
 belongs to nobody. The exact ones are the bar (College Navy 002244), the
 body text (Wolf Grey A5ACAF) and the hint line (Action Green 69BE28).

  dk-col-bar : Integer = #002244
  dk-col-wall : Integer = #001428
  dk-col-task : Integer = #000E1C
  dk-col-win : Integer = #003366
  dk-col-title : Integer = #004488
  dk-col-text : Integer = #A5ACAF
  dk-col-bright : Integer = #FFFFFF
  dk-col-hint : Integer = #69BE28
  dk-col-warn : Integer = #FFB040

  dk-bar-h : Integer = 22
  dk-task-h : Integer = 28

 `dk-task-h` is the band's FLOOR, not its height. It was chosen for the eight
 by sixteen bitmap cell, and a ppem is a device size that does not halve when
 `ui-wscale` doubles: at `s` = 2 the 28 logical band is 56 device against a 21
 device cell and has room to spare, at `s` = 1 it is 28 device against 18 and
 the entry inside it lays out at 16. One logical constant cannot serve both,
 which is why this is a measurement and not a bigger number.

 `dk-task-px` answers the band in DEVICE pixels. `desk-run` lays the real
 taskbar out once through `dk-task-init` and caches what the engine gave it,
 so the theme's own padding is never written down here; `desk-content-h`
 records what happens to an arithmetic that assumes one scheme's padding, and
 `desk-bro-h` reached the same conclusion from the other side. A cell nobody
 wrote reads zero and falls back to the floor, so a caller with no `ds` behaves
 exactly as it did before this existed.

  dk-task-band-cell : Integer = 76

  dk-task-px : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-task-px (ds) (w) =
    let c = peek-32 ds dk-task-band-cell
    in if c <= 0 then dk-task-h * ui-wscale w else c
  dk-win-w : Integer = 360
  dk-title-h : Integer = 20
  dk-body-y0 : Integer = 32
  dk-line-h : Integer = 18
  dk-para-gap : Integer = 6

  dk-win-pad-b : Integer = 30

  dk-palette : Palette
  dk-palette = Palette {
    pal-bg = dk-col-wall,
    pal-fg = dk-col-text,
    pal-primary = dk-col-hint,
    pal-secondary = dk-col-title,
    pal-accent = dk-col-bright,
    pal-muted = dk-col-task,
    pal-error = dk-col-warn,
    pal-success = dk-col-hint,
    pal-warning = dk-col-warn,
    pal-border = dk-col-bar
  }

Section: Appearance

 Two `ds` cells hold what the desktop looks like: a scheme index and a bitmask
 of adornments. They are cells rather than a record because every pane already
 reads its state out of `ds` with `peek-32`, so no pane signature had to grow a
 second parameter to carry a theme. Cell 48 is `desk-gpu-cell` and is not free.

  dk-scheme-cell : Integer = 52
  dk-adorn-cell : Integer = 56
  dk-sound-cell : Integer = 64
  dk-sound-on-cell : Integer = 68
  dk-icons-cell : Integer = 72

 THE QUIET PANES NEEDED A BLOCK AND HAD NONE. Windowing a pane costs no
 allocation when it already keeps state, because the window slot at 44 through
 60 is free space its block was carrying anyway. The Calendar and the Diffusion
 pane keep NOTHING -- that is what makes them quiet -- so a window is the first
 thing either has ever had to remember, and each takes a cell here pointing at
 a 64-byte block of which it uses only that slot.

 They are allocated in `desk-run` before the base mark for the reason every
 other pointer in this block is: a pane exit restores the heap to that mark and
 a cell still pointing above it is a stale pointer the next visit will use.

  dk-cal-cell : Integer = 84
  dk-dif-cell : Integer = 88
  dk-sty-cell : Integer = 92

 The Issues pane is the fourth of these and it keeps state without keeping a
 BLOCK: its filter and its selected row are `ds` cells 32 and 40, two bare
 integers, so there was nothing for a window slot to sit in. Same shape as the
 three above.

  dk-trk-cell : Integer = 96

 The Monitor is the fifth and it is a different shape again: it kept no block
 because it DREW rather than built a tree, and its block holds two facts the
 painter cannot reach as well as the window slot. `desk-wnd-paint-all` is
 handed the screen and `ds` and nothing else, so `kbd` and `mouse` are not in
 scope where this pane's tree is built. What it wants of them is whether each
 was enumerated, which is settled before `desk-run` takes the base mark and
 cannot change afterwards, so the answer is written into the block once at
 open rather than threaded through nine call sites for two booleans.

  dk-mon-cell : Integer = 100
  dk-mon-kbd-cell : Integer = 0
  dk-mon-mouse-cell : Integer = 4

 The two 3D panes take one each, and they need a block for the window slot
 alone: everything else either lives in `ScenePane`, which `DeskApps` carries,
 or is derived from the rect these cells hold. Two cells and not one, because
 two focus ids over one block would be two windows over one rectangle, which
 is the collision the cascade exists to prevent and no registry row can see.

  dk-scene-cell : Integer = 104
  dk-fish-cell : Integer = 108

 The Browser is the first HEAVY pane with a window, and its block holds the
 window slot and nothing else for the same reason the 3D panes' do: a heavy
 pane's own state is a typed record in `DeskApps`, which a cell cannot hold.

  dk-bro-cell : Integer = 112

 Files is the last pane to need a cell and it needs one for the same reason
 the Browser did. The EDITOR needs none: its block is the one `desk-edit-cell`
 has always pointed at, which grew to 192 bytes so that 44 through 60 could be
 the desk's.

  dk-files-cell : Integer = 116

  dk-adorn-border : Integer = 1
  dk-adorn-gradient : Integer = 2
  dk-adorn-shadow : Integer = 4
  dk-adorn-round : Integer = 8

 All four adornments on. This was 11, which is border, gradient and round
 with SHADOW left off, so the one adornment that makes a control look like
 it sits above the ground was the one nobody saw. It was never unreachable:
 the Appearance pane has always offered it and a user could always turn it
 on, which is also why turning it on by default is safe rather than a
 decision taken away from them. `s` in that pane puts it back.

 Looked at before it was flipped, at 1600x900 with a face on the ESP: what
 `shadow-subtle` draws here is a hard two pixel offset rectangle and not a
 blur, because this path has no alpha and says so, and at that size it
 reads as an edge under the control rather than as a smear.

  dk-adorn-default : Integer = 15

  dk-adorn-on : Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  dk-adorn-on (flags) (bit) = bit-and flags bit /= 0

  dk-on-off : Integer, Integer -> Text
  dk-on-off (flags) (bit) = if dk-adorn-on flags bit then "on" else "off"

  dk-adorn-flip : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-adorn-flip (flags) (bit) = if dk-adorn-on flags bit then flags - bit else flags + bit

 Schemes 0 to 3 are PALETTES over the desk's own surface style, and 4
 upward are whole design languages from `GopStyleKit` -- corners, relief,
 air and outline as well as colour. That is the difference between "lcars
 orange" and LCARS, and it is why the two kinds share one list: a person
 choosing an appearance is choosing one thing, not a colour and then a
 shape.

  dk-scheme-count : Integer = 6

  dk-lang : Integer -> DesignLanguage
  dk-lang (i) = if i == 4 then sk-flightdeck else sk-chevy

  dk-is-lang : Integer -> Boolean
  dk-is-lang (i) = i >= 4

  dk-scheme-name : Integer -> Text
  dk-scheme-name (i) =
    if dk-is-lang i then (dk-lang i).dl-name
    else if i == 1 then "terminal" else if i == 2 then "lcars" else if i == 3 then "minimal" else "seahawk"

  dk-scheme-palette : Integer -> Palette
  dk-scheme-palette (i) =
    if dk-is-lang i then (dk-lang i).dl-palette
    else if i == 1 then palette-terminal else if i == 2 then palette-lcars else if i == 3 then palette-minimal else dk-palette

Section: Appearance That Survives A Reboot

 The two cells above are the whole of what the Appearance pane edits, and
 until now the whole of what it lost: `desk-run` poked the default into
 `dk-adorn-cell` on every boot, so a user could pick a scheme, look at it,
 and find it gone the next time the machine came up.

 The cells stay the read path. A paint reads them with `peek-32` and costs
 nothing, and putting a keyed store on the frame budget to save two
 integers would be the machinery exceeding the cost. The store is touched
 exactly twice: once at boot, and once per keystroke that changes
 something.

  dk-set-scheme : Integer = 1
  dk-set-adorn : Integer = 2
  dk-set-sound : Integer = 3

  dk-settings-keys : List SettingKey
  dk-settings-keys = [
    gst-int-key dk-set-scheme "colour scheme" 0 0 (dk-scheme-count - 1),
    gst-int-key dk-set-adorn "adornments" dk-adorn-default 0 15,
    gst-int-key dk-set-sound "sounds" 0 0 1
  ]

  dk-settings-of : Integer -> SettingsStore
  dk-settings-of (ds) =
    let s0 = gst-store dk-settings-keys
    in let s1 = gst-or-keep s0 (gst-set s0 dk-set-scheme (peek-32 ds dk-scheme-cell))
    in let s2 = gst-or-keep s1 (gst-set s1 dk-set-adorn (peek-32 ds dk-adorn-cell))
    in gst-or-keep s2 (gst-set s2 dk-set-sound (peek-32 ds dk-sound-on-cell))

  dk-settings-save : Integer -> [Device.Port] Boolean
  dk-settings-save (ds) = gst-save (dk-settings-of ds)

  dk-settings-into : Integer, SettingsStore -> Integer
  dk-settings-into (ds) (st) =
    let sp = poke-32 ds dk-scheme-cell (gst-get st dk-set-scheme 0)
    in let ap = poke-32 ds dk-adorn-cell (gst-get st dk-set-adorn dk-adorn-default)
    in poke-32 ds dk-sound-on-cell (gst-get st dk-set-sound 0)

Section: Sound

 The controller reads the descriptor list and the samples by DMA after the
 call that starts the stream has returned, so both have to outlive the
 playback rather than the call. `desk-loop` restores the heap on every poll,
 so the click is rendered once at boot into a block the base mark cannot
 reach, and the click itself only writes registers.

  dk-snd-blk-buf : Integer = 16
  dk-snd-blk-len : Integer = 20

  dk-snd-init : Integer -> Integer
  dk-snd-init (ds) =
    let fx = snd-click
    in let n = hda-sample-rate * fx.snd-duration / 1000
    in let blk = alloc-zeroed 32 128
    in let buf = hda-square-tone-at (fx.snd-freq) n (hda-amplitude * fx.snd-volume / 1000)
    in let b0 = poke-32 blk dk-snd-blk-buf buf
    in let b1 = poke-32 blk dk-snd-blk-len (n * 4)
    in poke-32 ds dk-sound-cell blk

  dk-snd-play : Integer -> [Audio] Integer
  dk-snd-play (ds) =
    let blk = peek-32 ds dk-sound-cell
    in if peek-32 ds dk-sound-on-cell == 0 then 0
    else if blk == 0 then 0
    else hda-play-pcm-at blk (peek-32 blk dk-snd-blk-buf) (peek-32 blk dk-snd-blk-len)

  dk-snd-click : Integer, Boolean -> [Audio] Integer
  dk-snd-click (ds) (clicked) = if clicked then dk-snd-play ds else 0

Section: Drawn Icons

 The chrome's icons are drawings rather than eight pixel masks, rasterized
 ONCE at boot at the size the gutter asks for. It has to be once and it has
 to be here: `desk-loop` restores the heap on every poll and a pane exit
 restores to the base mark, so artwork rendered during a paint would be freed
 while the cell still named it. This runs above the base mark, like every
 other pointer the desk keeps.

 The set and its order are `GopIcon`'s, not restated here: a name added there
 is rendered here without this chapter changing, which is the mistake the
 chrome's own icon list already made once by naming its six twice.

  dk-icons-size : Integer -> Integer
  dk-icons-size (blk) = peek-32 blk 0

  dk-icons-at : Integer, Text -> Integer
  dk-icons-at (blk) (name) = peek-32 blk (4 + 4 * gicon-drawn-slot name 0)

  dk-icons-render : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-icons-render (blk) (n) (i) =
    if i >= gicon-drawn-count then 0
    else let w = poke-32 blk (4 + 4 * i) (gicon-render (gicon-vec-named (gicon-drawn-name i)) n)
    in dk-icons-render blk n (i + 1)

 A DRAWING is rasterized at whatever size is asked for, so it does not have
 to be a multiple of the eight pixel stencil it replaced. Quantising it to
 `avail / 8` threw away up to seven of the pixels the gutter had: at ppem 16
 the gutter measures 15 and the old expression rasterized at 8, which is an
 icon a third smaller than the type beside it.

 Four fifths of the gutter and not all of it: an icon drawn at the full
 measured width touches the first letter of the label, because the gutter IS
 the space the label reserved and nothing else separates them.

  dk-icon-px : Integer -> Integer
  dk-icon-px (avail) = if avail < 8 then 8 else avail * 4 / 5

  dk-icons-init : Integer, GopFont, Integer -> Integer
  dk-icons-init (ds) (tf) (w) =
    let ws = ui-wscale w
    in let avail = desk-text-w tf ws desk-chrome-gutter
    in let n = dk-icon-px avail
    in let blk = alloc-zeroed (8 + 4 * gicon-drawn-count) 64
    in let sp = poke-32 blk 0 n
    in let r = dk-icons-render blk n 0
    in poke-32 ds dk-icons-cell blk

Section: Chrome

  dk-corner-r : Integer = 6

  dk-corner : Integer -> CornerStyle
  dk-corner (f) = if dk-adorn-on f dk-adorn-round then CornerRound dk-corner-r else CornerSharp

  dk-bdr : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Border
  dk-bdr (f) (c) (n) = border-uniform (if dk-adorn-on f dk-adorn-border then n else 0) c (dk-corner f)

  dk-grad : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Gradient
  dk-grad (f) (a) (b) = if dk-adorn-on f dk-adorn-gradient then gradient-v a b else gradient-none

  dk-shade : Integer -> Shadow
  dk-shade (f) = if dk-adorn-on f dk-adorn-shadow then shadow-subtle 0 else shadow-none

  dk-theme-of : Integer, Integer -> Theme
  dk-theme-of (scheme) (f) =
    if dk-is-lang scheme then sk-theme (dk-lang scheme)
    else dk-theme-palette scheme f

  dk-theme-palette : Integer, Integer -> Theme
  dk-theme-palette (scheme) (f) =
    let pal = dk-scheme-palette scheme
    in let bdr = dk-bdr f (pal.pal-border) 1
    in let pad = edges-uniform 4
    in let mar = edges-uniform 2
    in let sh = dk-shade f
    in let base = widget-style-rich (pal.pal-bg) (pal.pal-fg) border-none pad edges-zero shadow-none (dk-grad f (pal.pal-bg) (pal.pal-muted)) accent-border-none
    in let btn = widget-style-rich (pal.pal-primary) (pal.pal-bg) bdr pad mar sh (dk-grad f (pal.pal-primary) (pal.pal-secondary)) accent-border-none
    in let btn-h = widget-style-rich (pal.pal-accent) (pal.pal-bg) bdr pad mar sh (dk-grad f (pal.pal-accent) (pal.pal-primary)) accent-border-none
    in let btn-p = widget-style-rich (pal.pal-secondary) (pal.pal-fg) bdr pad mar shadow-none (dk-grad f (pal.pal-secondary) (pal.pal-bg)) accent-border-none
    in let btn-d = widget-style (pal.pal-muted) (pal.pal-muted) bdr pad mar
    in let inp = widget-style (pal.pal-bg) (pal.pal-fg) (dk-bdr f (pal.pal-muted) 1) pad mar
    in let inp-f = widget-style (pal.pal-bg) (pal.pal-fg) (dk-bdr f (pal.pal-accent) 2) pad mar
    in let lbl = widget-style 0 (pal.pal-fg) border-none edges-zero mar
    in let sep = widget-style (pal.pal-border) (pal.pal-border) border-none edges-zero (edges-xy 0 4)
    in Theme {
      th-name = dk-scheme-name scheme,
      th-palette = pal,
      th-panel = state-styles-flat base,
      th-button = StateStyles { ss-normal = btn, ss-hover = btn-h, ss-pressed = btn-p, ss-disabled = btn-d, ss-focused = btn-h },
      th-label = state-styles-flat lbl,
      th-input = StateStyles { ss-normal = inp, ss-hover = inp, ss-pressed = inp, ss-disabled = btn-d, ss-focused = inp-f },
      th-gauge = state-styles-flat (widget-style (pal.pal-bg) (pal.pal-primary) bdr (edges-uniform 2) mar),
      th-separator = state-styles-flat sep
    }

  dk-theme-ds : Integer -> Theme
  dk-theme-ds (ds) = dk-theme-of (peek-32 ds dk-scheme-cell) (peek-32 ds dk-adorn-cell)

  dk-pal : Integer -> Palette
  dk-pal (ds) = dk-scheme-palette (peek-32 ds dk-scheme-cell)

  dk-wall : Integer -> Integer
  dk-wall (ds) = (dk-pal ds).pal-bg

  dk-gpu : Integer -> Integer
  dk-gpu (ds) = peek-32 ds desk-gpu-cell

Section: Window Metrics

 The welcome window is hand-drawn rather than laid out, so it is the one
 surface that has to do its own fitting. Its width in columns does not change
 with the scale, so its pixel height is the text height times the scale, and
 at scale 3 that is 1194 pixels against a 1080-pixel panel -- taller than the
 screen, with its title bar off the top. `dk-win-s` therefore picks the
 LARGEST scale that fits rather than trusting `ui-scale`.

 It is centred in the CONTENT area, which since the sidebar went is the whole
 width of the glass.

  dk-win-fits : Integer, GopFont, Integer -> Boolean
  dk-win-fits (h) (tf) (s) = dk-win-h tf s * s + dk-task-h * s + dk-bar-h * s <= h

  dk-win-s : Integer, Integer, GopFont -> Integer
  dk-win-s (w) (h) (tf) =
    let s = ui-scale w
    in if dk-win-fits h tf s then s
    else if s > 1 & dk-win-fits h tf (s - 1) then s - 1
    else 1

  dk-win-x : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-win-x (w) (s) =
    let left = dk-cbox-x w
    in left + (w - left - dk-win-w * s) / 2

  dk-win-y : Integer, Integer, GopFont -> Integer
  dk-win-y (h) (s) (tf) = dk-bar-h * s + (h - dk-bar-h * s - dk-task-h * s - dk-win-h tf s * s) / 2

Section: RTC

 The CMOS RTC answers on ports 0x70/0x71 on every PC, virtual or
 real -- no VM convenience involved. Registers 0, 2, 4 are seconds,
 minutes, hours, BCD-encoded. `rtc-read` and the guarded `rtc-seconds`
 come from GopRtc, which waits out Update-In-Progress and reads twice:
 a raw seconds read from a fast poll loop lands in the update window
 constantly and answers garbage.

  dk-bcd : Integer -> Integer
  dk-bcd (n) = (n / 16) * 10 + int-mod n 16

  dk-pad2 : Integer -> Text
  dk-pad2 (n) = if n < 10 then "0" & show n else show n

Section: Chrome

 The shell chrome built with the real widget system: a sidebar column of
 app buttons pinned 160 wide, and a right-hand column of flexible content
 over a 24-pixel taskbar row. The compositor lays it out against the real
 panel rectangle and paints it in software.

 Port 0x403 IN is the codex-vm host rasterizer's present probe: it reads
 1 under codex-vm, and bus-float on a real machine with no GPU driver.

  desk-gpu-cell : Integer = 48

 Named, not a literal, and that is the whole point of it. The 64-byte block
 holds cells 0 to 60, and on 2026-08-11 two lanes independently took 48
 because nothing in the file said it was spoken for. A literal offset is
 invisible to the next person; a constant shows up in one grep.

  desk-console-cell : Integer = 36

 The Edit pane's state block. `desk-edit` read this cell from the day it was
 written and NOTHING ever wrote it, so `ged-run` was handed zero and
 `ged-init` parked the editor's 8 MB buffer and 1 MB line index at physical
 address 0. It worked because nothing else on this platform uses those bytes.
 Measured 2026-08-15 on the desk under codex-vm: `peek-32 0 0` reads 0 before
 the pane is opened and 0x639100 after, which is the buffer pointer.

  desk-edit-cell : Integer = 28

 The desk's base heap mark, taken in `desk-run` after the state blocks above
 are allocated and BEFORE the first `root`. A pane visit restores to it on the
 way out, which is what makes the visit free rather than 20 KB: the desk never
 unwinds -- every pane tail-calls `desk-loop` again -- so without a restore the
 root that visit rebuilds is stacked on top of the root it replaced, forever.
 Nothing allocated after this mark survives a pane exit, so a pointer parked in
 a `ds` cell must be allocated in `desk-run` above.

  desk-mark-cell : Integer = 4
  desk-prog-cell : Integer = 44
  desk-clock-cell : Integer = 60

  desk-gpu-present : Integer -> [Gpu.Compute] Integer
  desk-gpu-present (dummy) = if gpu-in #403 == 1 then 1 else 0

  desk-task-clock-w : Integer = 220

 The `tasks` slot was an empty spacer until 2026-08-19. It reads the mark stack
 rather than a list of its own, which is what makes it correct by construction:
 an app is ALIVE exactly when it holds a live entry there, so the row cannot
 drift from the thing it reports. A dead entry takes `desk-focus-none` and names
 nothing. Only the five heavy panes ever push, so only they can appear.

 It is rebuilt by `desk-draw`, which runs at exactly the two moments the live set
 changes AND the desk is visible: `desk-app-hide` and `desk-app-close`. An open
 does not redraw the desk, and does not need to, because the pane covers it.

  desk-focus-name : Integer -> Text
  desk-focus-name (fid) =
    if fid == desk-focus-files then "Files"
    else if fid == desk-focus-bro then "Web"
    else if fid == desk-focus-scene then "3D"
    else if fid == desk-focus-fish then "Fish"
    else if fid == desk-focus-edit then "Edit"
    else ""

  desk-task-text : Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  desk-task-text (ms) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= desk-marks-depth ms then acc
    else
      let nm = desk-focus-name (desk-marks-id ms i)
      in let acc2 = if text-length nm == 0 then acc else if text-length acc == 0 then nm else acc & "   " & nm
      in desk-task-text ms (i + 1) acc2

 A PILL PER MINIMISED WINDOW, and it is a BUTTON IN THE TREE rather than
 something painted into the band. `desk-taskbar-clock` re-renders this whole
 subtree once a second to repaint the clock, so anything drawn into the band
 from outside the tree is erased within a second of appearing. Being in the
 tree also gives the pill its hit test for free: `ev-hit-widget` resolves it
 like any other button, so click-to-restore needs no rect arithmetic of its
 own and cannot drift from where the pill is drawn.

 The id carries the focus id because the label cannot: two panes could
 reasonably share a title, and the restore has to name a pane.

 EVERY OPEN WINDOW HAS A PILL, not only the minimised ones. Damian,
 2026-08-26: all apps should launch and put a pill in the taskbar, and Files
 does not pill, it is just text in the band.

 Those are one defect and the text WAS the workaround. The band carried two
 registers side by side: pills from the window registry for minimised windows,
 and a `tasks` LABEL reading the mark stack for whatever was alive. The label
 existed because the registry once held only the two windowed panes, so it
 could not see a live Files at all. Since 6.5 every pane is a window and the
 registry sees all fourteen, so the second register answers nothing the first
 does not, and it answered it as a word instead of a control you can click.
 The `tasks` slot is a plain spacer now.

  dk-pill-id : Integer -> Text
  dk-pill-id (fid) = "pill-" & show fid

 THE BAND IS ORDERED BY APP, NOT BY Z, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE REASON THIS WALKS
 IDS RATHER THAN THE REGISTRY. The registry's order IS the z order, so walking
 it put the most recently raised window last: clicking a pill raised its
 window and the pill then MOVED, out from under the pointer that had just hit
 it. Caught by a capture the first time a pill raise was tried -- Clock and
 Calculator swapped places in the band.

 A focus id is fixed per app, so ascending id is a stable order that no
 interaction disturbs. It also makes the band read the same way twice in a
 row, which is what lets a second click land on the pill the first one did.

  dk-focus-max : Integer = 16

  dk-pills : Integer, Integer, Integer, List WidgetNode -> List WidgetNode
  dk-pills (ws) (ds) (fid) (acc) =
    if fid > dk-focus-max then acc
    else if dk-wr-find ws fid 0 < 0 then dk-pills ws ds (fid + 1) acc
    else dk-pills ws ds (fid + 1) (list-push acc (widget-set-flex (widget-button (dk-pill-id fid) (desk-chrome-gutter & desk-wnd-title fid)) 0))

 The pill's icon is painted after the layout like every other one, keyed on
 the same id the button carries, so it cannot land anywhere but on the pill.
 It walks the REGISTRY rather than the laid tree because that is where the
 minimised set lives; `desk-icon-on` answers 0 for a window that is not
 minimised and therefore has no pill in the tree.

  desk-pill-icons : Integer, Integer, WidgetNode, Theme, Integer, GopFont, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  desk-pill-icons (base) (stride) (laid) (th) (ws) (tf) (ds) (i) =
    let wr = peek-32 ds dk-wr-cell
    in if i >= dk-wr-count wr then 0
    else
      let fid = dk-wr-at wr i
      in let d = desk-icon-on base stride laid th ws tf "" desk-chrome-gutter (dk-pill-id fid) (dk-pill-icon fid) ds
      in desk-pill-icons base stride laid th ws tf ds (i + 1)

  desk-taskbar : Integer, GopFont, Integer -> WidgetNode
  desk-taskbar (ds) (tf) (ws) =
    let mb = widget-button "menu" "   Cobblestone"
    in let menu = widget-set-flex (widget-set-min mb (mb.wn-min-w) (dk-task-btn-h tf ws)) 0
    in let tasks = widget-set-flex (widget-label "tasks" "") 1
    in let clock = widget-set-min (widget-label "task-clock" "") desk-task-clock-w 0
    in let kids = dk-pills (peek-32 ds dk-wr-cell) ds 0 (list-push [] menu)
    in let kids2 = list-push (list-push kids tasks) clock
    in widget-set-flex (widget-set-min (widget-panel "taskbar" DirRow 4 kids2) 0 dk-task-h) 0

 The entry asks for one LINE, in logical units, and the engine adds the
 theme's padding around it. `dk-line-step` is the leading the wrapped
 paragraphs already use, so the band inherits the same relation to the face
 that every other run of type on this desk has.

 It asks for nothing on the bitmap path. That path is not broken -- its cell
 is `comp-glyph-h * ws` and the 16 the entry gets today holds it exactly --
 and a request there would move `desk-taskbar-hit`, which runs faceless, for
 no defect.

  dk-task-btn-h : GopFont, Integer -> Integer
  dk-task-btn-h (tf) (ws) = if tf.gf-ok then dk-line-step tf ws / ws else 0

 The measured tree is a discarded frame and is bracketed, unlike the blocks
 `desk-run` allocates around it. Those are kept and pointed at from a `ds`
 cell; this one is read for one integer and never touched again, and it runs
 BELOW the base mark where nothing would ever reclaim it.

  dk-task-init : Integer, GopFont, Integer -> Integer
  dk-task-init (ds) (tf) (w) =
    let mk = __heap-save
    in let ws = ui-wscale w
    in let m = widget-measure (desk-taskbar ds tf ws) (dk-theme-ds ds)
    in let v = m.wn-min-h * ws
    in let d = __heap-restore mk
    in poke-32 ds dk-task-band-cell v

 The top bar is painted by `desk-topbar` OUTSIDE the widget tree, so nothing
 in the layout knows it is there unless it is told. `topgap` is that telling.

 The face reaches the CHROME only. A pane's own tree is handed through
 untouched because a pane that hit-tests its own subtree does so with plain
 `widget-layout` (`GopBrowser.codex`), and a cell fitted on one side of that
 pair and not the other puts a click on the wrong widget.

  desk-chrome-face : WidgetNode, Integer, Integer, GopFont -> WidgetNode
  desk-chrome-face (content) (w) (ds) (tf) =
    let s = ui-wscale w
    in let gap = widget-set-flex (widget-set-min (widget-panel "topgap" DirColumn 0 []) 0 (dk-bar-h * ui-scale w / ui-wscale w)) 0
    in widget-panel "root" DirRow 0 [widget-set-flex (widget-panel "right" DirColumn 0 [gap, widget-set-flex content 1, comp-fit-text (desk-taskbar ds tf s) tf s]) 1]

  desk-chrome-with : WidgetNode, Integer, Integer -> WidgetNode
  desk-chrome-with (content) (w) (ds) = desk-chrome-face content w ds gfont-none

 Every chrome render goes through here, and the reason is the taskbar clock.
 The band is painted from the widget tree, whose `task-clock` label is empty by
 design because the clock's TEXT is hand-painted over it once a second. So any
 chrome render ERASES the clock, and `desk-loop` runs `desk-clock` before the
 step, which means the pane wins the iteration and the band stays blank until
 the second turns over. Worse, the desk gates on `rtc-seconds` and the Clock
 pane on `rtc-seconds-unguarded`, so the two see the turnover on different
 iterations and which lands last was a race: measured, the clock was present at
 26 seconds open and absent at 14.

 Setting the gate cell to a value no second can equal makes the next
 `desk-clock` repaint unconditionally, and the next one is microseconds away
 rather than up to a second. The cost is one band repaint per chrome render
 instead of one per loop iteration, which is why it is done here and not by
 forcing the clock every time round.

 Each label carries two cells of leading gutter for the icon that lands at
 cell zero, the way a launcher row carries one after its selection mark.

 `desk-icon-on` answers 0 for an id the laid tree does not carry, so the
 `shutdown` line paints in the start menu and nothing on a plain desk.

  desk-chrome-gutter : Text = "   "

  desk-chrome-icons : Integer, Integer, WidgetNode, Theme, Integer, GopFont, Integer -> Integer
  desk-chrome-icons (base) (stride) (laid) (th) (ws) (tf) (ds) =
    let g = desk-chrome-gutter
    in let a = desk-icon-on base stride laid th ws tf "" g "menu" "menu" ds
    in let b = desk-icon-on base stride laid th ws tf "" g "shutdown" "power" ds
    in desk-pill-icons base stride laid th ws tf ds 0

  dk-chrome-paint : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, WidgetNode, Integer -> WidgetNode
  dk-chrome-paint (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (tree) (ds) =
    let laid = comp-render base stride font tf (desk-chrome-face tree w ds tf) (dk-theme-ds ds) w h (ui-wscale w)
    in let ic = desk-chrome-icons base stride laid (dk-theme-ds ds) (ui-wscale w) tf ds
    in let inval = poke-32 ds desk-second-cell (0 - 1)
    in laid

Section: Drawing

  desk-line : Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, Integer, GopFont, Integer, Integer, Text, Integer -> Integer
  desk-line (base) (stride) (h) (font) (s) (tf) (x) (y) (t) (fg) =
    if tf.gf-ok then gfont-text tf base stride h x y t fg
    else gop-draw-text base stride font x y t fg s 0

  dk-wrap-cols : Integer -> Integer
  dk-wrap-cols (s) =
    let c = (dk-win-w * s - 20 * s) / (8 * s)
    in if c < 8 then 8 else c

  dk-break : Text, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-break (t) (limit) (i) (best) =
    if i >= limit then best
    else if is-whitespace (char-at t i) then dk-break t limit (i + 1) i
    else dk-break t limit (i + 1) best

 The panel is sized from the text rather than the text trimmed to the
 panel. The body used to be four hand-broken lines drawn WITHOUT the
 wrapper: 44, 45 and 47 characters against a 42-column width, so three of
 them ran 50 pixels past the right frame, and the wrapped hint below them
 fell 26 pixels past the bottom of a fixed 160. Both were measured off the
 glass. A hand-broken line cannot be checked by anything, so there are no
 hand-broken lines here now.

  dk-wrap-count : Text, Integer -> Integer
  dk-wrap-count (t) (cols) =
    let n = text-length t
    in if n <= cols then 1
    else let b = dk-break t cols 0 0
    in let cut = if b <= 0 then cols else b
    in let skip = if b <= 0 then cut else cut + 1
    in 1 + dk-wrap-count (substring t skip (n - skip)) cols

  dk-para-intro : Text = "The Cobblestone desktop frame on the bare runtime. Input arrives over USB HID or PS/2; the clock runs on PIT ticks and the CMOS RTC. No emulator devices and no frame pacing."

  dk-para-hint : Text = "The Cobblestone button on the taskbar opens the start menu, and closes it again: every app by group, with Shutdown at the foot of it. An app opens in a window you can move by its title bar, and close with the x. Files browses this stick, Edit opens a source file, Console takes commands."

  dk-para-font : GopFont -> Text
  dk-para-font (tf) = if tf.gf-ok then "Font: TrueType CMUNSS.TTF from this stick's ESP." else "Font: CBF bitmap (no TTF on the ESP)."

 The line step in DEVICE pixels. It was a flat `dk-line-h * s` that never
 consulted the face, which was 36 against a 41 row cell at ppem 32 and is 36
 against a 21 row cell now, so the same constant was first too tight and then
 half again too loose. Five quarters of the cell is ordinary leading; the
 bitmap fallback keeps the old constant because its cell really is
 `comp-glyph-h * s`.

  dk-line-step : GopFont, Integer -> Integer
  dk-line-step (tf) (s) = if tf.gf-ok then tf.gf-gh * 5 / 4 else dk-line-h * s

  dk-body-lines : GopFont, Integer -> Integer
  dk-body-lines (tf) (s) =
    let cols = dk-wrap-cols s
    in dk-wrap-count dk-para-intro cols + dk-wrap-count (dk-para-font tf) cols + dk-wrap-count dk-para-hint cols

  dk-win-h : GopFont, Integer -> Integer
  dk-win-h (tf) (s) = dk-body-y0 + dk-body-lines tf s * dk-line-step tf s / s + 2 * dk-para-gap + dk-win-pad-b

  dk-wrap-draw : Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, Integer, GopFont, Integer, Integer, Text, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-wrap-draw (base) (stride) (h) (font) (s) (tf) (x) (y) (t) (fg) (cols) =
    let n = text-length t
    in if n <= cols then desk-line base stride h font s tf x y t fg
    else let b = dk-break t cols 0 0
    in let cut = if b <= 0 then cols else b
    in let skip = if b <= 0 then cut else cut + 1
    in let l = desk-line base stride h font s tf x y (substring t 0 cut) fg
    in dk-wrap-draw base stride h font s tf x (y + dk-line-step tf s) (substring t skip (n - skip)) fg cols

  
 THE BRAND TAKES THE FACE, like the welcome caption and the taskbar clock. All
 three were `gop-draw-text` straight to the bitmap grid while every widget
 beside them was proportional, which is the split `comp-text` closed for the
 widget layer and nobody had closed for the desk's own hand-drawn chrome.

  desk-topbar : Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, Integer, GopFont, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  desk-topbar (base) (w) (stride) (font) (s) (tf) (h) (ds) =
    let pal = dk-pal ds
    in let bar = gop-fill-rect base stride 0 0 w (dk-bar-h * s) (pal.pal-border)
    in desk-line base stride h font s tf (8 * s) (4 * s) "Cobblestone" (pal.pal-accent)

  desk-window : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, Integer, GopFont, Integer -> Integer
  desk-window (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (s0) (tf) (ds) =
    let pal = dk-pal ds
    in let s = dk-win-s w h tf
    in let wx = dk-win-x w s
    in let wy = dk-win-y h s tf
    in let cols = dk-wrap-cols s
    in let tx = wx + 10 * s
    in let body = gop-fill-rect base stride wx wy (dk-win-w * s) (dk-win-h tf s * s) (pal.pal-muted)
    in let title = gop-fill-rect base stride wx wy (dk-win-w * s) (dk-title-h * s) (pal.pal-secondary)
    in let cap = desk-line base stride h font s tf (wx + 6 * s) (wy + 2 * s) "Welcome" (pal.pal-accent)
    in let y1 = wy + dk-body-y0 * s
    in let p1 = dk-wrap-draw base stride h font s tf tx y1 dk-para-intro (pal.pal-fg) cols
    in let y2 = y1 + dk-wrap-count dk-para-intro cols * dk-line-step tf s + dk-para-gap * s
    in let p2 = dk-wrap-draw base stride h font s tf tx y2 (dk-para-font tf) (pal.pal-accent) cols
    in let y3 = y2 + dk-wrap-count (dk-para-font tf) cols * dk-line-step tf s + dk-para-gap * s
    in dk-wrap-draw base stride h font s tf tx y3 dk-para-hint (pal.pal-primary) cols

  desk-draw : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, Integer, GopFont, Integer -> WidgetNode
  desk-draw (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (s) (tf) (ds) =
    let wall = gop-fill-rect base stride 0 0 w h (dk-wall ds)
    in let laid = comp-render base stride font tf (desk-chrome-face (widget-panel "content" DirColumn 0 []) w ds tf) (dk-theme-ds ds) w h (ui-wscale w)
    in let ic = desk-chrome-icons base stride laid (dk-theme-ds ds) (ui-wscale w) tf ds
    in let inval = poke-32 ds desk-second-cell (0 - 1)
    in let bar = desk-topbar base w stride font s tf h ds
    in let win = desk-window base w h stride font s tf ds
    in laid

 The band carries a vertical gradient under the default adornments, so a flat
 fill sized to the text leaves a seam. `comp-walk` over the taskbar node costs
 one band-height fill and three labels once a second, against the full
 `comp-render` of the chrome that this loop must not pay for at that rate.

  desk-draw-clock : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Text, Integer, WidgetNode -> Integer
  desk-draw-clock (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (t) (ds) (root) =
    when widget-find root "taskbar"
      is None -> 0
      is Just (tb) -> desk-taskbar-clock base w h stride font tf t ds tb

 IT REPAINTS THE CLOCK'S CELL AND NOTHING ELSE, AND THAT IS WHAT STOPPED THE
 FLASH. It used to `comp-walk` the whole taskbar subtree, redraw the menu
 icon the walk had erased, and only then draw the time, so once a second the
 band was rebuilt and the clock was ABSENT for the length of the walk. On a
 framebuffer with no back buffer the scanout catches that gap, which is
 exactly what a person sees as the clock flashing. Nothing in the band except
 the time changes on a tick, so nothing else is repainted: the pills, the
 `tasks` label and the menu icon are left alone.

 The erase is `comp-box` over the TASKBAR's box CLIPPED to the clock's cell.
 That is not the same as filling the cell: a panel's background may be a
 gradient computed across its whole box, so a flat fill of a sub-rect would
 seam. Handing `comp-box` the panel's rect and the cell as the clip paints the
 background it would have painted, only where it is wanted. The old bound was
 the screen, for the good reason that a band-sized clip on a WALK drops
 children whose styles reach outside the band; that reason does not survive
 the walk going away.

 The time is drawn through `desk-line`, so it takes the loaded face like
 every other run of type on this desk rather than the bitmap grid, and the
 right edge is measured with `gfont-text-w` instead of assumed to be eight
 pixels a character.

 How tall the drawn time is, which is what the cell centres it against.
 `gfont-text` places the top-left of a glyph cell of `gf-gh` rows; the bitmap
 path's cell was the 14 this used to assume, and that number was right for
 exactly one of the two faces.

  dk-clock-ink : GopFont, Integer -> Integer
  dk-clock-ink (tf) (s) = if tf.gf-ok then tf.gf-gh else 14 * s

  desk-taskbar-clock : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Text, Integer, WidgetNode -> Integer
  desk-taskbar-clock (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (t) (ds) (tb) =
    let s = ui-scale w
    in let ws = ui-wscale w
    in let th = dk-theme-ds ds
    in let cell = when widget-find tb "task-clock"
      is Just (n) -> comp-mul-rect (n.wn-bounds) ws
      is None -> comp-mul-rect (tb.wn-bounds) ws
    in let st = widget-resolve-style tb th
    in let bg = comp-box base stride cell (tb.wn-bounds) st (st.ws-bg) ws
    in let tw = if tf.gf-ok then gfont-text-w tf t else text-length t * 8 * s
    in let want = cell.lr-x + cell.lr-w - tw - 8 * s
    in let tx = if want < cell.lr-x then cell.lr-x else want
    in let ty = cell.lr-y + (cell.lr-h - dk-clock-ink tf s) / 2
    in desk-line base stride h font s tf tx ty t ((dk-pal ds).pal-accent)

Section: Clock Gate

 It was the tick cell (28672, ~18 ticks per second) until 2026-08-04,
 when the ASUS TUF answered `pit=979` twice, 3405 seconds apart: the
 timer interrupt stops on that board at about 54 seconds and never
 resumes, while IRQ1 keeps delivering and the RTC keeps counting. A
 repaint gated on a dead counter never fires, so the clock froze and
 the Monitor pane below painted one frame for the life of the pane --
 which reads as a hung desktop and was reported as one. The RTC is
 read off ports 0x70/0x71 with no interrupt, no PIC and no handler in
 the path; a machine that boots has one.

  desk-second-cell : Integer = 8

  desk-clock : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Integer, WidgetNode -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-clock (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (ds) (root) = act
    now <- rtc-seconds 0
    if now < 0 then 0
    else if now == peek-32 ds desk-second-cell then 0
    else act
      hh <- rtc-read 4
      mm <- rtc-read 2
      let st = poke-32 ds desk-second-cell now
      in desk-draw-clock base w h stride font tf (dk-pad2 (dk-bcd hh) & ":" & dk-pad2 (dk-bcd mm) & ":" & dk-pad2 now) ds root
    end
  end

Section: Monitor

 What this machine is, read from the live sources at paint time rather
 than from anything cached at boot. It is the first pane worth having on
 a board nobody has booted before: the geometry row alone answers the
 question the whole 2026-08-02 display campaign was about, and it answers
 it on the glass without a serial port.

 The key column's width, in LOGICAL pixels now rather than device ones. It
 was `100 * s` into `desk-line`, an absolute x offset from the pane's left
 edge; it is a minimum on the key label and the layout engine does the rest,
 which is what lets the pane sit anywhere on the screen rather than only at
 the sidebar.

  dk-mon-val-dx : Integer = 100
  dk-mon-tick-cell : Integer = 28672

  dk-yn : Boolean -> Text
  dk-yn (b) = if b then "yes" else "no"

 A ROW IS A PAIR OF LABELS, and the key carries `tone-primary` because the
 two colours were the whole of this pane's legibility: the key was
 `pal-primary` and the value `pal-fg` when both were drawn by hand, and a
 tone is how a tree says that. `theme-tone-fg` falls through to the style's
 own foreground for `tone-none`, so the value needs nothing.

 The width pin reads the label's own guess back for the height, because
 `widget-set-min` sets BOTH fields and a zero there hands the node whatever
 default its kind carries.

  dk-mon-line : Text, Text -> WidgetNode
  dk-mon-line (k) (v) =
    let kl = widget-set-tone (widget-label ("mon-k-" & k) k) tone-primary
    in widget-panel ("mon-r-" & k) DirRow 4 [widget-set-min kl dk-mon-val-dx (kl.wn-min-h), widget-set-flex (widget-label ("mon-v-" & k) v) 1]

 THE PANE READS THE MACHINE AND SAYS SO, and every row is a fact somebody
 standing at an unknown board needs before anything else: the geometry row
 alone answers what the whole 2026-08-02 display campaign was about, and it
 answers it on the glass without a serial port.

 `Esc returns.` is gone, and that is the trade every full-box pane makes
 (`works-desk-contract.md`): a windowed pane has a close button, a menu and
 Esc, so the hint costs a line to restate what the titlebar already shows.

  dk-mon-tree : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, GopFont, AcpiInfo, Text, Integer -> WidgetNode
  dk-mon-tree (ds) (base) (w) (h) (stride) (tf) (ai) (clk) (ticks) =
    let blk = peek-32 ds dk-mon-cell
    in widget-panel "mon" DirColumn 2 [
      widget-label "mon-title" "SYSTEM MONITOR",
      dk-mon-line "display" (show w & " x " & show h & "   stride " & show stride),
      dk-mon-line "framebuffer" ("0x" & hex-i64 base & "   scale " & show (ui-scale w)),
      dk-mon-line "input" ("keyboard " & (if peek-32 blk dk-mon-kbd-cell == 1 then "USB HID" else "mailbox") & "   mouse " & dk-yn (peek-32 blk dk-mon-mouse-cell == 1)),
      dk-mon-line "font" (if tf.gf-ok then "TrueType CMUNSS.TTF   cell " & show (tf.gf-gh) & "   baseline " & show (tf.gf-asc) & "   cap " & show (tf.gf-cap) else "CBF bitmap"),
      dk-mon-line "acpi" (if ai.ai-ok then "rev " & show (ai.ai-revision) & "   tables " & show (ai.ai-tables) else "no RSDP: stub, EBDA and BIOS area all empty"),
      dk-mon-line "lapic" ("0x" & hex-i32 (ai.ai-lapic) & "   ioapic 0x" & hex-i32 (ai.ai-ioapic)),
      dk-mon-line "power" ("S5 " & dk-yn (ai.ai-s5-ok) & "   reset " & dk-yn (ai.ai-reset-ok) & "   sci " & show (ai.ai-sci)),
      dk-mon-line "clock" (clk & "   ticks " & show ticks),
      dk-mon-line "memory" ("heap frontier 0x" & hex-i64 __heap-save & "   desk mark 0x" & hex-i64 (peek-32 ds desk-mark-cell)),
      dk-mon-line "stack" desk-stack-text,
      widget-set-flex (widget-label "mon-spacer" "") 1
    ]

 The stack half of the arena had no instrument, which is the half of stage 0
 that stayed open longest. It has one now, and it is a READ of two cells the
 compiler already maintains rather than anything new.

 `28736` and `4072` are `stack-min-rsp-addr` and `ram-size-addr` from
 `codex/compiler/Emit/X86_64Boot.codex`. They are literals here because this
 chapter cannot cite the compiler, so they are hand-synced and will go stale
 silently if that file moves them.

 **The reading is honest in an ordinary build rather than absent, and that is
 the point.** The chapter's boot prologue stores `ram-size` into the minimum
 cell UNCONDITIONALLY (`X86_64Chapter.codex:385`), and only the per-function
 prologue narrows it, guarded by `if st1.trace-alloc` (`X86_64.codex:12`). So
 the two cells read EQUAL whenever nothing ever wrote a lower RSP, and the row
 says so rather than printing a depth of zero as though it had measured one.

 **It answers now, in a `-Trace` build, since root threaded the mode word
 through `compile-to-cdx-with-exit-mode` at main 17934.** Measured 2026-08-20
 on this pane: an ordinary desk still reads never-narrowed, which is correct
 and not a fault, while a `compile.ps1 -Trace` desk reads
 `min rsp 0x00000000bdffd700   depth 10496 B`. The two builds are also no
 longer the same size, 1,156,287 bytes against 1,085,879, which is the
 per-function prologue being emitted at last.

 That the sizes were IDENTICAL is what this block used to record, on
 2026-08-19, as the evidence that no CDX the shipped path produced could
 narrow the cell: `opening.codex` passed the emitter's `trace` argument as a
 literal `False` and took no parameter that could carry the mode word. The
 diagnosis was right and the fix was the one prescribed; only the tense here
 was left behind, which is what a measured claim does when somebody else
 repairs the thing it measured.

  desk-stack-min-addr : Integer = 28736
  desk-stack-top-addr : Integer = 4072

  desk-stack-text : Text
  desk-stack-text =
    let mn = peek-qword desk-stack-min-addr 0
    in let tp = peek-qword desk-stack-top-addr 0
    in if mn >= tp then "never narrowed -- this CDX was not built with -Trace"
    else "min rsp 0x" & hex-i64 mn & "   depth " & show (tp - mn) & " B"

 Repainted on the same RTC-second gate the taskbar clock uses, so the
 time is live rather than frozen at entry. The `ticks` figure it prints
 is the PIT cell READ rather than waited on: on a board whose timer
 dies it stops climbing while the rest of the pane keeps updating,
 which turns a fault that used to freeze this pane into a number
 reporting itself. The bracket is what keeps an open
 pane from growing: every row builds Text that nothing outside the frame
 reads. `ai` is parsed once in desk-monitor, below the mark, because
 re-walking the ACPI tables every second would be work with no answer
 that can change.

 The monitor repaints once a second on the RTC edge, so the cursor is hidden
 across that repaint and put back after it. Without the hide, the second
 that redraws under a displayed arrow leaves the save buffer describing
 pixels that no longer exist, and the next pointer move stamps them back.

 Monitor is a STEP, not a loop. It handles ONE event and returns: 1 to say it
 is still open, 0 to say it is done. `desk-loop` keeps the keyboard, so the
 desk goes on polling, painting its clock and answering the mouse while this
 pane is up, which is the whole point of the model and the thing the arm
 measures.

 The bracket stays where `desk-mon-loop` had it, around one event rather than
 around one iteration, because that is the same allocation either way: every
 row builds Text that nothing outside reads. It matters more here, not less --
 `desk-loop` has no bracket of its own, so an unbracketed step would leak into
 a loop that never unwinds.

 `acpi-boot` moved INSIDE the step and now runs on the repaint edge instead of
 once at pane entry. Its old home was a `let` above the loop, which a step has
 nowhere to put: state that outlives a step has to live in `ds` or in
 `desk-run`, and `AcpiInfo` is a record, so parking it in a cell would need a
 cast that the state blocks in this file deliberately avoid. A table walk once
 a second inside the bracket is the cheaper of the two, and the prose that used
 to justify hoisting it was arguing against re-walking every FRAME, which is
 not what this does.

 A step keeps INTEGERS in `ds` cells and RAW ADDRESSES in the state blocks
 `desk-run` allocates. Neither can hold a typed value, and the five panes still
 running their own loops all carry one: Files a `Fat16Volume` and a directory
 listing and the stack of where it has been, Browser a `BrowserState`, Edit a
 volume and a filename, the two 3D panes a render target and a view. This
 record is the channel for those, threaded beside `root`, and it is why
 `desk-loop` carries it rather than a cell doing so.

 **Where a value in here LIVES, which is the question that decides whether it
 works.** A pane's `-open` runs after `desk-run` took the base mark and before
 `desk-loop` takes its per-iteration mark, so anything it allocates sits ABOVE
 the base mark and BELOW the frame mark: the per-iteration restore never
 reaches it and it survives every step for as long as the pane is open. That is
 exactly the lifetime a step needs and it needs no new machinery.

 **What it does NOT survive is a close**, and that is deliberate rather than a
 gap. `desk-app-close` restores to the base mark, which reclaims the pane's
 entry paint and its state together, because the app is going away. State that
 must outlive a close still belongs in a `ds` block allocated in `desk-run`,
 which is how the Calculator remembers its number and the Console its
 scrollback across visits.

 **And the limit worth naming before someone meets it.** Several apps alive at
 once, rather than one focused app with its state, would need the base mark to
 rise when an app opens and fall when it quits. The heap is a bump allocator
 with save and restore and nothing else, so that is a mark STACK, and a mark
 stack is only correct if apps quit in the order they opened. That is a
 separate step and this record does not pretend to it.

 **`GopEdit`'s 9 MB does not move in here and must not.** It is allocated
 lazily and its POINTER is parked in a `ds` cell; only Edit's TYPED values,
 its volume and filename, belong here.

 **This block said the cell has no restore, and that was wrong.** The pointer
 survives a restore because the cell is below the base mark; the MEMORY it
 names does not, because `ged-init` runs from `desk-edit-open`, which is above
 it. Measured 2026-08-19 and corrected in the Editor section below, where the
 close-path fix lives.

  FilesState = record {
    fs-vol : Fat16Volume,
    fs-entries : List Fat16DirEntry,
    fs-clusters : List Integer,
    fs-names : List Text,
    fs-sel : Integer,
    fs-top : Integer,
    fs-ptr : Integer,
    fs-mode : Integer
  }

  DeskApps = record {
    da-files : Maybe FilesState,
    da-browser : Maybe BrowserPane,
    da-scene : Maybe ScenePane,
    da-edit : Maybe EditState
  }

  desk-apps-empty : DeskApps
  desk-apps-empty = DeskApps { da-files = None, da-browser = None, da-scene = None, da-edit = None }

 Cell 20, free until val took it 2026-08-19; announced in the contract. The
 block is 128 bytes allocated in `desk-run` before the base mark: offset 0 is
 the depth, entry `i` is a focus id at `8 * i + 8` and its mark at
 `8 * i + 12`. A dead entry keeps its mark and takes id 0, which is
 `desk-focus-none` and can never name a live app.

  desk-marks-cell : Integer = 20
  desk-marks-max : Integer = 8

  desk-marks-depth : Integer -> Integer
  desk-marks-depth (ms) = peek-32 ms 0

  desk-marks-id : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  desk-marks-id (ms) (i) = peek-32 ms (8 * i + 8)

  desk-marks-mark : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  desk-marks-mark (ms) (i) = peek-32 ms (8 * i + 12)

  desk-marks-push : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  desk-marks-push (ms) (fid) (mark) =
    let d = desk-marks-depth ms
    in if d >= desk-marks-max then d
    else
      let a = poke-32 ms (8 * d + 8) fid
      in let b = poke-32 ms (8 * d + 12) mark
      in poke-32 ms 0 (d + 1)

  desk-marks-clear : Integer -> Integer
  desk-marks-clear (ms) = poke-32 ms 0 0

  desk-marks-find : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  desk-marks-find (ms) (fid) (i) =
    if i < 0 then 0 - 1
    else if desk-marks-id ms i == fid then i
    else desk-marks-find ms fid (i - 1)

  desk-marks-kill : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  desk-marks-kill (ms) (fid) =
    let i = desk-marks-find ms fid (desk-marks-depth ms - 1)
    in if i < 0 then 0 else poke-32 ms (8 * i + 8) desk-focus-none

  desk-marks-reclaim : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  desk-marks-reclaim (ms) (acc) =
    let d = desk-marks-depth ms
    in if d <= 0 then acc
    else if desk-marks-id ms (d - 1) /= desk-focus-none then acc
    else
      let m = desk-marks-mark ms (d - 1)
      in let p = poke-32 ms 0 (d - 1)
      in desk-marks-reclaim ms m

  desk-marks-any-live : Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  desk-marks-any-live (ms) (i) =
    if i < 0 then False
    else if desk-marks-id ms i /= desk-focus-none then True
    else desk-marks-any-live ms (i - 1)

  desk-focus-cell : Integer = 12
  desk-focus-none : Integer = 0
  desk-focus-mon : Integer = 1
  desk-focus-cal : Integer = 2
  desk-focus-style : Integer = 3
  desk-focus-calc : Integer = 4
  desk-focus-clkp : Integer = 5
  desk-focus-prog : Integer = 6
  desk-focus-dif : Integer = 7
  desk-focus-trk : Integer = 8
  desk-focus-con : Integer = 9
  desk-focus-files : Integer = 10
  desk-focus-bro : Integer = 11
  desk-focus-scene : Integer = 12
  desk-focus-fish : Integer = 13
  desk-focus-edit : Integer = 14
  desk-focus-menu : Integer = 15
  desk-focus-rev : Integer = 16

  desk-review-cell : Integer = 0

  desk-mon-paint : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-mon-paint (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (ds) = act
    let bg = gop-fill-rect base stride 0 0 w h (dk-wall ds)
    in desk-wnd-paint-all base w h stride font tf ds
  end

 THE TICK GATE STAYS AND THE CHROME GOES IN FRONT OF IT. A window event has
 to be answered when it happens; the pane's own CONTENT is a second late at
 worst and always has been, because a monitor that repainted per poll would
 rebuild eleven rows of Text at the poll rate to change one digit.

 What the second costs now is one ACPI table walk per repaint of ANY window,
 since the painter builds every open window's tree: that is the same bargain
 `desk-wnd-tree`'s clock arm already makes with the RTC, and it is bounded by
 the repaint rate rather than the poll rate.

  desk-mon-step : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, Integer, Boolean -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-mon-step (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (sc) (clicked) = act
    ev <- desk-wnd-chrome-step base w h stride font tf mouse ds cs desk-focus-mon sc clicked
    if ev == desk-wnd-ev-close then act 0 end
    else if ev == desk-wnd-ev-min then act desk-step-hide end
    else if ev == desk-wnd-ev-stay then act 1 end
    else act
      let mark = __heap-save
      in act
        now <- rtc-seconds 0
        if sc == 88 then act
          s <- desk-shot base w h stride font ds
          let d = __heap-restore mark
          in 1
        end
        else if now < 0 | now == peek-32 ds 16 then act
          let d = __heap-restore mark
          in 1
        end
        else act
          let st = poke-32 ds 16 now
          in let hd = cursor-hide cs base stride
          in act
            p <- desk-mon-paint base w h stride font tf ds
            let c = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
            in let dropped = __heap-restore mark
            in 1
          end
        end
      end
    end
  end

 The two facts the painter cannot reach are written HERE and not in
 `desk-run`, because `desk-run` is where the block is allocated and `kbd` and
 `mouse` are in scope at both. Open is the later of the two and reads the
 same records, so the pane cannot be looking at a stale enumeration; putting
 it in `desk-run` would work equally and this is where the pane's own facts
 belong.

  desk-mon-open : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-mon-open (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) = act
    hd <- cursor-hide cs base stride
    let blk = peek-32 ds dk-mon-cell
    in let kp = poke-32 blk dk-mon-kbd-cell (if kbd.uk-ok then 1 else 0)
    in let mp = poke-32 blk dk-mon-mouse-cell (if mouse.um-ok then 1 else 0)
    in let o = desk-wnd-open desk-focus-mon w h ds
    in let due = poke-32 ds 16 255
    in act
      p0 <- desk-mon-paint base w h stride font tf ds
      let sf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell (dk-wr-top (peek-32 ds dk-wr-cell))
      in let c1 = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
      in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
    end
  end

 One dispatcher, so `desk-loop` names no pane. A pane joins by taking a focus
 id, adding a line here and a line in `desk-dispatch`, which is the same
 two-places rule section 4 of the contract already states for a loop pane.

  desk-step-of : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode, Integer, Integer, Boolean -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-step-of (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) (foc) (sc) (clicked) =
    if foc == desk-focus-mon then desk-mon-step base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs sc clicked
    else if foc == desk-focus-cal then desk-wnd-quiet-step base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs desk-focus-cal sc clicked
    else if foc == desk-focus-dif then desk-wnd-quiet-step base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs desk-focus-dif sc clicked
    else if foc == desk-focus-trk then desk-trk-step base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs sc clicked
    else if foc == desk-focus-rev then desk-rev-step base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs sc
    else if foc == desk-focus-con then desk-console-step base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs sc clicked
    else if foc == desk-focus-style then desk-style-step base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs sc clicked
    else if foc == desk-focus-calc then desk-calc-step base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs sc clicked
    else if foc == desk-focus-clkp then desk-clock-pane-step base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs sc clicked
    else if foc == desk-focus-prog then desk-prog-step base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs sc clicked
    else if foc == desk-focus-menu then desk-menu-step base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs sc clicked
    else if foc == desk-focus-files then desk-files-step base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps sc clicked
    else if foc == desk-focus-bro then desk-browser-step base w h stride font tf mouse ds cs apps root sc clicked
    else if foc == desk-focus-scene then desk-scene-step base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps gsc-scene desk-focus-scene sc clicked
    else if foc == desk-focus-fish then desk-scene-step base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps gf-scene desk-focus-fish sc clicked
    else if foc == desk-focus-edit then desk-edit-step base w h stride font tf mouse ds cs apps sc clicked
    else 0

 A still picture is a whole class of pane, not one pane. The Calendar draws its
 month once and the Diffusion pane draws its form once; neither repaints, and
 for both the entry paint happens where focus is taken, so every later call
 only has to answer Esc and F12. One step serves both, and the next such pane
 costs a line in `desk-step-of` and nothing else.

 The mouse is not their business either: `desk-loop` pumps it and moves the
 cursor before any step is called, which is what `dk-pump` used to do inside
 each pane's own loop.

  desk-quiet-step : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-quiet-step (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (sc) =
    if sc == 88 then act
      let mark = __heap-save
      in act
        s <- desk-shot base w h stride font ds
        let d = __heap-restore mark
        in 1
      end
    end
    else 1

 The same still picture, in a window. It is a second function rather than a
 parameter on the one above because the two differ in what they are handed as
 well as in what they do: a windowed step needs the pointer and the focus id,
 and a pane with no window has neither to give. The pill, the titlebar buttons
 and the focus cycle all arrive here for free, which is the whole of what a
 quiet pane gets out of being a window.

  desk-wnd-quiet-step : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Boolean -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-wnd-quiet-step (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (fid) (sc) (clicked) = act
    ev <- desk-wnd-chrome-step base w h stride font tf mouse ds cs fid sc clicked
    if ev == desk-wnd-ev-close then 0
    else if ev == desk-wnd-ev-min then desk-step-hide
    else if ev == desk-wnd-ev-stay then 1
    else if sc == 88 then act
      let mark = __heap-save
      in act
        s <- desk-shot base w h stride font ds
        let d = __heap-restore mark
        in 1
      end
    end
    else 1
  end

  desk-app-close-to : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, Integer -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
 Both close paths DROP the `apps` they were handed and build an empty one after
 the restore, and that is not tidiness. The restore frees everything above the
 base mark, which is exactly where a pane's `-open` put its state, so carrying
 the old record forward would carry pointers into memory that has just been
 handed back. The fresh record is built AFTER the restore, so it lives beside
 the new `root` and dies with it.

  desk-app-close-to (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (sc) =
    let fid = peek-32 ds desk-focus-cell
    in let unreg = dk-wr-close (peek-32 ds dk-wr-cell) fid
    in let ms = peek-32 ds desk-marks-cell
    in let sb = apps.da-browser
    in let ss = apps.da-scene
    in let se = apps.da-edit
    in let sfi = apps.da-files
    in let kl = desk-marks-kill ms fid
    in let rec = desk-marks-reclaim ms (0 - 1)
    in let dep = desk-marks-depth ms
    in let cf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell desk-focus-none
    in let hd2 = cursor-hide cs base stride
    in let tgt = if dep <= 0 then peek-32 ds desk-mark-cell else if rec < 0 then __heap-save else rec
    in let eb = peek-32 ds desk-edit-cell
    in let ev = if peek-32 eb 0 /= 0 & tgt <= peek-32 eb 0 then poke-32 eb 0 0 else 0
    in let dropped = __heap-restore tgt
    in let root = desk-draw base w h stride font (ui-scale w) tf ds
    in let nf = if fid == desk-focus-files then None else sfi
    in let nb = if fid == desk-focus-bro then None else sb
    in let nsc = if fid == desk-focus-scene | fid == desk-focus-fish then None else ss
    in let ne = if fid == desk-focus-edit then None else se
    in let fresh = if dep <= 0 then desk-apps-empty else DeskApps { da-files = nf, da-browser = nb, da-scene = nsc, da-edit = ne }
    in let c0 = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
    in desk-dispatch base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs fresh root sc False

  desk-step-hide : Integer = 1000000

  desk-app-hide : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
 MINIMISING ONE WINDOW MUST NOT PUT THE OTHERS AWAY. The desktop is redrawn
 first either way, because that is what builds the taskbar tree the pill lives
 in and what `desk-taskbar-clock` will re-render every second afterwards; a
 stale `root` here paints the band without the pill within a second. Then, if
 a window is still visible, focus returns to it and the windows are painted
 back over the fresh chrome.

  desk-app-hide (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) = act
    let cf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell desk-focus-none
    in let hd2 = cursor-hide cs base stride
    in let root = desk-draw base w h stride font (ui-scale w) tf ds
    in let vis = dk-wr-visible (peek-32 ds dk-wr-cell) ds
    in if vis == desk-focus-none then
      let c0 = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
      in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
    else act
      let rf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell vis
      in act
        p <- desk-wnd-paint-all base w h stride font tf ds
        let c0 = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
        in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
      end
    end
  end

  desk-app-close : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
 A WINDOW THAT CLOSES OVER ANOTHER WINDOW HANDS FOCUS DOWN INSTEAD OF LEAVING
 FOR THE DESKTOP, which is the one thing about closing that a second window
 changes. The restore still runs and still goes to the base mark: every
 windowed pane keeps its state in a block `desk-run` allocated BELOW that
 mark, so the window left standing survives it and only the closing pane's
 transients go. What changes is where control lands afterwards.

  desk-app-close (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) =
    let fid = peek-32 ds desk-focus-cell
    in let wsr = peek-32 ds dk-wr-cell
    in let nxt = if dk-wr-find wsr fid 0 >= 0 then dk-wr-close wsr fid else desk-focus-none
    in if nxt /= desk-focus-none then desk-wnd-close-to base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps nxt
    else desk-app-close-plain base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps

 The window is gone from the registry, another is still open, and the heap
 goes back to the base mark exactly as an ordinary close does.

  desk-wnd-close-to : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, Integer -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-wnd-close-to (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (nxt) = act
    hd <- act cursor-hide cs base stride end
    let dropped = __heap-restore (peek-32 ds desk-mark-cell)
    in let sf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell nxt
    in let root = desk-draw base w h stride font (ui-scale w) tf ds
    in let bg = gop-fill-rect base stride 0 0 w h (dk-wall ds)
    in act
      p <- desk-wnd-paint-all base w h stride font tf ds
      let c0 = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
      in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs desk-apps-empty root
    end
  end

  desk-app-close-plain : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-app-close-plain (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) =
    let fid = peek-32 ds desk-focus-cell
    in let ms = peek-32 ds desk-marks-cell
    in let sb = apps.da-browser
    in let ss = apps.da-scene
    in let se = apps.da-edit
    in let sfi = apps.da-files
    in let kl = desk-marks-kill ms fid
    in let rec = desk-marks-reclaim ms (0 - 1)
    in let dep = desk-marks-depth ms
    in let cf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell desk-focus-none
    in let hd2 = cursor-hide cs base stride
    in let tgt = if dep <= 0 then peek-32 ds desk-mark-cell else if rec < 0 then __heap-save else rec
    in let eb = peek-32 ds desk-edit-cell
    in let ev = if peek-32 eb 0 /= 0 & tgt <= peek-32 eb 0 then poke-32 eb 0 0 else 0
    in let dropped = __heap-restore tgt
    in let root = desk-draw base w h stride font (ui-scale w) tf ds
    in let nf = if fid == desk-focus-files then None else sfi
    in let nb = if fid == desk-focus-bro then None else sb
    in let nsc = if fid == desk-focus-scene | fid == desk-focus-fish then None else ss
    in let ne = if fid == desk-focus-edit then None else se
    in let fresh = if dep <= 0 then desk-apps-empty else DeskApps { da-files = nf, da-browser = nb, da-scene = nsc, da-edit = ne }
    in let c0 = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
    in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs fresh root
    end
  end

Section: Calculator

 The Calculator is a WINDOW now, so it draws through the same painter the
 Clock does and not through `dk-chrome-paint` with its tree in the content
 slot. That is what makes it the second window, and it is the whole of the
 change on this pane's paint side: the registry decides what is drawn and in
 what order, and this pane no longer has an opinion about it.

 These became actions because the painter is one: the Clock's tree reads the
 RTC, so painting whatever is open can read hardware even when the pane that
 asked for the repaint is this one.

  desk-calc-draw : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-calc-draw (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (ks) (ds) =
    desk-wnd-paint-all base w h stride font tf ds

  desk-calc-paint : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-calc-paint (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (ks) (ds) = act
    let bg = gop-fill-rect base stride 0 0 w h (dk-wall ds)
    in desk-calc-draw base w h stride font tf ks ds
  end

 The keypad is clickable. `dk-calc-hit` lays the same tree out again and
 answers the scancode of whatever button is under the pointer, so a click
 and a keystroke reach `gcalc-key` by the same road.

 IT MUST LAY THE TREE OUT WHERE THE PAINTER PUT IT, and that is the whole of
 what making this pane a window costs on the hit side. It used to lay out at
 the screen corner over the full width because that is where the chrome drew
 it; the painter now renders it inside the window's content box through
 `comp-render-at`, which puts the ORIGIN in the layout rect and hands back
 absolute logical bounds. Laying out at the corner and hit-testing that would
 have every keypad button answer for whichever button sits at the same offset
 from the corner -- a click landing on a wrong-but-plausible digit, which is
 the failure a screenshot cannot show and arithmetic can.

 So it repeats `comp-render-at`'s own rect arithmetic exactly: origin at the
 window's content box, which is the frame inset by the titlebar.

  dk-calc-hit : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, GopFont -> Integer
  dk-calc-hit (w) (h) (ks) (mx) (my) (ds) (tf) =
    let probe = __heap-save
    in let s = ui-wscale w
    in let blk = peek-32 ds 24
    in let st = dk-wnd-st blk
    in let bh = dk-wnd-bar-h tf s
    in let wx = dk-wnd-rx blk w st
    in let wy = dk-wnd-ry blk w h st ds + bh
    in let ww = dk-wnd-rw blk w st
    in let wh = dk-wnd-rh blk w h st ds - bh
    in let laid = widget-layout (gcalc-tree ks) (dk-theme-ds ds) (layout-rect (wx / s) (wy / s) (ww / s) (wh / s))
    in let code = when ev-hit-widget laid (comp-hit-x mx s) (comp-hit-y my s)
          is Just (wgt) -> gcalc-id-scan (wgt.wn-id)
          is None -> 0
    in let dropped = __heap-restore probe
    in code

  desk-calc-repaint : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Integer, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-calc-repaint (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (cs) (mouse) (ks) (ds) = act
    hd <- act cursor-hide cs base stride end
    p <- desk-calc-draw base w h stride font tf ks ds
    dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
  end

 `gcalc-key` APPLIES the key and answers whether it meant anything, so the
 test in the fourth arm is the press: it must be evaluated exactly once, and
 the arm below it only repaints. Calling it again to find out what it did
 would enter the digit twice. That was true of the loop this replaces and is
 the one thing about this pane a conversion could quietly break.

  desk-calc-step : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, Integer, Boolean -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-calc-step (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (sc) (clicked) = act
    ev <- desk-wnd-chrome-step base w h stride font tf mouse ds cs desk-focus-calc sc clicked
    let ks = peek-32 ds 24
    in if ev == desk-wnd-ev-close then 0
    else if ev == desk-wnd-ev-min then desk-step-hide
    else if ev == desk-wnd-ev-stay then 1
    else if sc == 88 then act
      let mark = __heap-save
      in act
        s <- desk-shot base w h stride font ds
        let d = __heap-restore mark
        in 1
      end
    end
    else
      let mark = __heap-save
      in let hit = if clicked then dk-calc-hit w h ks (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) ds tf else 0
      in if hit /= 0 then act
        let a = gcalc-key ks hit
        in act
          p <- desk-calc-repaint base w h stride font tf cs mouse ks ds
          let d = __heap-restore mark
          in 1
        end
      end
      else if gcalc-key ks sc == 0 then
        let d = __heap-restore mark
        in 1
      else act
        p <- desk-calc-repaint base w h stride font tf cs mouse ks ds
        let d = __heap-restore mark
        in 1
      end
  end

Section: The Console Pane

 The pane owns its input line and scrollback and calls GopConsole for every
 key, which is the same division desk-calc has with GopCalc: the desk owns
 the loop and the chrome, the chapter owns the state and what a key means.

 The wall fill is the ENTRY paint only. It writes every pixel on the
 screen, and doing it again before each redraw is what made typing flash:
 the eye catches the cleared frame because there is no back buffer and
 nothing pages the two apart. The widget layer draws its own opaque
 panels over the same rectangles every time, so the wall underneath them
 has nothing left to contribute after the first frame -- the clear was
 pure cost and pure flicker.

  desk-console-draw : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-console-draw (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (ns) (ds) =
    desk-wnd-paint-all base w h stride font tf ds

  desk-console-paint : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-console-paint (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (ns) (ds) = act
    let bg = gop-fill-rect base stride 0 0 w h (dk-wall ds)
    in desk-console-draw base w h stride font tf ns ds
  end

  desk-console-repaint : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Integer, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-console-repaint (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (cs) (mouse) (ns) (ds) = act
    hd <- act cursor-hide cs base stride end
    p <- desk-console-draw base w h stride font tf ns ds
    dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
  end

 `mods`, the shift state, was a loop parameter and goes to the console's own
 state block for the same reason the Clock's `last` did: `ds` has two cells
 left and this is not worth one. **`GopConsole` owns offsets 0 to 12 of that
 block and the desk owns 16 up**, the same split as the clock block, written
 here because nothing cross-checks it.

 THE CONSOLE IS THE THIRD WINDOWED PANE. Its block is 64 bytes of which the
 two halves above take 0 to 16, so the window slot at 44 through 60 was free
 space it was already carrying and no allocation moved. Tab reaches the window
 cycle rather than the console because `gcon-key` already answered nothing for
 it: `apply-mods` gives scancode 15 a character below 32 and the type arm
 refuses those, so the key was inert here before it meant anything.

  dk-conp-mods-cell : Integer = 16

  desk-console-step : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, Integer, Boolean -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-console-step (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (sc) (clicked) = act
    ev <- desk-wnd-chrome-step base w h stride font tf mouse ds cs desk-focus-con sc clicked
    let ns = peek-32 ds desk-console-cell
    in let md = gcon-shift-of (peek-32 ns dk-conp-mods-cell) sc
    in let sm = poke-32 ns dk-conp-mods-cell md
    in if ev == desk-wnd-ev-close then 0
    else if ev == desk-wnd-ev-min then desk-step-hide
    else if ev == desk-wnd-ev-stay then 1
    else if sc == 88 then act
      let mark = __heap-save
      in act
        s <- desk-shot base w h stride font ds
        let d = __heap-restore mark
        in 1
      end
    end
    else if sc == 0 then 1
    else act
      let mark = __heap-save
      in act
        a <- gcon-key ns sc md
        p <- desk-console-repaint base w h stride font tf cs mouse ns ds
        let d = __heap-restore mark
        in 1
      end
    end
  end

  desk-console-open : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-console-open (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (ns) (apps) (root) = act
    hd <- cursor-hide cs base stride
    let e = gcon-ensure ns
    in let o = desk-wnd-open desk-focus-con w h ds
    in act
      p0 <- desk-console-paint base w h stride font tf ns ds
      let m0 = poke-32 ns dk-conp-mods-cell 0
      in let sf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell (dk-wr-top (peek-32 ds dk-wr-cell))
      in let c1 = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
      in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
    end
  end

Section: Clock

 The face is rebuilt from the part on every pass rather than cached, so
 the pane cannot drift away from the hardware it is showing. That means
 this loop repaints on a tick like the Monitor pane does, and unlike the
 panes that only repaint on a key.

Section: Windows

 A pane in a WINDOW rather than filling the content area. Everything here is
 DEVICE pixels, because a window rectangle is a screen fact and the two
 scales the desk carries disagree below 1600.

 The content box is what the chrome leaves: below the top bar, above the
 taskbar, and the full width of the glass since the sidebar went. The window
 is three quarters of it, centred, so the desktop shows around it and the
 thing reads as a window rather than as a pane with a stripe on top.

 It stays a function rather than becoming the literal zero its callers could
 inline, because every window's left edge and every pane's origin derive from
 it and `codex/test/apps/desk-pane-origin` asserts that they do.

  dk-cbox-x : Integer -> Integer
  dk-cbox-x (w) = 0

  dk-cbox-y : Integer -> Integer
  dk-cbox-y (w) = dk-bar-h * ui-scale w

  dk-cbox-w : Integer -> Integer
  dk-cbox-w (w) = w - dk-cbox-x w

  dk-cbox-h : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-cbox-h (w) (h) (ds) = h - dk-cbox-y w - dk-task-px ds w

 A maximised window is the content box exactly, so `x` and `y` need no arm of
 their own: they centre whatever width and height come back, and at the full
 size that offset is zero.

 Nothing stores a restore rectangle, and that is right only while the normal
 rect is COMPUTED. `wm-move` and `wm-resize` exist in `Window.codex` and are
 called by nothing; the first caller makes the normal rect a fact about what
 the operator did rather than arithmetic over the content box, and the moment
 that happens restoring has to read a rectangle somebody kept.

  dk-wnd-st-normal : Integer = 0
  dk-wnd-st-max : Integer = 1

 A MINIMISED window is still in the registry and still holds its rect; it is
 simply not drawn, and a pill in the taskbar is where it went. That is the
 whole of the state: minimise stores nothing else, because the rect it should
 come back to is already a fact in the block (6.3c) rather than something a
 restore has to reconstruct.

  dk-wnd-st-min : Integer = 2

  dk-wnd-minimised : Integer -> Boolean
  dk-wnd-minimised (blk) = dk-wnd-st blk == dk-wnd-st-min

  dk-wnd-w : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-wnd-w (w) (st) = if st == dk-wnd-st-max then dk-cbox-w w else dk-cbox-w w * 3 / 4

  dk-wnd-h : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-wnd-h (w) (h) (st) (ds) = if st == dk-wnd-st-max then dk-cbox-h w h ds else dk-cbox-h w h ds * 3 / 4

  dk-wnd-x : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-wnd-x (w) (st) = dk-cbox-x w + (dk-cbox-w w - dk-wnd-w w st) / 2

  dk-wnd-y : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-wnd-y (w) (h) (st) (ds) = dk-cbox-y w + (dk-cbox-h w h ds - dk-wnd-h w h st ds) / 2

 The four above are the DEFAULT rect and they answer from the screen alone, so
 every window they place lands on the same pixels. That is what a second
 window cannot survive, and it is the obligation `dk-wnd-st-max` above already
 records: once a rect is a fact about what the operator did, restoring has to
 read a rectangle somebody kept.

 The rectangle is kept in the PANE's own block, at the offsets the desk owns
 rather than `GopClock`'s (`works-desk-contract.md` section 2), for the reason
 the window state is: it is a fact about this pane's window and the next pane
 to get one keeps its own.

 Width is the unwritten sentinel and it is the only one of the four that can
 be. A zero x or a zero y is a legal position on this screen and a zero width
 is not a window, so an untouched block reads as never-placed and the default
 answers, which is the same absent-is-zero road `dk-clkp-state-cell` takes.

  dk-wnd-st-cell : Integer = 44
  dk-wnd-rx-cell : Integer = 48
  dk-wnd-ry-cell : Integer = 52
  dk-wnd-rw-cell : Integer = 56
  dk-wnd-rh-cell : Integer = 60

  dk-wnd-st : Integer -> Integer
  dk-wnd-st (blk) = peek-32 blk dk-wnd-st-cell

  dk-wnd-placed : Integer -> Boolean
  dk-wnd-placed (blk) = peek-32 blk dk-wnd-rw-cell /= 0

  dk-wnd-place : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-wnd-place (blk) (x) (y) (ww) (wh) =
    let a = poke-32 blk dk-wnd-rx-cell x
    in let b = poke-32 blk dk-wnd-ry-cell y
    in let c = poke-32 blk dk-wnd-rw-cell ww
    in poke-32 blk dk-wnd-rh-cell wh

 A maximised window is the content box whatever the block says, so placement
 is read on the normal arm only. Wiring it into the maximised arm would make
 maximise mean "the last place you dropped it", and `desk-window-frame` keeps
 a row that cannot move under this change to say so.

 A CELL READ BACK IS UNSIGNED, AND A WINDOW MAY NOW SIT AT A NEGATIVE X.
 `peek-32` zero-extends, so a `poke-32` of -362 reads back as 4294966934.
 `GopCalc` carries the same helper for the same reason and `GopAcpi` says it
 in words: a sign-extending load is what this platform does NOT do.

 IT COST A GUEST CRASH AND TWO WRONG DIAGNOSES, and every symptom was the one
 defect. Dragging the Calculator off the left faulted at `CR2=0x4bf2ce658`,
 which is the framebuffer base plus `(y * stride + x) * 4` evaluated on the
 unsigned form. Guarding `gop-put` and `gop-get` against `x < 0` did not help,
 because the value that arrives is never negative -- it is enormous. With the
 guards in, the fault stopped and the window vanished instead: the erase
 clipped to nothing so old outlines were left behind, and the repaint placed
 the window at four billion and drew none of it.

 The fault dump named it from the first run. `R13=0xfffffe96` IS -362, and
 reading that one register as a signed value would have settled it before any
 guard was written (L-MECHANISM: read every number the failure hands you).

  dk-cell-signed : Integer -> Integer
  dk-cell-signed (v) = if v >= 2147483648 then v - 4294967296 else v

  dk-wnd-rx : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-wnd-rx (blk) (w) (st) =
    if st == dk-wnd-st-max then dk-cbox-x w
    else if dk-wnd-placed blk then dk-cell-signed (peek-32 blk dk-wnd-rx-cell)
    else dk-wnd-x w dk-wnd-st-normal

  dk-wnd-ry : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-wnd-ry (blk) (w) (h) (st) (ds) =
    if st == dk-wnd-st-max then dk-cbox-y w
    else if dk-wnd-placed blk then dk-cell-signed (peek-32 blk dk-wnd-ry-cell)
    else dk-wnd-y w h dk-wnd-st-normal ds

  dk-wnd-rw : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-wnd-rw (blk) (w) (st) =
    if st == dk-wnd-st-max then dk-cbox-w w
    else if dk-wnd-placed blk then peek-32 blk dk-wnd-rw-cell
    else dk-wnd-w w dk-wnd-st-normal

  dk-wnd-rh : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-wnd-rh (blk) (w) (h) (st) (ds) =
    if st == dk-wnd-st-max then dk-cbox-h w h ds
    else if dk-wnd-placed blk then peek-32 blk dk-wnd-rh-cell
    else dk-wnd-h w h dk-wnd-st-normal ds

Section: The window registry

 Which windows are open, and in what order they stack. THE ARRAY ORDER IS THE
 Z ORDER: entry 0 is the bottom, the last entry is the top, and the top is the
 focused window. That is what removes the `win-z` field a window record would
 carry, along with every question about what happens when two windows hold the
 same z. Raising a window is moving its entry to the end.

 The block is allocated in `desk-run` before the base mark and its pointer
 lives in `ds` cell 80, because a pane exit restores the heap to that mark and
 anything allocated later would be gone with a live cell still pointing at it
 (`works-desk-contract.md` section 2).

 `Window.codex`'s `WindowManager` is NOT what this is, and the design records
 why at length: it is a functional record, every `wm-focus` returns a new one,
 and a value like that cannot be the desk's durable state. Materializing one
 per frame to call `wm-focus` would allocate a `List Window` inside the frame
 bracket and then need a second walk to write the answer back, to do what
 `dk-wr-raise` does in six lines over the block with no allocation at all.

  dk-wr-cell : Integer = 80

 THE CAP IS THE BLOCK'S OWN BOUND. `desk-run` allocates 64 bytes, the count
 takes the first four and an entry takes four, so fifteen entries is what fits
 and a sixteenth would write past it. It was 4, which was the vertical cascade
 room divided by the step (`dk-wnd-cascade-y`'s own paragraph has the
 arithmetic) rather than anything about the block, and the cascade wrapping is
 what let it become the real bound.

 There are fewer windowed panes than this today, so the refusal is a guard
 rather than a limit anyone meets. `codex/test/apps/desk-window-registry` is
 the only thing that reaches it, and it is the only thing that can.

  dk-wr-max : Integer = 15

  dk-wr-count : Integer -> Integer
  dk-wr-count (ws) = peek-32 ws 0

  dk-wr-at : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-wr-at (ws) (i) = peek-32 ws (4 * i + 4)

  dk-wr-set : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-wr-set (ws) (i) (fid) = poke-32 ws (4 * i + 4) fid

  dk-wr-top : Integer -> Integer
  dk-wr-top (ws) =
    if dk-wr-count ws <= 0 then desk-focus-none
    else dk-wr-at ws (dk-wr-count ws - 1)

  dk-wr-find : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-wr-find (ws) (fid) (i) =
    if i >= dk-wr-count ws then 0 - 1
    else if dk-wr-at ws i == fid then i
    else dk-wr-find ws fid (i + 1)

 Everything above `i` slides down over it, which both raises and removes: the
 difference is only whether the count is decremented afterwards.

  dk-wr-slide : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-wr-slide (ws) (i) =
    if i + 1 >= dk-wr-count ws then i
    else
      let a = dk-wr-set ws i (dk-wr-at ws (i + 1))
      in dk-wr-slide ws (i + 1)

  dk-wr-raise : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-wr-raise (ws) (fid) =
    let i = dk-wr-find ws fid 0
    in if i < 0 then dk-wr-top ws
    else
      let last = dk-wr-count ws - 1
      in let s = dk-wr-slide ws i
      in let p = dk-wr-set ws last fid
      in dk-wr-top ws

 Opening a window that is already open RAISES it rather than adding a second
 entry, which is what makes the launcher's clock button and a click on the
 clock's own frame the same operation.

  dk-wr-open : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-wr-open (ws) (fid) =
    if dk-wr-find ws fid 0 >= 0 then dk-wr-raise ws fid
    else if dk-wr-count ws >= dk-wr-max then dk-wr-top ws
    else
      let n = dk-wr-count ws
      in let a = dk-wr-set ws n fid
      in let c = poke-32 ws 0 (n + 1)
      in dk-wr-top ws

  dk-wr-close : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-wr-close (ws) (fid) =
    let i = dk-wr-find ws fid 0
    in if i < 0 then dk-wr-top ws
    else
      let s = dk-wr-slide ws i
      in let c = poke-32 ws 0 (dk-wr-count ws - 1)
      in dk-wr-top ws

 Alt-tab. The top entry goes to the BOTTOM, so the one beneath it surfaces and
 repeated presses walk every window and return to where they started. Over one
 window it is deliberately a no-op rather than a cycle of length one, because
 a repaint that changes nothing is a repaint a reader cannot tell from a
 broken one.

  dk-wr-sink : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-wr-sink (ws) (i) =
    if i <= 0 then 0
    else
      let a = dk-wr-set ws i (dk-wr-at ws (i - 1))
      in dk-wr-sink ws (i - 1)

 The topmost window that is actually ON SCREEN. Focus follows this rather than
 `dk-wr-top`, because a minimised window is still the top of the registry and
 focusing it would step a pane nobody can see.

  dk-wr-top-vis : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-wr-top-vis (ws) (ds) (i) =
    if i < 0 then desk-focus-none
    else
      let fid = dk-wr-at ws i
      in if dk-wnd-minimised (desk-wnd-blk fid ds) then dk-wr-top-vis ws ds (i - 1)
      else fid

  dk-wr-visible : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-wr-visible (ws) (ds) = dk-wr-top-vis ws ds (dk-wr-count ws - 1)

  dk-wr-cycle : Integer -> Integer
  dk-wr-cycle (ws) =
    let n = dk-wr-count ws
    in if n <= 1 then dk-wr-top ws
    else
      let t = dk-wr-at ws (n - 1)
      in let s = dk-wr-sink ws (n - 1)
      in let b = dk-wr-set ws 0 t
      in dk-wr-top ws

Section: Windows, continued

 The titlebar is the face's own cell plus padding, so it follows the type
 rather than staying at the 40 device pixels `dk-title-h` gives at this
 scale. A button is a square of that height, which is what makes the three of
 them a strip the pointer can resolve with one division.

  dk-wnd-bar-h : GopFont, Integer -> Integer
  dk-wnd-bar-h (tf) (ws) = (if tf.gf-ok then tf.gf-gh else comp-glyph-h * ws) + 8 * ws

  dk-wnd-btn-x : Integer, Integer, GopFont, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-wnd-btn-x (wx) (ww) (tf) (ws) (i) =
    wx + ww - (3 - i) * dk-wnd-bar-h tf ws

 A window button carries a DRAWING at the size the desk rasterized its icon
 set to, centred in its square. It is not rasterized at the button's own size
 because `dk-icons-init` settles ONE size at boot and a second would be a
 second blob.

 The ink is contrast against THE BAR THIS BUTTON IS ON, which is a parameter
 because the bar is no longer one colour. It read `theme-ink-on` the palette's
 secondary unconditionally, which was right while every titlebar was
 `pal-secondary` and became wrong the moment a focused one turned
 `pal-primary`: in the default palette secondary is `#004488` and takes light
 ink, primary is `#69BE28` and takes dark, so the focused window's three
 buttons would have been drawn in the ink chosen for the unfocused colour while
 the caption beside them was right.

  dk-wnd-btn : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Text, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-wnd-btn (base) (stride) (bx) (by) (name) (side) (ds) (ground) =
    let blk = peek-32 ds dk-icons-cell
    in let pal = dk-pal ds
    in if blk == 0 then 0
    else let ih = dk-icons-size blk
    in gicon-blit-cov base stride (dk-icons-at blk name) ih (bx + (side - ih) / 2) (by + (side - ih) / 2) (theme-ink-on pal ground)

 The pane's own tree goes in through `comp-render-at`, so its bounds come
 back as absolute logical coordinates and the pane's hit test needs no origin
 of its own (`codex/test/apps/desk-window-origin`).

 The chrome is NOT painted here any more. It is painted once by
 `dk-wnd-paint-all` and then every open window is drawn over it, bottom to
 top, which is the whole of what a second window needed from the paint side.

 THE FOCUSED WINDOW'S TITLEBAR IS `pal-primary` AND THE REST ARE
 `pal-secondary`. That is not decoration: focus is otherwise invisible in a
 capture, and an alt-tab arm that can only read which pane STEPS is reading
 the thing it is trying to test through the thing it is testing. The caption's
 ink is `theme-ink-on` the bar it actually got, so the contrast follows the
 colour rather than assuming the unfocused one.

 THE BORDER IS DRAWN AFTER THE CONTENT, NEVER BEFORE IT, and it is one
 function because two callers need it. It used to be four fills at the top of
 `dk-wnd-frame`, before the tree render -- which is handed `wx` by `ww`, the
 FULL rect -- so the left, right and bottom borders were painted over the
 instant they were drawn and only the top survived, the tree starting below it.
 Measured 2026-08-26: `pal-border` present for two rows along the top of an
 unresized window and absent down both sides. Damian saw it as windows floating
 in blackness with fragments of blue around them, the fragments being wherever
 the tree happened not to cover.

 A SCENE PANE IS INSET INSTEAD, AND REDRAWING THE BORDER PER FRAME IS NOT THE
 FIX. `desk-scene-step` renders its viewport into the window's interior AFTER
 the frame is painted, so laying the border back over it looked right and
 measured wrong: the NEXT frame paints over it again, and at seventeen
 milliseconds a frame the border exists for a sliver of each one. Measured that
 way it covered 48 of 591 rows down each side -- the titlebar's worth, and
 nothing else. `gsc-place` is given a viewport inset by the border thickness
 now, so the scene cannot reach those pixels at all and the border is drawn
 once by the frame like every other pane's.

 Insetting the WIDGET content instead would have moved every windowed pane's
 layout by the border thickness and shifted the goldens that record pane
 origins; drawing over the outermost pixels is what a border is for.

  dk-wnd-border : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-wnd-border (base) (stride) (wx) (wy) (ww) (wh) (ds) (w) =
    let pal = dk-pal ds
    in let ws = ui-wscale w
    in let e0 = gop-fill-rect base stride wx wy ww ws (pal.pal-border)
    in let e1 = gop-fill-rect base stride wx (wy + wh - ws) ww ws (pal.pal-border)
    in let e2 = gop-fill-rect base stride wx wy ws wh (pal.pal-border)
    in gop-fill-rect base stride (wx + ww - ws) wy ws wh (pal.pal-border)

  dk-wnd-frame : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, WidgetNode, Text, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Boolean -> WidgetNode
  dk-wnd-frame (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (tree) (title) (ds) (wx) (wy) (ww) (wh) (focused) =
    let pal = dk-pal ds
    in let ws = ui-wscale w
    in let s = ui-scale w
    in let barcol = if focused then pal.pal-primary else pal.pal-secondary
    in let bh = dk-wnd-bar-h tf ws
    in let body = gop-fill-rect base stride wx wy ww wh (pal.pal-bg)
    in let bar = gop-fill-rect base stride (wx + ws) (wy + ws) (ww - 2 * ws) bh barcol
    in let cap = desk-line base stride h font s tf (wx + 8 * ws) (wy + 4 * ws) title (theme-ink-on pal barcol)
    in let b0 = dk-wnd-btn base stride (dk-wnd-btn-x wx ww tf ws 0) wy "win-min" bh ds barcol
    in let b1 = dk-wnd-btn base stride (dk-wnd-btn-x wx ww tf ws 1) wy "win-max" bh ds barcol
    in let b2 = dk-wnd-btn base stride (dk-wnd-btn-x wx ww tf ws 2) wy "win-close" bh ds barcol
    in let laid = comp-render-at base stride font tf tree (dk-theme-ds ds) wx (wy + bh) ww (wh - bh) ws
    in let bd = dk-wnd-border base stride wx wy ww wh ds w
    in laid

 What the pointer landed on. The three buttons are the right-hand strip of
 the titlebar and are answered BEFORE the bar itself, or a click on close
 would read as a drag of the window it is closing.

  dk-wnd-none : Integer = 0
  dk-wnd-min : Integer = 1
  dk-wnd-max : Integer = 2
  dk-wnd-close : Integer = 3
  dk-wnd-bar : Integer = 4
  dk-wnd-body : Integer = 5

  dk-wnd-hit : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, GopFont, Integer -> Integer
  dk-wnd-hit (wx) (wy) (ww) (wh) (mx) (my) (tf) (ws) =
    let bh = dk-wnd-bar-h tf ws
    in if mx < wx then dk-wnd-none
    else if mx >= wx + ww then dk-wnd-none
    else if my < wy then dk-wnd-none
    else if my >= wy + wh then dk-wnd-none
    else if my >= wy + bh then dk-wnd-body
    else if mx >= dk-wnd-btn-x wx ww tf ws 2 then dk-wnd-close
    else if mx >= dk-wnd-btn-x wx ww tf ws 1 then dk-wnd-max
    else if mx >= dk-wnd-btn-x wx ww tf ws 0 then dk-wnd-min
    else dk-wnd-bar

Section: What a windowed pane is

 Three facts per windowed pane, and a pane joins the window world by taking a
 line in each. It is the same two-places rule `desk-step-of` already states,
 which is why these sit together rather than being reached through the pane's
 own `-open`.

 The BLOCK is the one already allocated in `desk-run`, so a window slot costs
 no allocation: cells 44 through 60 of a block that was there anyway.

  desk-wnd-blk : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  desk-wnd-blk (fid) (ds) =
    if fid == desk-focus-clkp then peek-32 ds desk-clock-cell
    else if fid == desk-focus-calc then peek-32 ds 24
    else if fid == desk-focus-con then peek-32 ds desk-console-cell
    else if fid == desk-focus-prog then peek-32 ds desk-prog-cell
    else if fid == desk-focus-cal then peek-32 ds dk-cal-cell
    else if fid == desk-focus-dif then peek-32 ds dk-dif-cell
    else if fid == desk-focus-style then peek-32 ds dk-sty-cell
    else if fid == desk-focus-trk then peek-32 ds dk-trk-cell
    else if fid == desk-focus-mon then peek-32 ds dk-mon-cell
    else if fid == desk-focus-scene then peek-32 ds dk-scene-cell
    else if fid == desk-focus-fish then peek-32 ds dk-fish-cell
    else if fid == desk-focus-bro then peek-32 ds dk-bro-cell
    else if fid == desk-focus-files then peek-32 ds dk-files-cell
    else if fid == desk-focus-edit then peek-32 ds desk-edit-cell
    else 0

 THE SYSTEM MENU SHARES THE LAUNCHER'S BLOCK AND MUST NOT BE GIVEN AN ARM
 HERE. `desk-menu-open` reads `desk-prog-cell` too, deliberately, so that a row
 chosen in one presentation is the row chosen in the other. A window slot is
 not shared state of that kind: two ids over one block would be two windows
 over one rectangle, which is the collision the cascade exists to prevent and
 which no registry row can see.

  desk-wnd-title : Integer -> Text
  desk-wnd-title (fid) =
    if fid == desk-focus-clkp then "Clock"
    else if fid == desk-focus-calc then "Calculator"
    else if fid == desk-focus-con then "Console"
    else if fid == desk-focus-prog then "Programs"
    else if fid == desk-focus-cal then "Calendar"
    else if fid == desk-focus-dif then "Diffusion"
    else if fid == desk-focus-style then "Appearance"
    else if fid == desk-focus-trk then "Issues"
    else if fid == desk-focus-mon then "Monitor"
    else if fid == desk-focus-scene then "3D View"
    else if fid == desk-focus-fish then "Aquarium"
    else if fid == desk-focus-bro then "Web"
    else if fid == desk-focus-files then "Files"
    else if fid == desk-focus-edit then "Edit"
    else ""

 THE PILL'S ICON, KEYED BY FOCUS ID, and it is a second table on purpose.
 The obvious construction is to join a pill's TITLE against `gpr-entries` and
 take that row's `ge-icon`, which would keep one register. It does not work,
 and it fails silently: four of the fifteen titles above are not the
 launcher's label for the same pane (`Edit` against `Editor`, `Web` against
 `Browser`, `Monitor` against `System Info`, and `Programs` is not an entry at
 all), so the join would miss on four and `gicon-named` answers the `file`
 icon for an unknown name rather than refusing. Four pills would quietly wear
 the wrong picture and every count would still agree.

 So it is keyed by the same thing the title is, in the same arm order, and
 `codex/test/apps/desk-chrome-icons` asserts every title-bearing id has one
 and that none resolves to the fallback, which is what a missing arm and a
 misspelled name both look like.

  dk-pill-icon : Integer -> Text
  dk-pill-icon (fid) =
    if fid == desk-focus-clkp then "clock"
    else if fid == desk-focus-calc then "grid"
    else if fid == desk-focus-con then "terminal"
    else if fid == desk-focus-prog then "grid"
    else if fid == desk-focus-cal then "calendar"
    else if fid == desk-focus-dif then "star"
    else if fid == desk-focus-style then "settings"
    else if fid == desk-focus-trk then "bell"
    else if fid == desk-focus-mon then "info"
    else if fid == desk-focus-scene then "image"
    else if fid == desk-focus-fish then "eye"
    else if fid == desk-focus-bro then "cloud"
    else if fid == desk-focus-files then "folder"
    else if fid == desk-focus-edit then "edit"
    else ""

 The TREE is where the panes genuinely differ and it is why this is an
 action rather than a function. The Calculator's and the Console's trees are
 pure functions of their blocks, so painting one while it is not the focused
 window costs nothing beyond the walk. The Clock's is not: it reads the RTC, so a clock painted
 underneath another window still reads the hardware every time that window
 repaints. That is the honest cost of drawing an unfocused window whose
 content is live, and it is bounded by the repaint rate rather than the poll
 rate because `desk-loop`'s callers only repaint on an event or a tick.

 IT TAKES THE SCREEN AS WELL AS THE BLOCK, because one pane's tree is a
 function of the room it was given. The Issues table is built at the height
 the layout engine measured for it (WORKS-23), and inside a window that room
 is the window's content box rather than the pane's. `w`, `h` and `tf` are
 what `desk-trk-win-box` needs to find that box, and `desk-wnd-one` is the
 only caller, which is what makes this widening three lines rather than a
 campaign. The other arms ignore all three.

 AND IT TAKES `base` AND `stride`, WHICH THE MONITOR REPORTS RATHER THAN
 USES. That pane exists to say what the machine is on a board nobody has
 booted before, so the framebuffer address and the scanline stride are two of
 its rows. They could have been cached in its block beside the two device
 booleans and they are NOT, because a framebuffer address is 64 bits, a `ds`
 or block cell is 32, and there is no `poke-qword` -- a truncated address in
 the one pane whose job is to report the address would be a lie exactly where
 it costs most, on a board whose framebuffer sits above 4 GB.

  desk-wnd-tree : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, GopFont -> [Device.Port] WidgetNode
  desk-wnd-tree (fid) (ds) (base) (w) (h) (stride) (tf) =
    if fid == desk-focus-scene | fid == desk-focus-fish | fid == desk-focus-bro | fid == desk-focus-files | fid == desk-focus-edit then act widget-panel "scene-body" DirColumn 0 [] end
    else if fid == desk-focus-mon then act
      hh <- rtc-read 4
      mm <- rtc-read 2
      ss <- rtc-seconds 0
      let ai = acpi-boot 0
      in let clk = dk-pad2 (dk-bcd hh) & ":" & dk-pad2 (dk-bcd mm) & ":" & dk-pad2 ss
      in dk-mon-tree ds base w h stride tf ai clk (peek-32 dk-mon-tick-cell 0)
    end
    else if fid == desk-focus-trk then act
      let srv = tk-init-tracker-db
      in let filter = peek-32 ds 32
      in let sel = peek-32 ds 40
      in let box = desk-trk-win-box w h srv filter sel ds tf
      in gtk-tree srv filter sel (box.lr-h) (box.lr-w)
    end
    else if fid == desk-focus-calc then act gcalc-tree (peek-32 ds 24) end
    else if fid == desk-focus-con then act gcon-tree (peek-32 ds desk-console-cell) end
    else if fid == desk-focus-prog then act gpr-tree (peek-32 ds desk-prog-cell) end
    else if fid == desk-focus-dif then act gdf-tree 0 end
    else if fid == desk-focus-style then act dk-style-tree (peek-32 ds dk-scheme-cell) (peek-32 ds dk-adorn-cell) (peek-32 ds dk-sound-on-cell) end
    else if fid == desk-focus-cal then act
      yy <- rtc-read 9
      mm <- rtc-read 8
      dd <- rtc-read 7
      gcal-tree (2000 + dk-bcd yy) (dk-bcd mm) (dk-bcd dd)
    end
    else act
      idle <- rtc-wait-idle rtc-uip-fuel
      b <- clk-mode 0
      hh <- clk-hour-now b
      mi <- clk-field-value clk-reg-minutes b
      ss <- clk-field-value clk-reg-seconds b
      dd <- clk-field-value clk-reg-day b
      mo <- clk-field-value clk-reg-month b
      yy <- clk-field-value clk-reg-year b
      clk-tree (peek-32 ds desk-clock-cell) hh mi ss dd mo yy
    end

 OPENING A WINDOW CASCADES IT, and without this the second window is exactly
 on top of the first. The default rect is a function of the screen alone, so
 every window that has never been placed gets the same pixels: the registry
 held two, the painter drew two, the focus cycle moved between two, and the
 SCREEN showed one. Every arm passed. The picture is what found it.

 The offset is one step per window already open, applied at the moment of the
 first open and never again -- a window that has been placed keeps where it
 was, so closing and reopening does not walk it down the screen. How far a
 step may carry it, and why each axis wraps in its own room, is
 `dk-wnd-cascade-y`'s paragraph.

  dk-wnd-step-px : Integer -> Integer
  dk-wnd-step-px (w) = 24 * ui-wscale w

 THE CASCADE HAS TO WRAP, and raising the cap is what forced it. The default
 rect is three quarters of the content box centred, so the free margin on each
 side is an EIGHTH of the box, and the offset must stay inside it or the window
 hangs off the bottom of the screen. Measured at 1280x800: the box is 1120 by
 728, so the margins are 140 and 91 against a step of 24, and the FIFTH
 window's offset of 96 already leaves the box vertically. **`dk-wr-max` was 4
 because 91 divided by 24 is 3, not because four windows is what a person may
 have**, and the paragraph above said the step keeps a window inside the box
 "for as many windows as `dk-wr-max` allows", which was true only while the cap
 was that quotient.

 So each axis wraps in its OWN room. That is what keeps the positions distinct
 past the wrap rather than merely inside the box: the two spans are 6 and 4 at
 1280x800, so the pair repeats after twelve windows rather than after four, and
 a cap below that can never place two windows on the same pixels. A single
 shared span would repeat at the smaller of the two and put the fifth window
 exactly under the first, which is the defect the cascade exists to prevent and
 which only a photograph ever caught.

  dk-wnd-span : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-wnd-span (room) (step) = if room < step then 1 else room / step + 1

  dk-wnd-cascade-x : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-wnd-cascade-x (n) (w) =
    let step = dk-wnd-step-px w
    in int-mod n (dk-wnd-span (dk-cbox-w w / 8) step) * step

  dk-wnd-cascade-y : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-wnd-cascade-y (n) (w) (h) (ds) =
    let step = dk-wnd-step-px w
    in int-mod n (dk-wnd-span (dk-cbox-h w h ds / 8) step) * step

 A PANE MAY NEED THE WHOLE CONTENT BOX AS ITS FIRST RECT, and the launcher is
 the one that does. Its tree is two columns of entries plus their group
 headings, sized for the region the chrome used to hand it; in three quarters
 of that box the tail of each column falls past the bottom edge and
 `comp-render-at` clips it. Measured at 1600x900 the first time the launcher
 was drawn as a window: fourteen entries in the tree, TWELVE on the screen,
 the Review pane and the Browser gone, and nothing anywhere saying so. That is
 not a cosmetic loss; it is an application the operator cannot reach.

 NO HIT TEST COULD HAVE FOUND IT, which is why this is written here rather
 than left to the arm. `widget-layout` places a child past its container
 rather than clipping it -- that is the same overflow `works-desk-contract.md`
 section 5.1 records -- so `codex/test/apps/desk-prog-click` finds all
 fourteen reachable under the three-quarter rect too. The layout and the paint
 disagree, and only the paint is what a person can click.

 It is a first PLACEMENT and not the maximised state: the window keeps a
 normal state, so maximise still toggles and a rect the operator later moves
 is still their fact. A pane placed this way does not cascade, because there
 is nowhere for a full-box window to step to.

 THE THREE-QUARTER WINDOW IS SMALLER IN LOGICAL UNITS AT 1600 THAN AT 1280,
 AND THAT IS THE TRAP THIS LIST IS REALLY ABOUT. A tree is laid out in logical
 pixels, `ui-wscale` is 1 below 1600 and 2 at or above it, and the content box
 is 1120 by 728 device at 1280x800 and 1280 by 800 device at 1600x900. So in
 the units a tree is measured in, the WHOLE box goes from 1120 by 728 to 640
 by 400, and three quarters of it goes from 840 by 546 to 480 by 300. **The
 bigger screen is the tighter one, by nearly half.** Every instinct says to
 check the small size and every clipped line here was clipped at the large
 one: the Calendar loses `Esc returns.` at 1600 and shows it at 1280, and the
 Diffusion pane loses its whole status row and its hint the same way.

 So a pane goes in this list because it was LOOKED AT at both sizes and
 clipped at one, not because anybody predicted it would. The panes that are
 not here were looked at too: the Clock, the Calculator and the Console all
 paint their last line inside a three-quarter window at 1600.

 THE CONTENT BOX, ONCE. Every windowed pane that hit-tests or paints its own
 subtree needs the same six lines: read the block, read the state, take the
 titlebar off the top. This was written out four times before the Browser
 wanted a fifth, and four copies of an arithmetic is how the sidebar constant
 got four different values in WORKS-35.

 DEVICE pixels, because that is what a pane's own layout call and
 `comp-render-at` both want, and every caller divides by the scale itself
 where it needs logical.

  dk-wnd-content : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, GopFont, Integer -> LayoutRect
  dk-wnd-content (fid) (w) (h) (ds) (tf) (s) =
    let blk = desk-wnd-blk fid ds
    in let st = dk-wnd-st blk
    in let bh = dk-wnd-bar-h tf s
    in layout-rect (dk-wnd-rx blk w st) (dk-wnd-ry blk w h st ds + bh) (dk-wnd-rw blk w st) (dk-wnd-rh blk w h st ds - bh)

  dk-wnd-wants-box : Integer -> Boolean
  dk-wnd-wants-box (fid) =
    fid == desk-focus-prog | fid == desk-focus-cal | fid == desk-focus-dif | fid == desk-focus-trk | fid == desk-focus-mon

  desk-wnd-open : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  desk-wnd-open (fid) (w) (h) (ds) =
    let ws = peek-32 ds dk-wr-cell
    in let n = dk-wr-count ws
    in let blk = desk-wnd-blk fid ds
    in let full = dk-wnd-wants-box fid
    in let ox = if full then 0 else dk-wnd-cascade-x n w
    in let oy = if full then 0 else dk-wnd-cascade-y n w h ds
    in let p = if dk-wnd-placed blk then 0
       else if full then dk-wnd-place blk (dk-cbox-x w) (dk-cbox-y w) (dk-cbox-w w) (dk-cbox-h w h ds)
       else dk-wnd-place blk (dk-wnd-x w dk-wnd-st-normal + ox) (dk-wnd-y w h dk-wnd-st-normal ds + oy) (dk-wnd-w w dk-wnd-st-normal) (dk-wnd-h w h dk-wnd-st-normal ds)
    in dk-wr-open ws fid

 One window, at the rect its own block says, focused if it is the top of the
 registry.

 A FOURTH per-pane fact, and only one pane has ever needed it. A pane that
 paints something over its own laid tree cannot do it from its `-draw` any
 more, because the painter renders every window and hands the laid trees to
 nobody. The launcher is that pane: its icons ride the LAID tree rather than
 the widget tree, for the reason its own section gives, so they have to be
 drawn where this window put them and at the moment it is drawn.

 It answers 0 for every other pane, which is what "no overlay" means rather
 than a value anything reads.

  desk-wnd-over : Integer, Integer, Integer, GopFont, Integer, WidgetNode, Integer -> Integer
  desk-wnd-over (base) (stride) (ws) (tf) (fid) (laid) (ds) =
    if fid == desk-focus-prog then
      let es = gpr-entries
      in desk-gpr-icons base stride laid (dk-theme-ds ds) ws tf es (list-length es) (gpr-sel (peek-32 ds desk-prog-cell)) 0 ds
    else 0

  desk-wnd-one : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Integer, Integer, Boolean -> [Device.Port] WidgetNode
  desk-wnd-one (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (ds) (fid) (focused) = act
    tree <- desk-wnd-tree fid ds base w h stride tf
    let blk = desk-wnd-blk fid ds
    in let st = dk-wnd-st blk
    in let laid = dk-wnd-frame base w h stride font tf tree (desk-wnd-title fid) ds (dk-wnd-rx blk w st) (dk-wnd-ry blk w h st ds) (dk-wnd-rw blk w st) (dk-wnd-rh blk w h st ds) focused
    in let ov = desk-wnd-over base stride (ui-wscale w) tf fid laid ds
    in laid
  end

 Bottom to top, so the focused window is drawn LAST and therefore over the
 others. The recursion carries the index rather than reversing the array,
 because the array is already in the order the painter wants.

  desk-wnd-walk : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-wnd-walk (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (ds) (ws) (i) =
    if i >= dk-wr-count ws then act 0 end
    else if dk-wnd-minimised (desk-wnd-blk (dk-wr-at ws i) ds) then
      desk-wnd-walk base w h stride font tf ds ws (i + 1)
    else act
      one <- desk-wnd-one base w h stride font tf ds (dk-wr-at ws i) (dk-wr-at ws i == dk-wr-visible ws ds)
      desk-wnd-walk base w h stride font tf ds ws (i + 1)
    end

 ONE WINDOW AND EVERYTHING ABOVE IT, which is what a pane whose CONTENT ticks
 needs and what stops the desk flickering once a second. The Clock's repaint
 used to be `desk-wnd-paint-all`: the chrome and every open window, redrawn on
 the visible framebuffer every second, so with two windows open a person sees
 the whole desk rebuild itself once a second. Nothing outside the ticking
 window changed. Redrawing it alone would be wrong only if something sits over
 it, so the walk starts at its index and runs to the top of the registry,
 which is the array order and therefore the z order.

 It answers 0 for a pane that is not in the registry, which is what a pane
 that has never been opened as a window looks like.

  desk-wnd-index : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  desk-wnd-index (ws) (fid) (i) =
    if i >= dk-wr-count ws then 0 - 1
    else if dk-wr-at ws i == fid then i
    else desk-wnd-index ws fid (i + 1)

  desk-wnd-paint-from : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-wnd-paint-from (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (ds) (fid) =
    let ws = peek-32 ds dk-wr-cell
    in let i = desk-wnd-index ws fid 0
    in if i < 0 then act 0 end
    else desk-wnd-walk base w h stride font tf ds ws i

 THE TASKBAR IS PAINTED AFTER THE WINDOWS AND IS THEREFORE ALWAYS ON TOP.
 Windows may be dragged off the glass since 2026-08-26, and the bottom edge of
 the glass is behind the band: without this, dragging a window down buries the
 taskbar and every pill on it, which is a window you cannot restore from a
 control you cannot see. Measured on the first offscreen drag -- the Calculator
 covered the Cobblestone pill outright.

 It is a re-render of the LAID subtree rather than a second `dk-chrome-paint`,
 which would repaint the desktop panel over the windows as well and undo the
 walk. The clock gate is invalidated after it for the reason
 `dk-chrome-paint` invalidates it: the band's `task-clock` label is empty
 because the time is hand-painted over it, so any render of the band erases
 the time and the next `desk-clock` has to put it back.

 THE ICONS GO BACK ON AFTER IT, and leaving them out is a defect a capture
 caught in one look: the band's buttons carry a leading gutter that
 `desk-chrome-icons` paints into AFTER the chrome render, so a bare re-render
 of the subtree draws the labels and not the pictures. The Cobblestone pill
 came back reading `Cobblestone` with the menu glyph gone.

  desk-wnd-band : Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, WidgetNode, Integer -> Integer
  desk-wnd-band (base) (w) (stride) (font) (tf) (laid) (ds) =
    when widget-find laid "taskbar"
      is None -> 0
      is Just (tb) ->
        let ws = ui-wscale w
        in let r = comp-walk base stride (comp-mul-rect (tb.wn-bounds) ws) font tf tb (dk-theme-ds ds) ws
        in let ic = desk-chrome-icons base stride laid (dk-theme-ds ds) ws tf ds
        in poke-32 ds desk-second-cell (0 - 1)

  desk-wnd-paint-all : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-wnd-paint-all (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (ds) = act
    let chrome = dk-chrome-paint base w h stride font tf (widget-panel "content" DirColumn 0 []) ds
    in act
      all <- desk-wnd-walk base w h stride font tf ds (peek-32 ds dk-wr-cell) 0
      let band = desk-wnd-band base w stride font tf chrome ds
      in desk-topbar base w stride font (ui-scale w) tf h ds
    end
  end

 Which window a click landed on, searched from the TOP down, so an overlap
 resolves to the window that is drawn over the other. It answers the pane's
 focus id and the hit code together is not possible in one Integer, so the
 caller asks twice: this for WHICH, then `dk-wnd-hit` for WHAT.

 A MINIMISED WINDOW IS NOT UNDER THE POINTER, and it took two visible defects
 to say so. `dk-wnd-rx` answers a minimised window's rectangle exactly as it
 answers a visible one, so without the skip a window that is not on the screen
 stays a hit target over the space it used to fill and stays top of the z
 order. Damian, 2026-08-26, with two windows open: minimise one and the other
 takes the active titlebar, but clicking ITS minimise button does nothing --
 the click answers the HIDDEN window, so `onto /= fid`, so `btn` is never
 computed -- and clicking its titlebar falls to the raise arm, which raises the
 hidden window and drags it, drawing an outline the size of a window that is
 not there.

  desk-wnd-at : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, GopFont, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  desk-wnd-at (w) (h) (mx) (my) (tf) (ds) (ws) (i) =
    if i < 0 then desk-focus-none
    else
      let fid = dk-wr-at ws i
      in let blk = desk-wnd-blk fid ds
      in let st = dk-wnd-st blk
      in if dk-wnd-minimised blk then desk-wnd-at w h mx my tf ds ws (i - 1)
      else if dk-wnd-hit (dk-wnd-rx blk w st) (dk-wnd-ry blk w h st ds) (dk-wnd-rw blk w st) (dk-wnd-rh blk w h st ds) mx my tf (ui-wscale w) /= dk-wnd-none then fid
      else desk-wnd-at w h mx my tf ds ws (i - 1)

Section: Dragging a window by its titlebar

 A press on the bar grabs the window, holding the button and moving the
 pointer carries it, and releasing drops it.

 THE BUTTON LEVEL AND THE PRESS EDGE ARE DIFFERENT CELLS OF THE POINTER
 MAILBOX AND A DRAG NEEDS BOTH. `mouse-clicked` reads cell 16, which
 `mouse-consume` fills from `mouse-click-edge`, so it is true only on the
 report where the button went DOWN: that is what starts a drag and it cannot
 continue one. `mouse-buttons` reads cell 12, which is the level. A drag
 written against the edge alone moves the window exactly one sample and then
 stops, which reads as a drag that does not track rather than as a defect.

 The grab OFFSET is what makes the window follow the pointer rather than
 jumping its own corner under it: the distance from the window's origin to
 the point pressed, constant for the whole gesture.

 Two `ds` cells, and they are the LAST TWO the block has
 (`works-desk-contract.md` section 2). The offset is packed because there is
 one cell for it: `dx` is bounded by the window's width and `dy` by the
 titlebar's height, and both are non-negative because the press was inside
 the bar, so `dx * 65536 + dy` is exact for any screen this desk can drive.

  dk-drag-cell : Integer = 120
  dk-drag-off-cell : Integer = 124

Section: Resize zones

 A BAND AROUND THE TOPMOST WINDOW'S RECTANGLE, INSIDE AND OUT. The pointer
 landing in it is what turns the arrow into a sizer, and it is the same test
 the resize drag will grab by, so it is written once here rather than twice.

 IT STRADDLES THE BORDER RATHER THAN SITTING INSIDE IT. A zone wholly inside
 the frame cannot be hit on a window whose edge is against another window, and
 one wholly outside is unreachable when the window is against the glass. Half
 and half is what makes both reachable, and it is why the bound is `rx - g` on
 one side and `rx + g` on the other.

 THE TOPMOST WINDOW AND NOT THE FOCUSED ONE, because the registry's order IS
 the z order and the pointer is over whatever is drawn on top. They are almost
 always the same window; the case where they are not is the one this gets
 right.

 A MAXIMISED WINDOW HAS NO ZONES. Its rectangle is the content box by
 definition, so a resize would have nothing to change and the corner it offers
 would be a lie.

  dk-size-none : Integer = 0
  dk-size-l : Integer = 1
  dk-size-r : Integer = 2
  dk-size-t : Integer = 3
  dk-size-b : Integer = 4
  dk-size-tl : Integer = 5
  dk-size-tr : Integer = 6
  dk-size-bl : Integer = 7
  dk-size-br : Integer = 8

 Six logical pixels, which is twelve device pixels at 1600. The grip has to be
 wider than the border it grabs or it is a pixel hunt, and narrower than the
 titlebar or a drag near the top corner becomes a resize.

  dk-size-grip : Integer = 6

  dk-size-zone-rect : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-size-zone-rect (rx) (ry) (rw) (rh) (g) (mx) (my) =
    if mx < rx - g then dk-size-none
    else if mx > rx + rw + g then dk-size-none
    else if my < ry - g then dk-size-none
    else if my > ry + rh + g then dk-size-none
    else
      let l = mx <= rx + g
      in let r = mx >= rx + rw - g
      in let t = my <= ry + g
      in let b = my >= ry + rh - g
      in if t & l then dk-size-tl
      else if t & r then dk-size-tr
      else if b & l then dk-size-bl
      else if b & r then dk-size-br
      else if l then dk-size-l
      else if r then dk-size-r
      else if t then dk-size-t
      else if b then dk-size-b
      else dk-size-none

  dk-size-zone : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-size-zone (ds) (w) (h) (mx) (my) =
    let fid = dk-wr-visible (peek-32 ds dk-wr-cell) ds
    in if fid == desk-focus-none then dk-size-none
    else
      let blk = desk-wnd-blk fid ds
      in if blk == 0 then dk-size-none
      else
        let st = dk-wnd-st blk
        in if st == dk-wnd-st-max then dk-size-none
        else
          let g = dk-size-grip * ui-scale w
          in dk-size-zone-rect (dk-wnd-rx blk w st) (dk-wnd-ry blk w h st ds) (dk-wnd-rw blk w st) (dk-wnd-rh blk w h st ds) g mx my

 OPPOSITE CORNERS SHARE A CURSOR, which is not an economy: the shape says which
 DIAGONAL the drag runs along, and top-left and bottom-right run along the same
 one.

  dk-size-cursor : Integer -> Integer
  dk-size-cursor (z) =
    if z == dk-size-l then cursor-shape-ew
    else if z == dk-size-r then cursor-shape-ew
    else if z == dk-size-t then cursor-shape-ns
    else if z == dk-size-b then cursor-shape-ns
    else if z == dk-size-tl then cursor-shape-nwse
    else if z == dk-size-br then cursor-shape-nwse
    else if z == dk-size-tr then cursor-shape-nesw
    else if z == dk-size-bl then cursor-shape-nesw
    else cursor-shape-arrow

 THE RECT AT GRAB TIME IS RECORDED, NOT AN ANCHOR. Every one of the eight
 zones is "this side follows the pointer, the opposite side stays", so keeping
 the original rectangle makes all eight read the same way and a corner is just
 two edges answered independently.

 IT MUST BE THE RECT AT GRAB TIME AND NOT THE CURRENT ONE. The step rewrites
 the window's rectangle on every sample, so computing the new size from what
 the block holds NOW would compound each sample onto the last and the window
 would run away from the pointer.

  dk-size-cell : Integer = 144
  dk-size-fid-cell : Integer = 148
  dk-size-x0-cell : Integer = 152
  dk-size-y0-cell : Integer = 156
  dk-size-w0-cell : Integer = 160
  dk-size-h0-cell : Integer = 164

  dk-size-active : Integer -> Integer
  dk-size-active (ds) = peek-32 ds dk-size-cell

  dk-size-begin : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-size-begin (ds) (fid) (z) (x0) (y0) (w0) (h0) =
    let a = poke-32 ds dk-size-fid-cell fid
    in let b = poke-32 ds dk-size-x0-cell x0
    in let c = poke-32 ds dk-size-y0-cell y0
    in let d = poke-32 ds dk-size-w0-cell w0
    in let e = poke-32 ds dk-size-h0-cell h0
    in poke-32 ds dk-size-cell z

  dk-size-end : Integer -> Integer
  dk-size-end (ds) = poke-32 ds dk-size-cell dk-size-none

  dk-size-left : Integer -> Boolean
  dk-size-left (z) = z == dk-size-l | z == dk-size-tl | z == dk-size-bl

  dk-size-right : Integer -> Boolean
  dk-size-right (z) = z == dk-size-r | z == dk-size-tr | z == dk-size-br

  dk-size-top : Integer -> Boolean
  dk-size-top (z) = z == dk-size-t | z == dk-size-tl | z == dk-size-tr

  dk-size-bot : Integer -> Boolean
  dk-size-bot (z) = z == dk-size-b | z == dk-size-bl | z == dk-size-br

 A FLOOR, SO A WINDOW CANNOT BE RESIZED INTO NOTHING. Dragging a left edge
 past the right one would otherwise give a NEGATIVE width, and every consumer
 of that rectangle -- the clip, the frame, the hit test -- would read it a
 different wrong way.

  dk-size-minw : Integer -> Integer
  dk-size-minw (w) = 200 * ui-scale w

  dk-size-minh : Integer -> Integer
  dk-size-minh (w) = 120 * ui-scale w

 THE MOVING EDGE IS CLAMPED AND THE OPPOSITE ONE IS ARITHMETIC. `dk-size-new-x`
 is DERIVED from `dk-size-new-w` rather than computed beside it, and `new-y`
 from `new-h`, so the two edges cannot disagree about where the window went
 when the floor bites. That is an invariant a resize has and a move does not.

  dk-size-new-w : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-size-new-w (z) (x0) (w0) (mx) (minw) =
    if dk-size-left z then
      let nw = (x0 + w0) - mx
      in if nw < minw then minw else nw
    else if dk-size-right z then
      let nw = mx - x0
      in if nw < minw then minw else nw
    else w0

  dk-size-new-x : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-size-new-x (z) (x0) (w0) (mx) (minw) =
    if dk-size-left z then (x0 + w0) - dk-size-new-w z x0 w0 mx minw
    else x0

 THE TOP EDGE STOPS AT THE CONTENT BOX AND SO DOES THE BOTTOM, for the two
 reasons the drag already gives: above the topbar a titlebar cannot be reached,
 and below the content box a window's soft shadow lands under the framebuffer.

  dk-size-new-h : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-size-new-h (z) (y0) (h0) (my) (minh) (w) (h) (ds) =
    if dk-size-top z then
      let top = dk-cbox-y w
      in let ty = if my < top then top else my
      in let nh = (y0 + h0) - ty
      in if nh < minh then minh else nh
    else if dk-size-bot z then
      let bot = dk-cbox-y w + dk-cbox-h w h ds
      in let by = if my > bot then bot else my
      in let nh = by - y0
      in if nh < minh then minh else nh
    else h0

  dk-size-new-y : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-size-new-y (z) (y0) (h0) (my) (minh) (w) (h) (ds) =
    if dk-size-top z then (y0 + h0) - dk-size-new-h z y0 h0 my minh w h ds
    else y0

Section: The loop's own rate

 HOW MANY TIMES A SECOND `desk-loop` GOES ROUND, WITH WHATEVER PANE IS FOCUSED.
 `docs/Designs/Active/OS/DeskScheduler.md` asks for exactly this and says it has
 never been run: a pane whose step is expensive should move this number a lot
 today, and very little once a scheduler owns the tick.

 IT COUNTS ON THE HPET AND NOT ON `rtc-seconds`, which is not a preference.
 `desk-clock` already reads the CMOS once per iteration; a second port read per
 iteration would be a cost paid inside the very loop whose cost is the
 measurement. `hpet-ticks` is a counter read and carries no effect row, so
 nothing up the chain to `opening` changes to admit it.

 BOTH THE COUNTER AND THE STORED START ARE MASKED TO 32 BITS. `peek-32`
 zero-extends and the HPET passes 2^32 in about five minutes at 14 MHz, so a
 full-width tick stored in a cell and read back is a different number. Masking
 both sides makes the subtraction wrap correctly, which is sound as long as the
 window is far shorter than the wrap period: one second against about 300.

 A BOARD WITH NO HPET ANSWERS A ZERO RATE RATHER THAN A WRONG ONE, and so does
 a bed with a pinned clock. `build/desk.ps1 -Rtc` pins the HPET as well as the
 CMOS, so a frozen-clock capture never closes a window and the number stays 0.
 That is correct and it is the reason not to freeze the clock while measuring.

 THE COUNTER ONLY RUNS IF SOMETHING ENABLED IT, and until 2026-08-26 nothing
 here did. `hpet-start` sets the enable bit in the config register, and the
 only caller on the desk's side was `GopScene` building a scene -- so the HPET
 ran if you had opened the 3D pane and did not otherwise. `desk-run` starts it
 now. The first cut of this counter read a frozen counter, never closed a
 window, and therefore never painted at all, which is what made the missing
 start visible.

 A PAINT THAT ONLY HAPPENS ON SUCCESS CANNOT REPORT FAILURE. That first cut
 painted solely when a window closed, so "the HPET is stopped" and "this code
 was never called" were the same blank topbar. It arms on the first iteration
 and paints once immediately, and the text says `no hpet` when the rate is
 unavailable, so the three states are told apart on the glass (L-STATES).

  dk-rate-n-cell : Integer = 128
  dk-rate-t0-cell : Integer = 132
  dk-rate-cell : Integer = 136
  dk-rate-armed-cell : Integer = 140
  dk-rate-mask : Integer = 4294967295

  dk-rate-tick : Integer -> Integer
  dk-rate-tick (ds) =
    let n = peek-32 ds dk-rate-n-cell + 1
    in if peek-32 ds dk-rate-armed-cell == 0 then
      let a = poke-32 ds dk-rate-armed-cell 1
      in let b = poke-32 ds dk-rate-t0-cell (bit-and hpet-ticks dk-rate-mask)
      in let c = poke-32 ds dk-rate-n-cell n
      in 1
    else
      let tps = hpet-ticks-per-second
      in if tps <= 0 then (let z = poke-32 ds dk-rate-n-cell n in 0)
      else
        let now = bit-and hpet-ticks dk-rate-mask
        in let t0 = peek-32 ds dk-rate-t0-cell
        in let d = bit-and (now - t0) dk-rate-mask
        in if d < tps then (let z = poke-32 ds dk-rate-n-cell n in 0)
        else
          let r = poke-32 ds dk-rate-cell (n * tps / d)
          in let a = poke-32 ds dk-rate-t0-cell now
          in let b = poke-32 ds dk-rate-n-cell 0
          in 1

 The topbar and not the taskbar, and that is the whole point of the placement.
 `desk-loop` gates `desk-clock` off while a drag is in progress, so anything
 painted beside the clock stops updating exactly when the drag is happening --
 and the drag is the case this number exists to measure. Nothing gates the
 topbar.

  dk-rate-w : Integer = 96

  desk-rate-paint : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, Integer, GopFont, Integer, UsbMouse, Integer -> Integer
  desk-rate-paint (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (s) (tf) (cs) (mouse) (ds) =
    let hd = cursor-hide cs base stride
    in let pal = dk-pal ds
    in let x = w - dk-rate-w * s
    in let bg = gop-fill-rect base stride x 0 (dk-rate-w * s) (dk-bar-h * s) (pal.pal-border)
    in let t = if hpet-ticks-per-second <= 0 then "no hpet" else show (peek-32 ds dk-rate-cell) & " it/s"
    in let d = desk-line base stride h font s tf x (4 * s) t (pal.pal-accent)
    in dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse

  dk-drag-fid : Integer -> Integer
  dk-drag-fid (ds) = peek-32 ds dk-drag-cell

  dk-drag-dx : Integer -> Integer
  dk-drag-dx (ds) = peek-32 ds dk-drag-off-cell / 65536

  dk-drag-dy : Integer -> Integer
  dk-drag-dy (ds) = peek-32 ds dk-drag-off-cell - dk-drag-dx ds * 65536

  dk-drag-begin : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-drag-begin (ds) (fid) (dx) (dy) =
    let a = poke-32 ds dk-drag-off-cell (dx * 65536 + dy)
    in poke-32 ds dk-drag-cell fid

  dk-drag-end : Integer -> Integer
  dk-drag-end (ds) = poke-32 ds dk-drag-cell desk-focus-none

 BIT 0 AND NOT THE WHOLE WORD, because a drag with a second button also down
 is still a drag.

  dk-drag-held : UsbMouse -> Boolean
  dk-drag-held (mouse) = bit-and (mouse-buttons mouse) 1 /= 0

  dk-drag-clamp : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-drag-clamp (v) (lo) (hi) =
    if v < lo then lo else if v > hi then hi else v

 A WINDOW MAY HANG OFF THE GLASS, AND WHAT STOPS IT HIDING IS A MINIMUM THAT
 STAYS ON. Damian, 2026-08-26: allow them to overlap offscreen, define a
 minimum so it cannot hide, and a window that is totally offscreen is
 basically minimised. The clamp is what makes that last sentence unreachable
 rather than a state to handle: at least `dk-drag-keep` pixels of the window
 remain on both axes, so there is always something to grab and no window can
 be lost by dragging it away.

 It was clamped WHOLLY inside the content box before this, on both axes.

 THE TOP IS THE ONE EDGE THAT STAYS CLOSED, and it is not symmetry that is
 missing. Every other edge can be crossed because the titlebar is still
 reachable afterwards; above the topbar it is not, and a window whose bar
 cannot be reached cannot be brought back by any gesture the desk has.

 SIDEWAYS ONLY, AND THE REASON IS THE PAINTER RATHER THAN TASTE. `gop-put`
 and `gop-get` bound a pixel against `stride`, which is the scan line, so a
 window hanging off the left or right is clipped correctly on both the write
 and the read. Neither has a HEIGHT to bound against, so a row below the glass
 is a write past the end of the framebuffer rather than a visible artifact.

 THE BOTTOM STOPS AT THE CONTENT BOX AND NOT AT THE GLASS, and the difference
 is the SHADOW. `shadow-subtle` is offset 2 with blur 4, so a window paints up
 to six logical pixels -- twelve device pixels at 1600 -- BELOW its own
 rectangle. A bottom of `h - wh` puts the window's last row on the last row of
 the screen and its shadow underneath the framebuffer. The content box ends a
 taskbar above that, which is what leaves the shadow somewhere to land, and it
 is why this bound reads as the old one rather than as the glass.

  dk-drag-keep : Integer = 64

  dk-drag-x : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-drag-x (x) (ww) (w) =
    dk-drag-clamp x (dk-drag-keep - ww) (w - dk-drag-keep)

  dk-drag-y : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-drag-y (y) (wh) (w) (h) (ds) =
    dk-drag-clamp y (dk-cbox-y w) (dk-cbox-y w + dk-cbox-h w h ds - wh)

 WHERE THE WINDOW GOES FOR A POINTER AT `mx`, `my`, which is the whole of the
 move and is named rather than written inline in the step. `desk-wnd-drag-step`
 is effectful and needs a screen, so a test that re-derived this arithmetic
 beside it would be a SHADOW of the painter's, free to drift from it while both
 stayed green. `codex/test/apps/desk-window-drag` calls these two.

  dk-drag-to-x : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-drag-to-x (ds) (mx) (ww) (w) = dk-drag-x (mx - dk-drag-dx ds) ww w

  dk-drag-to-y : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-drag-to-y (ds) (my) (wh) (w) (h) = dk-drag-y (my - dk-drag-dy ds) wh w h ds

 A MAXIMISED WINDOW IS NOT DRAGGABLE, and that is a refusal rather than an
 omission. `dk-wnd-rx` reads the content box on the maximised arm whatever
 the block says, so a drag would write a rectangle nothing reads and the
 window would sit still under a pointer carrying it. Restoring on grab is
 what a desktop usually does and it is a second gesture, not this one.

 THE MOVE ERASES THE RECTANGLE IT LEFT AND NOTHING ELSE, and that is what
 stops a drag flickering. It called `desk-wnd-repaint`, which fills the WHOLE
 screen with the wall and then rebuilds the chrome and every window on top of
 it. On a framebuffer with no back buffer the scanout catches the gap between
 the erase and the redraw, so at a mouse sample's rate the whole desk strobes:
 Damian saw it on the first drag he tried, 2026-08-26. That is the same defect
 `desk-draw-clock`'s own paragraph records one level down, where rebuilding
 the taskbar subtree once a second made the clock flash.

 NOTHING IS REPAINTED WHILE THE BUTTON IS DOWN. An outline follows the
 pointer and the window itself moves once, on release.

 THE FIRST TWO ATTEMPTS BOTH MISSED, and the second missed because the first
 measurement was never taken. The full repaint was narrowed from the whole
 screen down to the chrome, on the reasoning that the screen-sized wall fill
 ahead of it was the strobe. Damian's answer after testing it was "still
 flickers just the same", which is the measurement that says the mechanism was
 wrong rather than the fix insufficient (L-MECHANISM). The wall fill WAS
 redundant, and removing it was still not the defect: the window's own pixels
 are the ones that blink, and every repaint of a window erases and rebuilds
 them.

 THE OUTLINE IS THE WINDOW'S OWN RECT, which is why this needs no state at
 all. A drag writes each new position through `dk-wnd-place` as it goes, so
 the cells that say where the window IS are the cells that say where the
 outline is, and the release has nothing left to commit. The alternative was
 two more `ds` cells, and the block is full, so it would have grown the
 allocation and every one of the twenty test chapters that builds one.

 The window is therefore PAINTED at one place and RECORDED at another for the
 length of the gesture. Nothing may repaint it in that window, and the one
 thing that would have is the taskbar clock, which `desk-loop` now holds while
 a drag is live.

 THE CURSOR IS BRACKETED AROUND EVERY XOR, and this is the trap section 5 of
 the contract already names for repaints. The arrow keeps the pixels it
 covered so it can put them back; an outline drawn under a shown cursor is
 inside that saved copy, and the next cursor move puts a stale piece of it
 back on the wall.

 PICKING A WINDOW UP REPAINTS NOTHING, even when the press also RAISES it.
 The raise arm used to run `desk-wnd-repaint` -- the whole screen, every
 window's tree rebuilt -- and only then draw the outline, which put a full
 relayout of the grabbed window on the critical path of the gesture. When the
 press begins a drag that repaint is skipped entirely: the window is raised
 and focused in the registry, the outline goes up, and the z order and the
 titlebar colours are settled by the repaint the RELEASE does anyway. A press
 that raises WITHOUT dragging, on a window's body, still repaints at once,
 because nothing later would.

 Measured in the bed the repaint it skips is under 30 ms, which is not what
 Damian is seeing; the reason to cut it anyway is that this file records a
 desk paint as near a second ON METAL, where it is the whole gesture.

  dk-drag-edge : Integer = 2

  desk-wnd-drag-step : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-wnd-drag-step (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (mouse) (ds) (cs) =
    let fid = dk-drag-fid ds
    in let blk = desk-wnd-blk fid ds
    in let st = dk-wnd-st blk
    in let ww = dk-wnd-rw blk w st
    in let wh = dk-wnd-rh blk w h st ds
    in let ox = dk-wnd-rx blk w st
    in let oy = dk-wnd-ry blk w h st ds
    in if dk-drag-held mouse == False then act
      hd <- act cursor-hide cs base stride end
      let e = gop-xor-frame base stride w h ox oy ww wh dk-drag-edge
      in let d = dk-drag-end ds
      in act
        r <- desk-wnd-paint-all base w h stride font tf ds
        c <- act dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse end
        desk-wnd-ev-stay
      end
    end
    else
      let nx = dk-drag-to-x ds (mouse-x mouse) ww w
      in let ny = dk-drag-to-y ds (mouse-y mouse) wh w h
      in if nx == ox & ny == oy then act desk-wnd-ev-stay end
      else act
        hd <- act cursor-hide cs base stride end
        let e = gop-xor-frame base stride w h ox oy ww wh dk-drag-edge
        in let p = dk-wnd-place blk nx ny ww wh
        in let n = gop-xor-frame base stride w h nx ny ww wh dk-drag-edge
        in act
          c <- act dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse end
          desk-wnd-ev-stay
        end
      end

 THE RESIZE STEP IS THE DRAG STEP WITH A RECTANGLE INSTEAD OF AN ORIGIN, and
 it is a second function rather than an arm inside the first because the two
 differ in what they read: a move needs the grab offset, a resize needs the
 rect it started from and which of its edges is moving.

 IT ERASES THE RECTANGLE IT LEFT AND NOTHING ELSE, for the reason the move
 records one level up: nothing repaints while the button is down, so the desk
 cannot strobe.

  desk-wnd-size-step : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-wnd-size-step (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (mouse) (ds) (cs) =
    let z = dk-size-active ds
    in let fid = peek-32 ds dk-size-fid-cell
    in let blk = desk-wnd-blk fid ds
    in let st = dk-wnd-st blk
    in let ox = dk-wnd-rx blk w st
    in let oy = dk-wnd-ry blk w h st ds
    in let ow = dk-wnd-rw blk w st
    in let oh = dk-wnd-rh blk w h st ds
    in if dk-drag-held mouse == False then act
      hd <- act cursor-hide cs base stride end
      let e = gop-xor-frame base stride w h ox oy ow oh dk-drag-edge
      in let d = dk-size-end ds
      in act
        r <- desk-wnd-paint-all base w h stride font tf ds
        c <- act dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse end
        desk-wnd-ev-stay
      end
    end
    else
      let x0 = dk-cell-signed (peek-32 ds dk-size-x0-cell)
      in let y0 = dk-cell-signed (peek-32 ds dk-size-y0-cell)
      in let w0 = peek-32 ds dk-size-w0-cell
      in let h0 = peek-32 ds dk-size-h0-cell
      in let nw = dk-size-new-w z x0 w0 (mouse-x mouse) (dk-size-minw w)
      in let nx = dk-size-new-x z x0 w0 (mouse-x mouse) (dk-size-minw w)
      in let nh = dk-size-new-h z y0 h0 (mouse-y mouse) (dk-size-minh w) w h ds
      in let ny = dk-size-new-y z y0 h0 (mouse-y mouse) (dk-size-minh w) w h ds
      in if nx == ox & ny == oy & nw == ow & nh == oh then act desk-wnd-ev-stay end
      else act
        hd <- act cursor-hide cs base stride end
        let e = gop-xor-frame base stride w h ox oy ow oh dk-drag-edge
        in let p = dk-wnd-place blk nx ny nw nh
        in let n = gop-xor-frame base stride w h nx ny nw nh dk-drag-edge
        in act
          c <- act dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse end
          desk-wnd-ev-stay
        end
      end

Section: The window chrome's own step

 Focus, alt-tab and the titlebar buttons, once, for every windowed pane. Both
 panes were going to need the identical eight branches, and a second copy of
 focus handling is how two windows start disagreeing about which of them is
 focused.

 It answers what the pane should do rather than doing it: `pass` means this
 was not a window event and the pane should carry on with its own keys and
 its own clicks, which is what keeps a click on the keypad reaching the
 keypad.

  desk-wnd-ev-pass : Integer = 0
  desk-wnd-ev-stay : Integer = 1
  desk-wnd-ev-close : Integer = 2

 MINIMISE answers its own code because the pane must leave through
 `desk-step-hide` rather than through a repaint. The taskbar is part of the
 chrome, so the pill only exists once something rebuilds the chrome tree, and
 `desk-app-hide` is the shipped path that does it and leaves the pane ALIVE.
 Answering `stay` and repainting would draw the window out of existence with
 no pill anywhere, which is the second close the design warns about.

  desk-wnd-ev-min : Integer = 3

 Tab cycles. It is Tab and not Alt-Tab because the desk tracks no modifier
 state: `kbd-take` answers a scancode and nothing accumulates a shift or an
 alt across calls, so an Alt-Tab today would be a claim the keyboard layer
 cannot support. The design's word for the feature is alt-tab and the key is
 Tab; that difference is written here rather than left for somebody to
 discover by pressing it.

  desk-wnd-key-cycle : Integer = 15

  desk-wnd-chrome-step : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Boolean -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-wnd-chrome-step (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (fid) (sc) (clicked) =
    let ws = peek-32 ds dk-wr-cell
    in let blk = desk-wnd-blk fid ds
    in let st = dk-wnd-st blk
    in let onto = if clicked then desk-wnd-at w h (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) tf ds ws (dk-wr-count ws - 1) else desk-focus-none
    in let btn = if clicked & onto == fid then dk-wnd-hit (dk-wnd-rx blk w st) (dk-wnd-ry blk w h st ds) (dk-wnd-rw blk w st) (dk-wnd-rh blk w h st ds) (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) tf (ui-wscale w) else dk-wnd-none
    in let zone = if clicked & fid == dk-wr-visible ws ds & st /= dk-wnd-st-max then dk-size-zone-rect (dk-wnd-rx blk w st) (dk-wnd-ry blk w h st ds) (dk-wnd-rw blk w st) (dk-wnd-rh blk w h st ds) (dk-size-grip * ui-scale w) (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) else dk-size-none
    in if dk-size-active ds /= dk-size-none then desk-wnd-size-step base w h stride font tf mouse ds cs
    else if dk-drag-fid ds /= desk-focus-none then desk-wnd-drag-step base w h stride font tf mouse ds cs
    else if sc == desk-wnd-key-cycle & dk-wr-count ws > 1 then act
      let t = dk-wr-cycle ws
      in let f = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell t
      in act
        r <- desk-wnd-repaint base w h stride font tf cs mouse ds
        desk-wnd-ev-stay
      end
    end
    else if btn == dk-wnd-close then act desk-wnd-ev-close end
    else if btn == dk-wnd-max then act
      let t = poke-32 blk dk-wnd-st-cell (if st == dk-wnd-st-max then dk-wnd-st-normal else dk-wnd-st-max)
      in act
        r <- desk-wnd-repaint base w h stride font tf cs mouse ds
        desk-wnd-ev-stay
      end
    end
    else if btn == dk-wnd-min then act
      let t = poke-32 blk dk-wnd-st-cell dk-wnd-st-min
      in desk-wnd-ev-min
    end

 THE BUTTONS COME FIRST, THE BAND SECOND, AND THE RAISE THIRD, and none of
 those three placements is free. A corner zone overlaps the close button, so
 buttons first or the top-right corner resizes the window it should be
 closing. And the band reaches OUTSIDE the rect, where the point may also be
 inside a window further down the z order: raise first and that grab raises the
 lower window instead of resizing the top one, which is the half-working
 behaviour Damian found. `btn` is non-none only when `onto == fid` and the
 raise arm runs only when `onto /= fid`, so the two are mutually exclusive and
 reordering them changes nothing else.

    else if zone /= dk-size-none then act
      hd <- act cursor-hide cs base stride end
      let g = dk-size-begin ds fid zone (dk-wnd-rx blk w st) (dk-wnd-ry blk w h st ds) (dk-wnd-rw blk w st) (dk-wnd-rh blk w h st ds)
      in let o = gop-xor-frame base stride w h (dk-wnd-rx blk w st) (dk-wnd-ry blk w h st ds) (dk-wnd-rw blk w st) (dk-wnd-rh blk w h st ds) dk-drag-edge
      in act
        c <- act dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse end
        desk-wnd-ev-stay
      end
    end
    else if clicked & onto /= desk-focus-none & onto /= fid then act
      let t = dk-wr-raise ws onto
      in let f = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell t
      in let ob = desk-wnd-blk onto ds
      in let ost = dk-wnd-st ob
      in let ohit = dk-wnd-hit (dk-wnd-rx ob w ost) (dk-wnd-ry ob w h ost ds) (dk-wnd-rw ob w ost) (dk-wnd-rh ob w h ost ds) (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) tf (ui-wscale w)
      in let g = if ohit == dk-wnd-bar & ost /= dk-wnd-st-max then dk-drag-begin ds onto (mouse-x mouse - dk-wnd-rx ob w ost) (mouse-y mouse - dk-wnd-ry ob w h ost ds) else 0
      in if dk-drag-fid ds /= desk-focus-none then act
        hd <- act cursor-hide cs base stride end
        let o = gop-xor-frame base stride w h (dk-wnd-rx ob w ost) (dk-wnd-ry ob w h ost ds) (dk-wnd-rw ob w ost) (dk-wnd-rh ob w h ost ds) dk-drag-edge
        in act
          c <- act dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse end
          desk-wnd-ev-stay
        end
      end
      else act
        r <- desk-wnd-repaint base w h stride font tf cs mouse ds
        desk-wnd-ev-stay
      end
    end
    else if btn == dk-wnd-bar & st /= dk-wnd-st-max then act
      hd <- act cursor-hide cs base stride end
      let g = dk-drag-begin ds fid (mouse-x mouse - dk-wnd-rx blk w st) (mouse-y mouse - dk-wnd-ry blk w h st ds)
      in let o = gop-xor-frame base stride w h (dk-wnd-rx blk w st) (dk-wnd-ry blk w h st ds) (dk-wnd-rw blk w st) (dk-wnd-rh blk w h st ds) dk-drag-edge
      in act
        c <- act dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse end
        desk-wnd-ev-stay
      end
    end
    else act desk-wnd-ev-pass end

 A full repaint, wall included, because a window that moved to the back
 leaves its old pixels where they were and only the wall can take them off.

  desk-wnd-repaint : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Integer, UsbMouse, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-wnd-repaint (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (cs) (mouse) (ds) = act
    hd <- act cursor-hide cs base stride end
    let bg = gop-fill-rect base stride 0 0 w h (dk-wall ds)
    in act
      p <- desk-wnd-paint-all base w h stride font tf ds
      dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
    end
  end

  desk-clock-read : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
 THIS WINDOW AND WHATEVER IS OVER IT, not the whole desk. It was every
 window and the chrome with them, on the reasoning that a repaint of one pane
 would erase the window beside it -- which is true of a pane that paints the
 screen and false of one that paints inside its own rectangle. What it cost
 was a full-screen rebuild once a second, visible as a flicker the moment two
 windows are open. The RTC reads that used to sit here are in
 `desk-wnd-tree`'s clock arm.

  desk-clock-read (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (ks) (ds) =
    desk-wnd-paint-from base w h stride font tf ds desk-focus-clkp

  desk-clock-paint : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
 OPENING PAINTS EVERYTHING AND A TICK PAINTS ONE WINDOW, and the two must not
 share a function. They did, and the tick's narrowing silently narrowed the
 open with it: the wall went down, the clock's window went on top of it, and
 the chrome was never drawn at all. The screen then looked correct for a
 second, because the next tick redrew the chrome under the old arrangement.

  desk-clock-paint (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (ks) (ds) = act
    let bg = gop-fill-rect base stride 0 0 w h (dk-wall ds)
    in desk-wnd-paint-all base w h stride font tf ds
  end

  desk-clock-repaint : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Integer, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-clock-repaint (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (cs) (mouse) (ks) (ds) = act
    hd <- act cursor-hide cs base stride end
    p <- desk-clock-read base w h stride font tf ks ds
    dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
  end

 The tick gate is the RTC second rather than a spin count, for the reason
 `desk-clock` records: the PIT stops on the ASUS at about 54 seconds and
 a repaint gated on a dead counter never fires again.

 `last`, the second this pane painted, was a loop PARAMETER and a step has no
 loop to carry one in. It goes in the pane's own state block rather than a `ds`
 cell: `ds` has two cells left and this is a repaint cache, while the clock
 block is 64 bytes of which `GopClock` uses 0 to 36. **Offsets 40 and up in
 that block are the desk's, 0 to 36 are `GopClock`'s**, and that split is
 written here and in the contract because nothing in the tree cross-checks it.

  dk-clkp-last-cell : Integer = 40

 Cell 44 is the window state and it is `dk-wnd-st-cell`, in the Windows
 section, because the Calculator now keeps its state at the same offset. It
 was `dk-clkp-state-cell` here while the clock was the only windowed pane.

 The F12 arm is NEW. The contract requires every pane to carry it and this
 loop never did, so the screenshot key was silently dead in the clock pane;
 `clk-key` answers 0 for scancode 88 and the key fell through to the ignore
 arm. Converting the pane is when that shows up, so it is fixed here.

  desk-clock-pane-step : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, Integer, Boolean -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-clock-pane-step (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (sc) (clicked) = act
    let mark = __heap-save
    in act
      now <- rtc-seconds-unguarded 0
      ev <- desk-wnd-chrome-step base w h stride font tf mouse ds cs desk-focus-clkp sc clicked
      let ks = peek-32 ds desk-clock-cell
      in if ev == desk-wnd-ev-close then let d = __heap-restore mark in 0
      else if ev == desk-wnd-ev-min then let d = __heap-restore mark in desk-step-hide
      else if ev == desk-wnd-ev-stay then
        let u = poke-32 ks dk-clkp-last-cell now
        in let d = __heap-restore mark
        in 1
      else if sc == 88 then act
        s <- desk-shot base w h stride font ds
        let d = __heap-restore mark
        in 1
      end
      else if sc == 0 then
        if now == peek-32 ks dk-clkp-last-cell then
          let d = __heap-restore mark
          in 1
        else act
          p <- desk-clock-repaint base w h stride font tf cs mouse ks ds
          let u = poke-32 ks dk-clkp-last-cell now
          in let d = __heap-restore mark
          in 1
        end
      else act
        let a = clk-key ks sc
        in if a == 3 then let d = __heap-restore mark in 0
        else if a == 2 then act
          l <- clk-load ks
          p <- desk-clock-repaint base w h stride font tf cs mouse ks ds
          let u = poke-32 ks dk-clkp-last-cell now
          in let d = __heap-restore mark
          in 1
        end
        else if a == 4 then act
          c <- clk-commit ks
          z <- zone-save (clk-zone ks)
          let e = poke-32 ks clk-editing-cell 0
          in act
            p <- desk-clock-repaint base w h stride font tf cs mouse ks ds
            let u = poke-32 ks dk-clkp-last-cell now
            in let d = __heap-restore mark
            in 1
          end
        end
        else if a == 1 then act
          p <- desk-clock-repaint base w h stride font tf cs mouse ks ds
          let u = poke-32 ks dk-clkp-last-cell now
          in let d = __heap-restore mark
          in 1
        end
        else
          let d = __heap-restore mark
          in 1
      end
    end
  end

  desk-clock-open : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-clock-open (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (ks) (apps) (root) = act
    hd <- cursor-hide cs base stride
    e <- clk-ensure ks
    let o = desk-wnd-open desk-focus-clkp w h ds
    in act
      p0 <- desk-clock-paint base w h stride font tf ks ds
      c1 <- act dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse end
      let u = poke-32 ks dk-clkp-last-cell (0 - 1)
      in let sf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell (dk-wr-top (peek-32 ds dk-wr-cell))
      in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
    end
  end

Section: Programs

 The launcher pane. It answers a SCANCODE and hands it to `desk-dispatch`,
 which is the same road a keystroke takes, so adding an app to
 `gpr-entries` needs no change here and no second dispatch table.

 Icons ride the LAID tree and not the widget tree, which is the same road
 `desk-taskbar-clock` takes over the band it repaints. A node carrying an
 icon child would be found by `ev-hit-widget` before its parent, so every
 click on an icon would resolve to a node the dispatch table has never
 heard of; painting after the walk cannot move a hit test at all.

 The bounds on a laid tree are LOGICAL, so a device rectangle is
 `comp-mul-rect` of the content box, exactly as the compositor does it. The
 icon centres on the content box rather than on the glyph height, because
 the glyph height depends on which face loaded and the box does not.

 An icon sits in the LEADING GUTTER of a label, so where it starts and how
 much room it has are both questions about rendered TEXT, and neither can be
 answered by counting characters. A loaded face is proportional: a string's
 width is not its length times anything, and three spaces of gutter are far
 narrower than three 8 pixel cells. This measured the gutter as
 `cells * comp-cell-w * ws` until 2026-08-20, which is the monospace
 assumption `GopFont`'s own prose warns about, and the moment the labels
 were centred properly and the face was actually rendering, every icon on
 the launcher and the sidebar sat on top of its first letter.

 So the caller names the two STRINGS instead: what precedes the icon, and
 the gutter the icon occupies. Both are measured through the face when there
 is one, and fall back to the cell only when there is not.

 The icon is then scaled to the room it actually has rather than to a fixed
 multiple, which is what keeps it inside the gutter at any face and any
 scale. Eight is the artwork's width; a gutter narrower than eight device
 pixels still draws, at scale 1, rather than vanishing.

  desk-text-w : GopFont, Integer, Text -> Integer
  desk-text-w (tf) (ws) (t) =
    if tf.gf-ok then gfont-text-w tf t else text-length t * comp-cell-w * ws

 A drawing is used when one exists for the name AND it was rasterized at the
 size this gutter asks for. The size is settled in `desk-run` from the same
 expression below, so the two agree on every ordinary paint; a face or a
 scale that moved since boot makes them disagree, and the eight pixel mask
 draws instead of a wrong size being stretched.

  desk-icon-in : Integer, Integer, WidgetNode, Theme, Integer, GopFont, Text, Text, Text, Integer -> Integer
  desk-icon-in (base) (stride) (n) (th) (ws) (tf) (prefix) (gutter) (name) (ds) =
    let st = widget-resolve-style n th
    in let c = comp-mul-rect (box-content-from-style (n.wn-bounds) st) ws
    in let avail = desk-text-w tf ws gutter
    in let scale = if avail < 8 then 1 else avail / 8
    in let sh = 8 * scale
    in let ih = dk-icon-px avail
    in let sy = c.lr-y + (if c.lr-h > sh then (c.lr-h - sh) / 2 else 0)
    in let iy = c.lr-y + (if c.lr-h > ih then (c.lr-h - ih) / 2 else 0)
    in let ix = c.lr-x + desk-text-w tf ws prefix
    in let blk = peek-32 ds dk-icons-cell
    in if blk == 0 then gicon-blit base stride (gicon-named name) ix sy scale (st.ws-fg)
    else if gicon-drawn name == False then gicon-blit base stride (gicon-named name) ix sy scale (st.ws-fg)
    else if dk-icons-size blk /= ih then gicon-blit base stride (gicon-named name) ix sy scale (st.ws-fg)
    else gicon-blit-cov base stride (dk-icons-at blk name) ih ix iy (st.ws-fg)

  desk-icon-on : Integer, Integer, WidgetNode, Theme, Integer, GopFont, Text, Text, Text, Text, Integer -> Integer
  desk-icon-on (base) (stride) (laid) (th) (ws) (tf) (prefix) (gutter) (id) (name) (ds) =
    when widget-find laid id
      is Just (n) -> desk-icon-in base stride n th ws tf prefix gutter name ds
      is None -> 0

 `gpr-entries` is a module-level list, so every `gpr-at` rebuilds all
 fourteen records. The list is bound ONCE here and indexed, which is why
 this loop carries it and a length rather than calling `gpr-at`.

 The prefix is the row's own selection mark and not a constant, because
 `> ` and two spaces are different WIDTHS in a proportional face. The label
 shifts by that difference on the selected row, so the icon has to shift
 with it or it sits under the `C` of one row in fourteen.

  desk-gpr-icons : Integer, Integer, WidgetNode, Theme, Integer, GopFont, List GprEntry, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  desk-gpr-icons (base) (stride) (laid) (th) (ws) (tf) (es) (n) (sel) (i) (ds) =
    if i >= n then 0
    else let e = list-at es i
    in let d = desk-icon-on base stride laid th ws tf (gpr-mark i sel) (gpr-gutter) ("gpr-" & e.ge-label) (e.ge-icon) ds
    in desk-gpr-icons base stride laid th ws tf es n sel (i + 1) ds

  desk-programs-draw : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-programs-draw (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (ps) (ds) =
    desk-wnd-paint-all base w h stride font tf ds

  desk-programs-paint : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-programs-paint (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (ps) (ds) = act
    let bg = gop-fill-rect base stride 0 0 w h (dk-wall ds)
    in desk-programs-draw base w h stride font tf ps ds
  end

  desk-programs-repaint : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Integer, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-programs-repaint (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (cs) (mouse) (ps) (ds) = act
    hd <- act cursor-hide cs base stride end
    p <- desk-programs-draw base w h stride font tf ps ds
    dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
  end

 IT MUST LAY THE TREE OUT WHERE THE PAINTER PUT IT, which is the fifth thing a
 pane takes on joining the window world and the one that fails silently. This
 laid at the screen corner over the whole chrome, which is where
 `dk-chrome-paint` drew it; the painter now renders it inside the window's
 content box, so a click resolved against the old layout answers whichever ROW
 sits at the same offset from the corner -- a launcher entry that is wrong but
 plausible, which is exactly what a screenshot cannot show. `dk-calc-hit` is
 the worked example and this repeats its rect arithmetic.

  dk-prog-hit : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, GopFont -> Integer
  dk-prog-hit (w) (h) (ps) (mx) (my) (ds) (tf) =
    let probe = __heap-save
    in let s = ui-wscale w
    in let blk = peek-32 ds desk-prog-cell
    in let st = dk-wnd-st blk
    in let bh = dk-wnd-bar-h tf s
    in let wx = dk-wnd-rx blk w st
    in let wy = dk-wnd-ry blk w h st ds + bh
    in let ww = dk-wnd-rw blk w st
    in let wh = dk-wnd-rh blk w h st ds - bh
    in let laid = widget-layout (gpr-tree ps) (dk-theme-ds ds) (layout-rect (wx / s) (wy / s) (ww / s) (wh / s))
    in let code = when ev-hit-widget laid (comp-hit-x mx s) (comp-hit-y my s)
          is Just (wgt) -> gpr-id-scan (wgt.wn-id) 0
          is None -> 0
    in let dropped = __heap-restore probe
    in code

 The launcher is the pane that made the step protocol grow a third answer. It
 does not merely close: it closes AND names the app to open, which is the whole
 point of it. **A step returns 0 to close, 1 to stay open, and anything else as
 a SCANCODE to dispatch after closing.** That is unambiguous rather than a
 convention: every scancode `desk-dispatch` tests is 2 or more, 1 is Esc and
 already means close, and 0 is no key. So the launcher's answer travels as
 itself and no cell has to carry it.

 `gpr-key` moves the selection as well as answering, so like `gcalc-key` it is
 evaluated exactly once.

  desk-prog-step : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, Integer, Boolean -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-prog-step (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (sc) (clicked) = act
    ev <- desk-wnd-chrome-step base w h stride font tf mouse ds cs desk-focus-prog sc clicked
    let ps = peek-32 ds desk-prog-cell
    in let hit = if clicked & ev == desk-wnd-ev-pass then dk-prog-hit w h ps (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) ds tf else 0
    in if ev == desk-wnd-ev-close then 0
    else if ev == desk-wnd-ev-min then desk-step-hide
    else if ev == desk-wnd-ev-stay then 1
    else if hit /= 0 then hit
    else if sc == 88 then act
      let mark = __heap-save
      in act
        s <- desk-shot base w h stride font ds
        let d = __heap-restore mark
        in 1
      end
    end
    else if sc == 0 then 1
    else
      act
        let mark = __heap-save
        in act
          p <- desk-programs-repaint base w h stride font tf cs mouse ps ds
          let d = __heap-restore mark
          in 1
        end
      end
  end

 The system menu is the LAUNCHER, placed differently. `gpr-tree` is the whole
 of its content and `gpr-key` and `gpr-id-scan` are the whole of its behaviour,
 so a row and the key it names still arrive by the same road and there is no
 second table to keep in step. What this adds is placement and one row the
 launcher does not have.

 It shares `desk-prog-cell` rather than taking a cell of its own, and the
 shared SELECTION is the point rather than a saving: the two presentations are
 one model, so a row chosen in one is the row chosen in the other.

 `widget-set-min` preserves flex, which is the trap `BROWSER-2` records, so
 every wrapper here sets flex explicitly. A menu column that stayed flexible
 would stretch to the full height and stop being a menu.

 THREE FLEX SPACERS PIN IT TO THE BOTTOM LEFT, ABOVE THE PILL THAT OPENS IT.
 A paragraph here used to say a panel does not size to its children on either
 axis, and therefore that a bottom-anchored box gets zero height. That is
 false: `widget-measure` sets `wn-min-h` to `box-max` of the node's own
 minimum and the SUM of its children's, and `widget-layout` is
 `widget-arrange (widget-measure w th)`, so a minimum does propagate. What
 actually bites is the sentence above it -- `widget-panel` defaults flex to 1
 and `widget-set-min` preserves it -- so the box stretched rather than
 collapsed, and the two look alike from the far side of a capture. A flex-0
 body takes exactly its measured minimum out of `flex-col-place` and a flex-1
 spacer above it takes the rest, which is the whole of the anchoring.

 A FLOOR NOW, NOT THE WIDTH. It was the width: `gpr-row` pinned every row's
 width minimum to zero, so the menu measured 40 and this constant was the only
 thing sizing it, which is why it drew far wider than its longest row. Damian,
 2026-08-26, on testing it: way too wide, autosize it to fit the pills. The
 rows carry the constructor's guess now and `comp-fit-text` replaces that with
 what the FACE measures, so the panel sizes to its children the way
 `widget-measure` has always said a panel does. The floor stays so a menu whose
 group is collapsed to two short words is still a menu and not a sliver.

  desk-menu-w : Integer = 120

 IT DOES NOT USE `gpr-tree`, AND THE MEASUREMENT THAT SETTLED THAT IS ON THE
 GLASS. The window's tree is two columns given the whole content region. In a
 box above the pill there are about 400 logical rows at 1600x900, and the flat
 list wants more: captured, the Shutdown row landed past the bottom of the
 screen and the taskbar was behind it, and the box measured 1000 device pixels
 wide because a panel minimum is a floor and `gpr-tree`'s own was larger.
 `gpr-menu-tree` is the collapsed presentation of the same model and it is
 what fits; `GopPrograms` carries the account of why it is not a second model.

 Shutdown is here because it is the one thing on the desk that is not an app,
 and it was the last button left in the sidebar when the sidebar went. It
 carries `desk-chrome-gutter` rather than `gpr-gutter` because
 `desk-chrome-icons` paints its icon and a launcher row's icon is painted by
 `desk-gpr-icons` from a different prefix.

  desk-menu-body : Integer -> WidgetNode
  desk-menu-body (ps) =
    let bye = widget-set-min (widget-button "shutdown" (desk-chrome-gutter & "Shutdown")) 0 22
    in widget-panel "sysmenu-body" DirColumn 4 [widget-set-flex (gpr-menu-tree ps) 0, bye]

 THE FIT RUNS HERE AND NOT IN THE PAINTER, because the HIT test lays this
 same tree out and the two must agree. `desk-chrome-face` fits the taskbar
 subtree only, so the menu's own rows would otherwise keep the eight pixel
 guess in the layout while the face drew something wider: the measuring pass
 and the paint disagreeing is the failure `works-desk-contract.md` names for
 the Issues table, and here it would put every row's right edge in the wrong
 place for the click. One function builds the tree, both callers take it
 fitted.

  desk-menu-tree : Integer, GopFont, Integer -> WidgetNode
  desk-menu-tree (ps) (tf) (s) =
    let body = widget-set-flex (comp-fit-text (desk-menu-body ps) tf s) 0
    in let tgap = widget-set-flex (widget-label "sysmenu-tgap" "") 1
    in let col = widget-set-flex (widget-set-min (widget-panel "sysmenu-col" DirColumn 0 [tgap, body]) desk-menu-w 0) 0
    in let rgap = widget-set-flex (widget-panel "sysmenu-rgap" DirColumn 0 []) 1
    in widget-panel "sysmenu-root" DirRow 0 [col, rgap]

  desk-menu-draw : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  desk-menu-draw (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (ps) (ds) =
    let laid = dk-chrome-paint base w h stride font tf (desk-menu-tree ps tf (ui-wscale w)) ds
    in let ic = desk-gpr-icons base stride laid (dk-theme-ds ds) (ui-wscale w) tf gpr-entries gpr-count (gpr-sel ps) 0 ds
    in desk-topbar base w stride font (ui-scale w) tf h ds

  desk-menu-repaint : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Integer, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  desk-menu-repaint (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (cs) (mouse) (ps) (ds) =
    let hd = cursor-hide cs base stride
    in let p = desk-menu-draw base w h stride font tf ps ds
    in dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse

 NOT A SCANCODE, AND DELIBERATELY OUT OF THE RANGE OF ONE. A menu row answers
 the scancode the desk already dispatches for that pane, which is what keeps
 one table; Shutdown is not a pane and has no key. `desk-app-close-to` hands
 whatever a step answered back to `desk-dispatch` with `clicked` false, so a
 verdict that is not a real scancode rides the same road, falls past every
 `sc ==` in the table, and is caught at the bottom. Above 255 no PS/2 set can
 produce it, so this cannot be typed by accident at a power-off.

  dk-sc-bye : Integer = 65536

 A GROUP HEADING ANSWERS WHICH GROUP, AND IT CANNOT LEAVE THE STEP. The id is
 a Text allocated inside this function's own bracket, so it cannot be handed
 back; the entry index can. `dk-sc-group` plus the group's first entry is that
 index in a form the two real answers cannot collide with, and the step turns
 it back into a selection rather than passing it on.

  dk-sc-group : Integer = 131072

 THE PILL TOGGLES, and the hit for it is already in this tree. The menu is
 drawn through `desk-chrome-face`, so the taskbar and its `menu` button are
 laid out with it and `ev-hit-widget` reaches the pill without a second hit
 path. Before this, clicking the pill while the menu was open fell through
 every arm to "no row was hit, stay open", so the menu could only be dismissed
 by choosing something: Damian, 2026-08-26, testing it -- it should close when
 clicked and open. Out of scancode range for the same reason the two above it
 are.

  dk-sc-toggle : Integer = 262144

 THE DESKTOP NO LONGER LAUNCHES AN APP FROM A KEYSTROKE. Damian, 2026-08-26:
 remove all the hotkeys for app launch. There is ONE place that did it, and it
 is `desk-loop`'s last arm rather than the table in `desk-dispatch`: a bare
 scancode from the desktop went straight into that table. **The table STAYS**,
 because it is also how a start-menu row opens its app -- `desk-app-close-to`
 hands a row's answer back to `desk-dispatch` directly, with `clicked` false,
 so it never passes through the arm that changed. Deleting the table would
 have taken the launcher with it, and the two look like the same thing from
 the table's side.

 F12 IS NOT AN APP LAUNCH and is the one keystroke the desktop still answers,
 so it is named here rather than left as a bare 88 in a condition.

  desk-sc-shot : Integer = 88

  dk-menu-hit : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, GopFont -> Integer
  dk-menu-hit (w) (h) (ps) (mx) (my) (ds) (tf) =
    let probe = __heap-save
    in let s = ui-wscale w
    in let laid = widget-layout (desk-chrome-face (desk-menu-tree ps tf s) w ds tf) (dk-theme-ds ds) (layout-rect 0 0 (w / s) (h / s))
    in let code = when ev-hit-widget laid (comp-hit-x mx s) (comp-hit-y my s)
          is Just (wgt) ->
            if wgt.wn-id == "shutdown" then dk-sc-bye
            else if wgt.wn-id == "menu" then dk-sc-toggle
            else
              let g = gpr-group-scan (wgt.wn-id) 0
              in if g /= 0 then dk-sc-group + g - 1 else gpr-id-scan (wgt.wn-id) 0
          is None -> 0
    in let dropped = __heap-restore probe
    in code

  desk-menu-step : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, Integer, Boolean -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-menu-step (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (sc) (clicked) =
    let ps = peek-32 ds desk-prog-cell
    in let hit = if clicked then dk-menu-hit w h ps (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) ds tf else 0
    in if hit == dk-sc-toggle then 0
    else if hit >= dk-sc-group then
      let g = gpr-set-sel ps (hit - dk-sc-group)
      in let mark = __heap-save
      in let p = desk-menu-repaint base w h stride font tf cs mouse ps ds
      in let d = __heap-restore mark
      in 1
    else if hit /= 0 then hit
    else if sc == 88 then act
      let mark = __heap-save
      in act
        s <- desk-shot base w h stride font ds
        let d = __heap-restore mark
        in 1
      end
    end
    else if sc == 0 then 1
    else
      let mark = __heap-save
      in let p = desk-menu-repaint base w h stride font tf cs mouse ps ds
      in let d = __heap-restore mark
      in 1

  desk-menu-open : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-menu-open (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (ps) (apps) (root) = act
    hd <- cursor-hide cs base stride
    let e = gpr-ensure ps
    in let p0 = desk-menu-draw base w h stride font tf ps ds
    in let sf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell desk-focus-menu
    in let c1 = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
    in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
  end

  desk-programs-open : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-programs-open (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (ps) (apps) (root) = act
    hd <- cursor-hide cs base stride
    let e = gpr-ensure ps
    in let o = desk-wnd-open desk-focus-prog w h ds
    in act
      p0 <- desk-programs-paint base w h stride font tf ps ds
      let sf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell (dk-wr-top (peek-32 ds dk-wr-cell))
      in let c1 = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
      in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
    end
  end

  desk-calc-open : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-calc-open (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (ks) (apps) (root) = act
    hd <- cursor-hide cs base stride
    let o = desk-wnd-open desk-focus-calc w h ds
    in act
      p0 <- desk-calc-paint base w h stride font tf ks ds
      let sf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell (dk-wr-top (peek-32 ds dk-wr-cell))
      in let c1 = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
      in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
    end
  end

Section: Calendar

 The calendar pane. The month is a still picture until the date changes,
 so there is no tick gate here: a calendar that repainted every second
 would be writing the same pixels 86,399 times a day to be right once.
 Being a window does not change that. It is repainted when a window event
 asks for it, which is the pointer's rate and not the clock's, and the tree
 that gets built then is `desk-wnd-tree`'s calendar arm.

 RTC registers 7, 8 and 9 are day of month, month and year, BCD like the
 time registers. The year register carries two digits, so the century is
 the caller's to supply and this assumes 2000. That reading is in
 `desk-wnd-tree` now, because the tree is what needs it.

  desk-cal-paint : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-cal-paint (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (ds) = act
    let bg = gop-fill-rect base stride 0 0 w h (dk-wall ds)
    in desk-wnd-paint-all base w h stride font tf ds
  end

 The month never repaints, so the cursor is the only thing that moves here
 and it needs no hide-repaint-update bracket: `cursor-update` restores what
 it covered before drawing itself at the new place.

  desk-cal-open : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-cal-open (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) = act
    hd <- cursor-hide cs base stride
    let o = desk-wnd-open desk-focus-cal w h ds
    in act
      p0 <- desk-cal-paint base w h stride font tf ds
      let sf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell (dk-wr-top (peek-32 ds dk-wr-cell))
      in let c1 = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
      in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
    end
  end

Section: Issues

 The table is laid out once to be MEASURED and then rebuilt to the height
 the layout engine gave it (WORKS-23). The alternative is deriving that
 height here from the title, the two separators and the help line, which is
 a second copy of an arithmetic the engine already did and would drift from
 it the first time a pane gains a row. The measuring pass is bracketed and
 dropped, so what it costs is one layout of a tree this pane rebuilds every
 paint anyway.

 WORKS-47 wants the table's width from the same pass, so what comes back is
 the measured node's rect rather than one of its numbers. A second bracketed
 layout to read the other number would double what this already costs.

 THE ROOM IT MEASURES IN IS THE WINDOW'S CONTENT BOX, not the screen's. The
 pane is a window, so the tree goes in through `comp-render-at` at the rect
 the block holds less the titlebar, and a table measured against the whole
 chrome would page for a height nothing draws at. The arithmetic is
 `dk-style-hit`'s and `dk-calc-hit`'s before it.

 The rect is built AFTER the restore, from two integers the tree gave up
 before it, for the reason `desk-rev-box` states: a record built inside the
 bracket is one the restore has already released, and it reads correctly
 until the next allocation lands on it. This function returned one for as
 long as it has existed.

  desk-trk-win-box : Integer, Integer, DbServer, Integer, Integer, Integer, GopFont -> LayoutRect
  desk-trk-win-box (w) (h) (srv) (filter) (sel) (ds) (tf) =
    let probe = __heap-save
    in let s = ui-wscale w
    in let blk = peek-32 ds dk-trk-cell
    in let st = dk-wnd-st blk
    in let bh = dk-wnd-bar-h tf s
    in let wx = dk-wnd-rx blk w st
    in let wy = dk-wnd-ry blk w h st ds + bh
    in let ww = dk-wnd-rw blk w st
    in let wh = dk-wnd-rh blk w h st ds - bh
    in let measured = widget-layout (gtk-tree srv filter sel (0 - 1) 0) (dk-theme-ds ds) (layout-rect (wx / s) (wy / s) (ww / s) (wh / s))
    in let bw = when widget-find measured "issues"
      is Just (n) -> (n.wn-bounds).lr-w
      is None -> 0
    in let bxh = when widget-find measured "issues"
      is Just (n) -> (n.wn-bounds).lr-h
      is None -> 0
    in let dropped = __heap-restore probe
    in layout-rect 0 0 bw bxh

 WORKS-13. The same bracket-and-read-an-integer shape the desk uses
 everywhere else: lay the tree out again inside a heap bracket, ask the
 table which row the hit id names, drop the tree and keep the number. The
 tree is built at the height the paint used, so a click answers about the
 page the reader is looking at rather than about a table nobody drew.

 IT LAYS THE TREE OUT WHERE THE PAINTER PUT IT, which is the thing a pane
 takes on joining the window world that fails silently. Laid at the screen
 corner under the full-width chrome, a click answers whichever ROW sits at the
 same offset from that corner, so the reader selects a different issue: the
 table repaints with a row highlighted and it looks exactly like a click that
 worked.

  desk-trk-hit : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, DbServer, Integer, Integer, GopFont -> Integer
  desk-trk-hit (w) (h) (mx) (my) (ds) (srv) (filter) (sel) (tf) =
    let box = desk-trk-win-box w h srv filter sel ds tf
    in let probe = __heap-save
    in let s = ui-wscale w
    in let blk = peek-32 ds dk-trk-cell
    in let st = dk-wnd-st blk
    in let bh = dk-wnd-bar-h tf s
    in let wx = dk-wnd-rx blk w st
    in let wy = dk-wnd-ry blk w h st ds + bh
    in let ww = dk-wnd-rw blk w st
    in let wh = dk-wnd-rh blk w h st ds - bh
    in let laid = widget-layout (gtk-tree srv filter sel (box.lr-h) (box.lr-w)) (dk-theme-ds ds) (layout-rect (wx / s) (wy / s) (ww / s) (wh / s))
    in let row = when ev-hit-widget laid (comp-hit-x mx s) (comp-hit-y my s)
      is Just (wgt) -> gtk-row-at srv filter sel (box.lr-h) (box.lr-w) (wgt.wn-id)
      is None -> 0 - 1
    in let dropped = __heap-restore probe
    in row

 The tree is no longer built here. The painter builds every open window's,
 including this one's, which is what `desk-wnd-tree`'s Issues arm is for, and
 a pane that drew its own would draw over the window beside it.

  desk-trk-paint : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-trk-paint (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (ds) = act
    let bg = gop-fill-rect base stride 0 0 w h (dk-wall ds)
    in desk-wnd-paint-all base w h stride font tf ds
  end

  desk-trk-rows : DbServer, Integer -> Integer
  desk-trk-rows (srv) (filter) =
    let r = tk-db-all-issues srv
    in list-length (gtk-rows-from-db (r.qr-rows) filter 0 (r.qr-count) [])

 The tracker database was a `let` above the loop, which a step has nowhere to
 put, and `DbServer` is a record so a `ds` cell cannot hold it. It is rebuilt
 instead, and only when there is a key to answer. That is CHEAPER than the loop
 it replaces, not dearer: the old loop ran `desk-trk-rows` on every iteration,
 which is a query and a list build per poll, to compute a clamp it usually did
 not use. The step does nothing at all until a key arrives, and a key arrives
 at human speed. `tk-init-tracker-db` is pure construction from literals, so
 rebuilding it is safe as well as affordable.

 Scancode 33 is `f`, which opens Files from the desk and cycles the filter
 here. A focused step gets the key and the dispatch does not see it, which is
 what makes a pane able to own a letter the desk also uses.

  desk-trk-step : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, Integer, Boolean -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-trk-step (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (sc) (clicked) = act
    ev <- desk-wnd-chrome-step base w h stride font tf mouse ds cs desk-focus-trk sc clicked
    let pass = clicked & ev == desk-wnd-ev-pass
    in if ev == desk-wnd-ev-close then act 0 end
    else if ev == desk-wnd-ev-min then act desk-step-hide end
    else if ev == desk-wnd-ev-stay then act 1 end
    else if sc == 88 then act
      let mark = __heap-save
      in act
        s <- desk-shot base w h stride font ds
        let d = __heap-restore mark
        in 1
      end
    end
    else if (sc /= 0) | pass then act
      let mark = __heap-save
      in let srv = tk-init-tracker-db
      in let filter = peek-32 ds 32
      in let sel = peek-32 ds 40
      in let n = desk-trk-rows srv filter
      in let hi = if n > 0 then n - 1 else 0
      in let nf = if sc == 33 then int-mod (filter + 1) 3 else filter
      in let ns0 = if sc == 72 then (if sel > 0 then sel - 1 else 0) else sel
      in let ns1 = if sc == 80 then (if ns0 < hi then ns0 + 1 else hi) else ns0
      in let ns2 = if sc == 33 then 0 else ns1
      in let hit-row = if pass then desk-trk-hit w h (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) ds srv filter sel tf else 0 - 1
      in let ns = if hit-row >= 0 then hit-row else ns2
      in if nf == filter & ns == sel then act
        let d = __heap-restore mark
        in 1
      end
      else act
        let f1 = poke-32 ds 32 nf
        in let s1 = poke-32 ds 40 ns
        in let hd = cursor-hide cs base stride
        in act
          p <- desk-trk-paint base w h stride font tf ds
          let c = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
          in let d = __heap-restore mark
          in 1
        end
      end
    end
    else act 1 end
  end

  desk-trk-open : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-trk-open (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) = act
    hd <- cursor-hide cs base stride
    let o = desk-wnd-open desk-focus-trk w h ds
    in act
      p0 <- desk-trk-paint base w h stride font tf ds
      let sf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell (dk-wr-top (peek-32 ds dk-wr-cell))
      in let c1 = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
      in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
    end
  end

Section: Review

 What a pane gets for itself, in the layout's own units: the glass height
 divided by the widget scale, less the top gap `desk-chrome-with` inserts and
 the taskbar band it puts underneath. A pane that sizes anything to the space
 it has needs this, and it cannot compute it from inside because these two
 constants live here and `GopDesk` cites the panes rather than the reverse.

  desk-content-h : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  desk-content-h (w) (h) (ds) =
    h / ui-wscale w - dk-bar-h * ui-scale w / ui-wscale w - dk-task-px ds w / ui-wscale w

 The width a wrapped line has to fit is measured rather than computed, the way
 the Issues table's height is (WORKS-23). Deriving it here means subtracting the
 sidebar, the panel padding and the label's own padding by hand, and the padding
 is a THEME's choice: `edges-uniform 4` in one scheme, `edges-xy 16 8` in
 another, so an arithmetic that agreed with the engine under the default would
 wrap at the wrong column under `lcars`. The measuring pass lays the detail out
 with wrapping off, reads the rect the description label was given, and drops
 the tree. Width does not depend on the wrap, because a column gives every child
 the container's width whatever its height, so one pass settles it and there is
 no second round.

 What comes back is a rect rather than one number because the detail needs
 both: the width a wrapped line has to fit, and the room left under it. Reading
 the second with a second bracketed layout would double what this costs.

 The rect is built AFTER the restore, from two integers the tree gave up before
 it. Building it inside the bracket and returning it hands back a record the
 restore has already released, which reads correctly only until the next
 allocation lands on it.

  desk-rev-box : Integer, Integer, GrvData, Integer, Maybe Text, Integer, GopFont -> LayoutRect
  desk-rev-box (w) (h) (data) (rs) (signer) (ds) (tf) =
    let probe = __heap-save
    in let s = ui-wscale w
    in let measured = widget-layout (desk-chrome-face (grv-tree data rs signer (desk-content-h w h ds) (0 - 1) 0) w ds tf) (dk-theme-ds ds) (layout-rect 0 0 (w / s) (h / s))
    in let bw = when widget-find measured "grv-body-0"
      is Just (n) -> (n.wn-bounds).lr-w
      is None -> 0 - 1
    in let sh = when widget-find measured "grv-fill"
      is Just (n) -> (n.wn-bounds).lr-h
      is None -> 0
    in let dropped = __heap-restore probe
    in layout-rect 0 0 bw sh

  desk-rev-draw : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, GrvData, Integer, Integer, Maybe Text -> Integer
  desk-rev-draw (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (data) (rs) (ds) (signer) =
    let box = if grv-in-detail rs then desk-rev-box w h data rs signer ds tf else layout-rect-zero
    in let view = if box.lr-h <= 0 then 1 else 1 + (box.lr-h) / grv-row-h
    in let tree = grv-tree data rs signer (desk-content-h w h ds) (box.lr-w) (if grv-in-detail rs then view else 0)
    in let laid = dk-chrome-paint base w h stride font tf tree ds
    in desk-topbar base w stride font (ui-scale w) tf h ds

  desk-rev-paint : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, GrvData, Integer, Integer, Maybe Text -> Integer
  desk-rev-paint (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (data) (rs) (ds) (signer) =
    let bg = gop-fill-rect base stride 0 0 w h (dk-wall ds)
    in desk-rev-draw base w h stride font tf data rs ds signer

  desk-rev-stamp : Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-rev-stamp (dummy) = act
    yy <- rtc-read 9
    mm <- rtc-read 8
    dd <- rtc-read 7
    hh <- rtc-read 4
    mi <- rtc-read 2
    ss <- rtc-read 0
    let day = ((2000 + dk-bcd yy) * 100 + dk-bcd mm) * 100 + dk-bcd dd
    in (day * 100 + dk-bcd hh) * 10000 + dk-bcd mi * 100 + dk-bcd ss
  end

  desk-rev-step : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port, Identity] Integer
  desk-rev-step (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (sc) =
    if sc == 0 then 1
    else if sc == 88 then act
      let mark = __heap-save
      in act
        s <- desk-shot base w h stride font ds
        let d = __heap-restore mark
        in 1
      end
    end
    else act
      let mark = __heap-save
      in let rs = peek-32 ds desk-review-cell
      in let md = grv-shift-of (peek-32 rs grv-mods-cell) sc
      in let sm = poke-32 rs grv-mods-cell md
      in act
       data <- grv-load 0
       signer0 <- loaded-fingerprint
       let can = when signer0
         is None -> 0
         is Just (fp) -> 1
       in let r0 = grv-key rs sc (grv-count data) md can
       in act
        cast <- if r0 == 6 then act
            ts <- desk-rev-stamp 0
            grv-supersede data rs ts
          end else if r0 >= 3 then act
            ts <- desk-rev-stamp 0
            grv-cast data rs r0 ts
          end else 0
        let r = if r0 >= 3 then 1 else r0
        in if r == 2 then
          let m = grv-set-mode rs grv-mode-list
          in let s = grv-set-sel rs 0 1
          in let d = __heap-restore mark
          in 0
        else if r == 0 then
          let d = __heap-restore mark
          in 1
        else act
          data2 <- grv-reload cast data
          let hd = cursor-hide cs base stride
          in let p = desk-rev-paint base w h stride font tf data2 rs ds signer0
          in let c = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
          in let d = __heap-restore mark
          in 1
        end
       end
      end
    end

  desk-rev-open : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-rev-open (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) = act
    hd <- cursor-hide cs base stride
    signer <- loaded-fingerprint
    let mark = __heap-save
    in let rs = peek-32 ds desk-review-cell
    in act
     data <- grv-load 0
     let s0 = grv-set-sel rs (grv-sel rs) (grv-count data)
     in let p0 = desk-rev-paint base w h stride font tf data rs ds signer
     in let d = __heap-restore mark
     in let sf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell desk-focus-rev
     in let c1 = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
     in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
    end
  end

Section: Diffusion

  desk-dif-paint : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-dif-paint (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (ds) = act
    let bg = gop-fill-rect base stride 0 0 w h (dk-wall ds)
    in desk-wnd-paint-all base w h stride font tf ds
  end

 The pointer tracks here and no click reaches the FORM, which is honest rather
 than lazy: the Generate button dispatches to nothing (WORKS-3), so a click
 that appeared to arm it would be the worse outcome. Wire the click when the
 button does something. `desk-wnd-quiet-step` is what this pane takes, and it
 answers the titlebar, Esc and F12 and nothing else -- so the window's own
 three buttons work and the tree underneath is still untouched by the pointer.

  desk-dif-open : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-dif-open (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) = act
    hd <- cursor-hide cs base stride
    let o = desk-wnd-open desk-focus-dif w h ds
    in act
      p0 <- desk-dif-paint base w h stride font tf ds
      let sf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell (dk-wr-top (peek-32 ds dk-wr-cell))
      in let c1 = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
      in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
    end
  end

Section: Appearance Pane

  dk-style-tree : Integer, Integer, Integer -> WidgetNode
  dk-style-tree (scheme) (f) (snd) =
    widget-panel "style" DirColumn 4 [widget-label "style-title" "APPEARANCE", widget-button "style-scheme" ("Colour scheme: " & dk-scheme-name scheme), widget-button "style-border" ("Borders: " & dk-on-off f dk-adorn-border), widget-button "style-grad" ("Gradients: " & dk-on-off f dk-adorn-gradient), widget-button "style-shadow" ("Shadows: " & dk-on-off f dk-adorn-shadow), widget-button "style-round" ("Rounded corners: " & dk-on-off f dk-adorn-round), widget-button "style-sound" ("Sounds: " & dk-sound-name snd), widget-label "style-hint" "Click a row, or space b g s r a.", widget-set-flex (widget-label "style-spacer" "") 1]

  dk-sound-name : Integer -> Text
  dk-sound-name (v) = if v == 0 then "off" else "on"

  dk-style-id-code : Text -> Integer
  dk-style-id-code (id) =
    if id == "style-scheme" then 1
    else if id == "style-border" then 2
    else if id == "style-grad" then 3
    else if id == "style-shadow" then 4
    else if id == "style-round" then 5
    else if id == "style-sound" then 6
    else 0

  dk-style-key : Integer -> Integer
  dk-style-key (sc) =
    if sc == 57 then 1
    else if sc == 48 then 2
    else if sc == 34 then 3
    else if sc == 31 then 4
    else if sc == 19 then 5
    else if sc == 30 then 6
    else 0

 IT LAYS THE TREE OUT WHERE THE PAINTER PUT IT, which is the fifth thing a pane
 takes on joining the window world and the one that fails silently. Laid at the
 screen corner over the whole chrome, a click answers whichever ROW sits at the
 same offset from the corner, so the wrong toggle flips: this pane's rows are
 the colour scheme, four adornment bits and the sound, and every one of them is
 a plausible thing to have meant. `dk-calc-hit` is the worked example and this
 repeats its rect arithmetic.

  dk-style-hit : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, GopFont -> Integer
  dk-style-hit (w) (h) (mx) (my) (ds) (tf) =
    let probe = __heap-save
    in let tree = dk-style-tree (peek-32 ds dk-scheme-cell) (peek-32 ds dk-adorn-cell) (peek-32 ds dk-sound-on-cell)
    in let s = ui-wscale w
    in let blk = peek-32 ds dk-sty-cell
    in let st = dk-wnd-st blk
    in let bh = dk-wnd-bar-h tf s
    in let wx = dk-wnd-rx blk w st
    in let wy = dk-wnd-ry blk w h st ds + bh
    in let ww = dk-wnd-rw blk w st
    in let wh = dk-wnd-rh blk w h st ds - bh
    in let laid = widget-layout tree (dk-theme-ds ds) (layout-rect (wx / s) (wy / s) (ww / s) (wh / s))
    in let code = when ev-hit-widget laid (comp-hit-x mx s) (comp-hit-y my s)
          is Just (wgt) -> dk-style-id-code (wgt.wn-id)
          is None -> 0
    in let dropped = __heap-restore probe
    in code

  dk-style-flip : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-style-flip (ds) (bit) = poke-32 ds dk-adorn-cell (dk-adorn-flip (peek-32 ds dk-adorn-cell) bit)

  dk-style-apply : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-style-apply (ds) (code) =
    if code == 1 then poke-32 ds dk-scheme-cell (int-mod (peek-32 ds dk-scheme-cell + 1) dk-scheme-count)
    else if code == 2 then dk-style-flip ds dk-adorn-border
    else if code == 3 then dk-style-flip ds dk-adorn-gradient
    else if code == 4 then dk-style-flip ds dk-adorn-shadow
    else if code == 5 then dk-style-flip ds dk-adorn-round
    else if code == 6 then poke-32 ds dk-sound-on-cell (1 - peek-32 ds dk-sound-on-cell)
    else 0

  desk-style-paint : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-style-paint (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (ds) = act
    let bg = gop-fill-rect base stride 0 0 w h (dk-wall ds)
    in desk-wnd-paint-all base w h stride font tf ds
  end

 The first step that needs the POINTER as well as the key, which is why the
 protocol carries the whole event: `desk-step-of` takes `sc` and `clicked`, and
 a pane that only reads keys ignores the second. Appearance is driven from
 either, and `dk-style-key` and `dk-style-hit` already fold both into one code,
 which is the same "one road" the launcher rule asks for.

 All of its state is `dk-scheme-cell` and `dk-adorn-cell`, both integers, so
 nothing here has to survive in a pointer and the step keeps no state of its
 own at all.

  desk-style-step : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, Integer, Boolean -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-style-step (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (sc) (clicked) = act
    ev <- desk-wnd-chrome-step base w h stride font tf mouse ds cs desk-focus-style sc clicked
    let click = if clicked & ev == desk-wnd-ev-pass then dk-style-hit w h (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) ds tf else 0
    in let code = if click /= 0 then click else dk-style-key sc
    in if ev == desk-wnd-ev-close then 0
    else if ev == desk-wnd-ev-min then desk-step-hide
    else if ev == desk-wnd-ev-stay then 1
    else if sc == 88 then act
      let mark = __heap-save
      in act
        s <- desk-shot base w h stride font ds
        let d = __heap-restore mark
        in 1
      end
    end
    else if code == 0 then 1
    else act
      let mark = __heap-save
      in let a = dk-style-apply ds code
      in act
        sv <- dk-settings-save ds
        let hd = cursor-hide cs base stride
        in act
          p <- desk-style-paint base w h stride font tf ds
          let c = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
          in let d = __heap-restore mark
          in 1
        end
      end
    end
  end

  desk-style-open : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-style-open (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) = act
    hd <- cursor-hide cs base stride
    let o = desk-wnd-open desk-focus-style w h ds
    in act
      p0 <- desk-style-paint base w h stride font tf ds
      let sf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell (dk-wr-top (peek-32 ds dk-wr-cell))
      in let c1 = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
      in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
    end
  end

Section: Loop

  desk-widget-hit : WidgetNode, Integer, Integer, Text, Integer -> Boolean
  desk-widget-hit (root) (mx) (my) (id) (s) =
    when ev-hit-widget root (comp-hit-x mx s) (comp-hit-y my s)
      is Just (wgt) -> wgt.wn-id == id
      is None -> False

 Every pane hid the cursor on entry and left it hidden, so the arrow died
 the moment a pane opened and came back only on the way out. These two put
 it back on the desktop's own terms: `dk-cursor` is the paint, and `dk-pump`
 is the poll that answers whether the pointer moved.

 The panes do not need the browser's memoized tree. A pane's state lives in
 `ds` cells written with `poke-32` rather than on the heap, so a hit test can
 lay the tree out again inside a bracket, read an INTEGER out of it, and drop
 the whole thing -- there is no state allocated above the tree that a release
 would take with it. `widget-layout` on the same tree the paint hands
 `comp-render` gives the same bounds, because comp-render lays out exactly
 that and nothing else.

  dk-cursor : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, UsbMouse -> Integer
  dk-cursor (base) (w) (h) (stride) (cs) (mouse) =
    if mouse.um-ok
    then cursor-update cs base stride w h (ui-scale w) (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse)
    else 0

  dk-pump : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, UsbMouse -> Integer
  dk-pump (base) (w) (h) (stride) (cs) (mouse) =
    if mouse-pump mouse w h == 1
    then let c = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse in 1
    else 0

Section: The Editor

 Its 9 MB buffer is allocated lazily by `ged-ensure` and parked in
 `desk-edit-cell`, and handed to the step as `es`. Only its typed values ride
 in `EditState`.

 **This block said the base mark cannot reach that buffer. It can, and this is
 the correction.** `ged-init` runs from `desk-edit-open`, which is ABOVE the
 base mark, so a close restores over it. `ged-ensure` (`GopEdit.codex:49`) only
 allocates when the pointer is zero, so the next open would reuse an address
 the allocator has already handed out again. Measured 2026-08-19: an Edit pane
 alive and hidden holds 9,598,200 bytes, and after it closes the frontier is
 72 bytes over a desk that never opened one, so the 9,437,184 that `ged-init`
 allocates is demonstrably reclaimed. Both close paths therefore zero the
 pointer whenever the restore target is at or below it, which costs one
 re-allocation per Edit session and removes the dangling reuse.

 It was two loops, a file list with the editor nested inside it, and a step
 cannot nest. They are one step over a mode now, and leaving the editor is a
 mode change rather than a stack unwind.

  desk-edit-step : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, Integer, Boolean -> [Identity, Device.Port] Integer
  desk-edit-step (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (sc) (clicked) =
    when apps.da-edit
      is None -> act 0 end
      is Just (st) -> act
        ev <- desk-wnd-chrome-step base w h stride font tf mouse ds cs desk-focus-edit sc clicked
        if ev == desk-wnd-ev-close then act 0 end
        else if ev == desk-wnd-ev-min then act desk-step-hide end
        else if ev == desk-wnd-ev-stay then act
          let p = ged-reenter base (dk-wnd-content desk-focus-edit w h ds tf (ui-wscale w)) w h stride font (dk-pal ds) mouse cs (peek-32 ds desk-edit-cell) st
          in 1
        end
        else act
          r <- ged-step base (dk-wnd-content desk-focus-edit w h ds tf (ui-wscale w)) w h stride font (dk-pal ds) mouse cs (peek-32 ds desk-edit-cell) st sc clicked
          if r == ged-step-hide then desk-step-hide else r
        end
      end

  desk-edit-focus : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-edit-focus (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) =
    when apps.da-edit
      is Just (st) -> desk-edit-reenter base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root st
      is None -> desk-edit-open base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root

  desk-edit-reenter : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode, EditState -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-edit-reenter (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) (st) =
    let hd = cursor-hide cs base stride
    in let root2 = desk-draw base w h stride font (ui-scale w) tf ds
    in let o = desk-wnd-open desk-focus-edit w h ds
    in let mk = __heap-save
    in act
      w0 <- desk-wall-paint base w h stride font tf ds
      let p = ged-reenter base (dk-wnd-content desk-focus-edit w h ds tf (ui-wscale w)) w h stride font (dk-pal ds) mouse cs (peek-32 ds desk-edit-cell) st
      in let dr = __heap-restore mk
      in let sf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell (dk-wr-top (peek-32 ds dk-wr-cell))
      in let inval = poke-32 ds desk-second-cell (0 - 1)
      in let c1 = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
      in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root2
    end

  desk-edit-open : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-edit-open (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) =
    let ke = desk-browser-evict ds
    in let mp = desk-marks-push (peek-32 ds desk-marks-cell) desk-focus-edit __heap-save
    in let o = desk-wnd-open desk-focus-edit w h ds
    in act
    w0 <- desk-wall-paint base w h stride font tf ds
    st <- ged-new base (dk-wnd-content desk-focus-edit w h ds tf (ui-wscale w)) w h stride (ui-scale w) font (dk-pal ds) mouse cs (peek-32 ds desk-edit-cell)
    let apps2 = DeskApps { da-files = apps.da-files, da-browser = None, da-scene = apps.da-scene, da-edit = Just st }
    in let sf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell (dk-wr-top (peek-32 ds dk-wr-cell))
    in let inval = poke-32 ds desk-second-cell (0 - 1)
    in let c1 = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
    in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps2 root
  end

Section: Files

 Files is the first pane whose state the desk carries in the typed channel
 rather than in a `ds` cell. A mounted volume, a directory listing, the
 cluster stack and the names down to it are records and lists, and a cell
 holds 32 bits. `GopFiles` still owns every measure and every pixel; what
 moves here is only the loop.

 The state is allocated by `desk-files-open`, which runs after `desk-run`
 took the base mark and before `desk-loop` takes its frame mark. That places
 it above the one and below the other, so the per-iteration restore never
 reaches it and the close restore always does. Moving the selection stores
 an INTEGER into the record in place, which is safe across a restore however
 the record's other fields were allocated. Changing directory stores new
 LISTS, which is not, and that is what the negative answer is for.

  desk-files-focus : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-files-focus (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) =
    when apps.da-files
      is Just (st) -> desk-files-reenter base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root st
      is None -> desk-files-open base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root

  desk-files-reenter : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode, FilesState -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-files-reenter (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) (st) =
    let s = ui-scale w
    in let o = desk-wnd-open desk-focus-files w h ds
    in let box = dk-wnd-content desk-focus-files w h ds tf (ui-wscale w)
    in let mk = __heap-save
    in act
      hd <- cursor-hide cs base stride
      w0 <- desk-wall-paint base w h stride font tf ds
      let d = gfl-draw-list base box stride s font (dk-pal ds) (st.fs-entries) (st.fs-sel) (st.fs-top) (st.fs-names)
      in let dr = __heap-restore mk
      in let sf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell (dk-wr-top (peek-32 ds dk-wr-cell))
      in let c1 = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
      in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
    end

  desk-files-open : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-files-open (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) =
    let ke = desk-browser-evict ds
    in let mp = desk-marks-push (peek-32 ds desk-marks-cell) desk-focus-files __heap-save
    in act
    m <- gfat-mount-esp
    hd <- cursor-hide cs base stride
    let s = ui-scale w
    in let o = desk-wnd-open desk-focus-files w h ds
    in let box = dk-wnd-content desk-focus-files w h ds tf (ui-wscale w)
    in act
    w0 <- desk-wall-paint base w h stride font tf ds
    if m.gm-ok == False then act
      f <- gfl-draw-frame base box stride s font (dk-pal ds) ""
      t <- gop-draw-text base stride font (box.lr-x + 10 * s) (box.lr-y + 26 * s) (shot-diag-text "no FAT ESP on the boot medium") (gfl-dim (dk-pal ds)) s 0
      t2 <- gop-draw-text base stride font (box.lr-x + 10 * s) (box.lr-y + box.lr-h - 18 * s) "Esc back" (gfl-hint (dk-pal ds)) s 0
      let st = FilesState { fs-vol = m.gm-vol, fs-entries = [], fs-clusters = [0 - 1], fs-names = [], fs-sel = 0, fs-top = 0, fs-ptr = 0, fs-mode = 0 }
      in let sf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell (dk-wr-top (peek-32 ds dk-wr-cell))
      in let c1 = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
      in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs (DeskApps { da-files = Just st, da-browser = None, da-scene = apps.da-scene, da-edit = apps.da-edit }) root
    end
    else act
      let flag = if gfat-is32 (m.gm-vol) then (m.gm-vol).vol-root-start else 0 - 1
      in let clusters = [flag]
      in act
        ents <- gfl-list-cur (m.gm-vol) clusters
        let st = FilesState { fs-vol = m.gm-vol, fs-entries = ents, fs-clusters = clusters, fs-names = [], fs-sel = 0, fs-top = 0, fs-ptr = 0, fs-mode = 0 }
        in let d = gfl-draw-list base box stride s font (dk-pal ds) ents 0 0 (st.fs-names)
        in let sf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell (dk-wr-top (peek-32 ds dk-wr-cell))
        in let c1 = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
        in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs (DeskApps { da-files = Just st, da-browser = None, da-scene = apps.da-scene, da-edit = apps.da-edit }) root
      end
    end
    end
  end

  desk-files-step : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, Integer, Boolean -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-files-step (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (sc) (clicked) =
    when apps.da-files
      is None -> act 0 end
      is Just (st) -> act
        ev <- desk-wnd-chrome-step base w h stride font tf mouse ds cs desk-focus-files sc clicked
        if ev == desk-wnd-ev-close then act 0 end
        else if ev == desk-wnd-ev-min then act desk-step-hide end
        else if ev == desk-wnd-ev-stay then act
          let hd = cursor-hide cs base stride
          in let box = dk-wnd-content desk-focus-files w h ds tf (ui-wscale w)
          in let p = if st.fs-mode == 1 then 0 else gfl-draw-list base box stride (ui-scale w) font (dk-pal ds) (st.fs-entries) (st.fs-sel) (st.fs-top) (st.fs-names)
          in let c = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
          in 1
        end
        else desk-files-on base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs st sc clicked
      end

 The pointer position the pane last acted on is a field rather than a `ds`
 cell because it is the pane's, and the record is where the pane's state now
 lives. It is what stands in for the `mv` flag the old loop got from
 `mouse-pump`: without it, a pointer resting over a row would drag the
 selection back on every poll and the arrow keys would not work.

  desk-files-moved : FilesState, UsbMouse -> Boolean
  desk-files-moved (st) (mouse) = st.fs-ptr /= (mouse-x mouse) * 4096 + (mouse-y mouse)

  desk-files-on : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, FilesState, Integer, Boolean -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-files-on (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (st) (sc) (clicked) =
    if st.fs-mode == 1 then desk-files-viewing base w h stride font tf mouse ds cs st sc clicked
    else desk-files-listing base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs st sc clicked

 A previewed file is a still picture, so this is the shared quiet step's shape
 rather than a mode with machinery: answer Esc and the close box by repainting
 the list, answer F12, and answer everything else by doing nothing. The preview
 keeps no state at all beyond the mode integer, because nothing repaints it --
 the only band the desk redraws on the second is the taskbar, and the Files
 panel ends above it.

  desk-files-viewing : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, FilesState, Integer, Boolean -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-files-viewing (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (st) (sc) (clicked) =
    let s = ui-scale w
    in let box = dk-wnd-content desk-focus-files w h ds tf (ui-wscale w)
    in if sc == 88 then act
      let mark = __heap-save
      in act
        r <- desk-shot base w h stride font ds
        let d = __heap-restore mark
        in 1
      end
    end
    else if sc == 1 | sc == 28 then
      let a = __record-set st "fs-mode" 0
      in let b = __record-set st "fs-ptr" ((mouse-x mouse) * 4096 + (mouse-y mouse))
      in let hd = cursor-hide cs base stride
      in let p = gfl-draw-list base box stride s font (dk-pal ds) (st.fs-entries) (st.fs-sel) (st.fs-top) (st.fs-names)
      in let c = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
      in 1
    else 1

  desk-files-listing : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, FilesState, Integer, Boolean -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-files-listing (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (st) (sc) (clicked) =
    let s = ui-scale w
    in let box = dk-wnd-content desk-focus-files w h ds tf (ui-wscale w)
    in let n = list-length (st.fs-entries)
    in let sel = st.fs-sel
    in let mv = desk-files-moved st mouse
    in let hover = if mv | clicked then gfl-row-at box s (st.fs-top) n (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) else 0 - 1
    in let pk = __record-set st "fs-ptr" ((mouse-x mouse) * 4096 + (mouse-y mouse))
    in if sc == 88 then act
      let mark = __heap-save
      in act
        r <- desk-shot base w h stride font ds
        let d = __heap-restore mark
        in 1
      end
    end
    else if sc == 15 then desk-step-hide
    else if sc == 1 then
      if list-length (st.fs-clusters) <= 1 then 1
      else desk-files-up base w h stride font tf mouse cs st ds
    else if (sc == 28 & n > 0) | (clicked & hover >= 0) then
      desk-files-pick base w h stride font tf kbd mouse cs st (if clicked & hover >= 0 then hover else sel) ds
    else if sc == 72 & sel > 0 then desk-files-move base w h stride font tf mouse cs st (sel - 1) ds
    else if sc == 80 & sel < n - 1 then desk-files-move base w h stride font tf mouse cs st (sel + 1) ds
    else if hover >= 0 & hover /= sel then desk-files-move base w h stride font tf mouse cs st hover ds
    else 1

  desk-files-move : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbMouse, Integer, FilesState, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-files-move (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (mouse) (cs) (st) (ns) (ds) =
    let s = ui-scale w
    in let box = dk-wnd-content desk-focus-files w h ds tf (ui-wscale w)
    in let t2 = gfl-clamp-top ns (st.fs-top) (gfl-rows-vis box s)
    in let a = __record-set st "fs-sel" ns
    in let b = __record-set st "fs-top" t2
    in let hd = cursor-hide cs base stride
    in let p = gfl-draw-list base box stride s font (dk-pal ds) (st.fs-entries) ns t2 (st.fs-names)
    in let c = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
    in 1

 Opening a file paints the preview and takes a mode. It used to call
 `GopFiles`' modal wait loop, which stopped the desk clock for as long as the
 file was on screen -- the last place in the desk where that was still true
 (WORKS-39). The preview keeps nothing: its sector buffer and its text are
 allocated inside the frame and answering 1 frees them, because no later
 iteration reads them again.

  desk-files-pick : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, FilesState, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-files-pick (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (cs) (st) (idx) (ds) =
    let e = list-at (st.fs-entries) idx
    in if gfl-is-dir e then desk-files-enter base w h stride font tf mouse cs st e ds
    else act
      p <- gfl-preview-paint base (dk-wnd-content desk-focus-files w h ds tf (ui-wscale w)) stride (ui-scale w) font (dk-pal ds) cs (st.fs-vol) e
      let a = __record-set st "fs-sel" idx
      in let b = __record-set st "fs-top" (gfl-clamp-top idx (st.fs-top) (gfl-rows-vis (dk-wnd-content desk-focus-files w h ds tf (ui-wscale w)) (ui-scale w)))
      in let m = __record-set st "fs-mode" 1
      in let c = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
      in 1
    end

  desk-files-enter : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbMouse, Integer, FilesState, Fat16DirEntry, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-files-enter (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (mouse) (cs) (st) (e) (ds) = act
    let c2 = list-push (st.fs-clusters) (e.de-cluster)
    in let n2 = list-push (st.fs-names) (e.de-name)
    in act
      ents <- gfl-list-cur (st.fs-vol) c2
      desk-files-land base w h stride font tf mouse cs st ents c2 n2 ds
    end
  end

  desk-files-up : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbMouse, Integer, FilesState, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-files-up (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (mouse) (cs) (st) (ds) = act
    let c2 = gfl-pop-int (st.fs-clusters)
    in let n2 = gfl-pop-text (st.fs-names)
    in act
      ents <- gfl-list-cur (st.fs-vol) c2
      desk-files-land base w h stride font tf mouse cs st ents c2 n2 ds
    end
  end

 The three list stores happen together and the answer is negative, so the
 frame that holds them is kept. Answering 1 here would free the listing the
 record now points at and the next repaint would walk freed memory.

  desk-files-land : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbMouse, Integer, FilesState, List Fat16DirEntry, List Integer, List Text, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-files-land (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (mouse) (cs) (st) (ents) (c2) (n2) (ds) =
    let a = __record-set st "fs-clusters" c2
    in let b = __record-set st "fs-names" n2
    in let c = __record-set st "fs-entries" ents
    in let d = __record-set st "fs-sel" 0
    in let f = __record-set st "fs-top" 0
    in let hd = cursor-hide cs base stride
    in let p = gfl-draw-list base (dk-wnd-content desk-focus-files w h ds tf (ui-wscale w)) stride (ui-scale w) font (dk-pal ds) ents 0 0 n2
    in let cu = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
    in 0 - 1

 The close bracket is not decoration and it now matters more, not less.
 `gsc-new` allocates the render target and its depth buffer outside any extent
 of its own, measured at 4,617,256 bytes for one visit at 1024x768 and the same
 again for the next, and the boot image gives Codex a 128 MB heap
 (`build-option-a.ps1 -AllocPages 32768`, which is `cdx-to-pe`'s
 `-HeapPages`) rather than the ~3 GB a codex-vm bed hands out. Nothing inside
 the pane releases it; `desk-app-close` restoring to the base mark is what does,
 on every exit, and without that every trip into the pane spends the arena the
 shot path's sector buffers are drawn from, permanently, and no bed at the
 default memory size can express the cost.

Section: The 3D panes

 One step and one record serve both, because `GopScene` and `GopFish` always
 shared the loop. What separates them here is the two focus ids and the scene
 builder each passes, which is where the difference already lived.

 This pane is the one whose per-iteration cost is a FRAME rather than a poll,
 so the desk's loop turns over at the frame rate: about 16 ms in the bed on the
 host rasterizer, about 86 to 135 ms on the software path, and near a second on
 metal. The clock still advances, just no finer than a frame. Nothing here
 keeps a frame -- the toggles and the counter are integers stored into the
 record in place, and everything a frame builds is freed by `desk-loop`'s own
 bracket, which is the job `gsc-loop`'s mark used to do.

 THE RECT IS READ BEFORE EVERY FRAME, not kept. A window can be moved,
 maximised or restored between two frames of an animating pane, and the
 cheapest correct answer is to place the pane again each time rather than to
 notice that it changed: `gsc-place` writes four fields in place and
 allocates nothing, because `gsc-new` sized the buffers to the whole content
 box once.

 CLOSING FROM THE TITLEBAR MUST RELEASE THE VIEWPORT, which `gsc-step` does
 for Esc and Tab and cannot do for a button it never sees. A held GPU
 viewport outlives the pane and the next pane to ask for one gets a refusal.

  desk-scene-step : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, (Integer -> Scene3DState), Integer, Integer, Boolean -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-scene-step (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (build) (foc) (sc) (clicked) =
    when apps.da-scene
      is None -> act 0 end
      is Just (sp) -> act
        ev <- desk-wnd-chrome-step base w h stride font tf mouse ds cs foc sc clicked
        if ev == desk-wnd-ev-close then act
          v <- (gs-viewport-release)
          0
        end
        else if ev == desk-wnd-ev-min then act
          v <- (gs-viewport-release)
          desk-step-hide
        end
        else if ev == desk-wnd-ev-stay then act 1 end
        else act
          let blk = desk-wnd-blk foc ds
          in let s = ui-wscale w
          in let bh = dk-wnd-bar-h tf s
          in let st = dk-wnd-st blk
          in let pl = gsc-place sp base stride (dk-wnd-rx blk w st + s) (dk-wnd-ry blk w h st ds + bh) (dk-wnd-rw blk w st - 2 * s) (dk-wnd-rh blk w h st ds - bh - s)
          in act
            r <- gsc-step base w h stride font kbd mouse cs build sp sc
            if r == gsc-step-hide then desk-step-hide else r
          end
        end
      end

  desk-scene-sibling : Integer -> Integer
  desk-scene-sibling (foc) = if foc == desk-focus-scene then desk-focus-fish else desk-focus-scene

  desk-scene-focus : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-scene-focus (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) (sky) (foc) =
    let ms = peek-32 ds desk-marks-cell
    in let live = desk-marks-find ms foc (desk-marks-depth ms - 1) >= 0
    in if live then desk-scene-reenter base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root foc
    else desk-scene-open base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root sky foc

  desk-scene-reenter : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode, Integer -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-scene-reenter (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) (foc) = act
    let hd = cursor-hide cs base stride
    in let root2 = desk-draw base w h stride font (ui-scale w) tf ds
    in let o = desk-wnd-open foc w h ds
    in act
      p0 <- desk-wall-paint base w h stride font tf ds
      let sf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell (dk-wr-top (peek-32 ds dk-wr-cell))
      in let inval = poke-32 ds desk-second-cell (0 - 1)
      in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root2
    end
  end

  desk-scene-open : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-scene-open (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) (sky) (foc) =
    let ms = peek-32 ds desk-marks-cell
    in let ko = desk-marks-kill ms (desk-scene-sibling foc)
    in let ke = desk-browser-evict ds
    in let mp = desk-marks-push ms foc __heap-save
    in act
    sp <- gsc-new base stride cs (dk-gpu ds) sky (dk-cbox-w w) (dk-cbox-h w h ds)
    let apps2 = DeskApps { da-files = apps.da-files, da-browser = None, da-scene = Just sp, da-edit = apps.da-edit }
    in let o = desk-wnd-open foc w h ds
    in act
      p0 <- desk-wall-paint base w h stride font tf ds
      let sf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell (dk-wr-top (peek-32 ds dk-wr-cell))
      in let inval = poke-32 ds desk-second-cell (0 - 1)
      in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps2 root
    end
  end

 The window's chrome is painted ONCE, at open and on re-entry, and not per
 frame. Every frame after that writes only inside the content box, so the
 titlebar and the border it drew stay where they are. A pane that repainted
 the whole desk at the frame rate would spend a frame's budget on pixels that
 did not move.

 IT IS BRACKETED, and that matters only for the panes that call it. A heavy
 pane pushes its own heap mark on the way in and restores to it on the way
 out, so anything allocated between the two is retained for as long as the
 pane is open -- and this call allocates a chrome tree and one laid tree per
 open window. The pixels are already written when it returns and nothing
 holds the trees, so dropping them is exactly what `desk-loop`'s own bracket
 does on every other iteration.

  desk-wall-paint : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-wall-paint (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (ds) = act
    let mark = __heap-save
    in act
      bg <- act gop-fill-rect base stride 0 0 w h (dk-wall ds) end
      p <- desk-wnd-paint-all base w h stride font tf ds
      let d = __heap-restore mark
      in 0
    end
  end


 F12 writes the live frame to the boot stick as SHhhmmss.BMP and paints
 the verdict into the taskbar -- the telemetry channel that retires the
 camera rig. The whole capture is heap-bracketed: the 2-and-a-bit
 megabyte frame copy and the FAT image are reclaimed before the loop
 continues, after the verdict Text has been painted.

  desk-shot : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-shot (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (ds) = act
    let mark = __heap-save
    in act
      t <- shot-take base w h stride
      let pal = dk-pal ds
      in let s = ui-scale w
      in let bg = gop-fill-rect base stride (200 * s) (h - dk-task-px ds w + 4 * s) (300 * s) (16 * s) (pal.pal-muted)
      in let tx = gop-draw-text base stride font (204 * s) (h - dk-task-px ds w + 6 * s) t (pal.pal-primary) s 0
      in let d = __heap-restore mark
      in 0
    end
  end

  desk-snap : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-snap (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) = act
    r <- desk-shot base w h stride font ds
    desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
  end

  desk-shutdown : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-shutdown (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) = act
    r <- acpi-poweroff (acpi-boot 0)
    let s = dk-win-s w h tf
    in let m = desk-line base stride h font s tf (dk-win-x w s + 10 * s) (dk-win-y h s tf + (dk-win-h tf s - 24) * s) "firmware declined the power transition" ((dk-pal ds).pal-error)
    in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
  end

Section: Browser

 The Browser is the one pane that covers the desk instead of floating over it,
 and it stays that way here. Its click handling tests absolute Y against
 `chrome-tab-bar-height` and `chrome-height`, so its tree has to start at the
 top of the framebuffer; composing it into the content slot beside the sidebar
 would move every hit by the sidebar's width and the top bar's height, which is
 a change to `Browser.codex`'s coordinate model and not to this pane.

 What it does give back is the taskbar strip, and the height is MEASURED from
 the laid-out band rather than computed from `dk-task-h`. The arithmetic
 assumed the band sits flush with the bottom of the screen and it does not:
 the root panel's own padding leaves 8 logical rows below it, which
 `desk-taskbar-hit.expected` has recorded since it was written. At 1600x900
 that arithmetic answered 844 where the band begins at 828, so the pane was
 granted sixteen rows of the band and painted a page into them on every
 repaint. Measured 2026-08-20 against the goldens: rows 828 to 843 carried
 page content after a dispatched key and the band's gradient before it.

 A tree with no taskbar falls back to the old arithmetic, which is what the
 standalone browser and any bed that renders this chrome without a band get.



 **Why a heavy open EVICTS a live Browser, when it does not evict Files or the
 3D panes.** `gbr-repaint` takes `bs-laid-mark` at the CURRENT frontier and
 rebuilds `bp-st` there, so a Browser re-entered above another pane has its
 state above THAT pane's mark. When the pane below closes and the cascade pops
 to its mark, the Browser's state goes with it and the record still points at
 it. Files and the 3D panes cannot do this: their state is built once by their
 own `-open`, above their own mark and below everything later. So the rule is
 that the Browser may only ever be the topmost heavy pane, and every other
 heavy `-open` kills its entry and clears its field. A LIGHT pane opening over
 it is safe and is the case this buys: a light close finds the Browser's entry
 live on top, reclaims nothing, and the Browser's marks are untouched.

  desk-browser-evict : Integer -> Integer
  desk-browser-evict (ds) = desk-marks-kill (peek-32 ds desk-marks-cell) desk-focus-bro

  desk-browser-focus : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-browser-focus (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) =
    when apps.da-browser
      is Just (bp) -> desk-browser-reenter base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root bp
      is None -> desk-browser-open base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root

  desk-browser-reenter : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode, BrowserPane -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-browser-reenter (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) (bp) = act
    hd <- cursor-hide cs base stride
    let o = desk-wnd-open desk-focus-bro w h ds
    in act
      c0 <- desk-wall-paint base w h stride font tf ds
      let box = dk-wnd-content desk-focus-bro w h ds tf (ui-wscale w)
      in act
        p <- gbr-first-paint base (box.lr-x) (box.lr-y) (box.lr-w) (box.lr-h) stride font tf mouse cs (ui-scale w) bp
        let sf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell (dk-wr-top (peek-32 ds dk-wr-cell))
        in let inval = poke-32 ds desk-second-cell (0 - 1)
        in let c1 = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
        in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
      end
    end
  end

  desk-browser-open : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-browser-open (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) =
    let mp = desk-marks-push (peek-32 ds desk-marks-cell) desk-focus-bro __heap-save
    in act
    hd <- cursor-hide cs base stride
    let o = desk-wnd-open desk-focus-bro w h ds
    in let box0 = dk-wnd-content desk-focus-bro w h ds tf (ui-wscale w)
    in act
      bp <- gbr-new (box0.lr-w) (box0.lr-h)
      let apps2 = DeskApps { da-files = apps.da-files, da-browser = Just bp, da-scene = apps.da-scene, da-edit = apps.da-edit }
      in act
        c0 <- desk-wall-paint base w h stride font tf ds
        p <- gbr-first-paint base (box0.lr-x) (box0.lr-y) (box0.lr-w) (box0.lr-h) stride font tf mouse cs (ui-scale w) bp
        let sf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell (dk-wr-top (peek-32 ds dk-wr-cell))
        in let inval = poke-32 ds desk-second-cell (0 - 1)
        in let c1 = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
        in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps2 root
      end
    end
  end

 A CHROME EVENT THAT REPAINTS BLANKS THIS PANE, and that is the one thing a
 heavy windowed pane needs that a light one does not. `desk-wnd-chrome-step`
 answers `stay` after raising, cycling or maximising a window, and the repaint
 it did on the way draws this window's EMPTY body panel: the 3D panes get
 their picture back on the next frame and this one has no next frame until
 somebody presses a key. So `stay` is where the Browser paints itself again,
 at whatever rect the event left it, and it answers NEGATIVE for the reason
 `gbr-step` always does -- the repaint restores below the desk's frame mark.

  desk-browser-step : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode, Integer, Boolean -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-browser-step (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) (sc) (clicked) =
    when apps.da-browser
      is None -> act 0 end
      is Just (bp) -> act
        ev <- desk-wnd-chrome-step base w h stride font tf mouse ds cs desk-focus-bro sc clicked
        let box = dk-wnd-content desk-focus-bro w h ds tf (ui-wscale w)
        in if ev == desk-wnd-ev-close then act 0 end
        else if ev == desk-wnd-ev-min then act desk-step-hide end
        else if ev == desk-wnd-ev-stay then act
          let d = __heap-restore ((bp.bp-st).bs-laid-mark)
          in let p = gbr-first-paint base (box.lr-x) (box.lr-y) (box.lr-w) (box.lr-h) stride font tf mouse cs (ui-scale w) bp
          in 0 - 1
        end
            else if sc == 15 then act desk-step-hide end
        else if sc == 88 then act
          let mark = __heap-save
          in act
            r <- desk-shot base w h stride font ds
            let d = __heap-restore mark
            in 1
          end
        end
        else gbr-step base (box.lr-x) (box.lr-y) (box.lr-w) (box.lr-h) stride font tf mouse cs (ui-scale w) bp sc clicked
      end

 The dispatch is separate from the polling so that the launcher can reach
 it. A pane is chosen by a scancode, and `desk-programs` answers one, so a
 row in the launcher and the key it names arrive here by the same road
 rather than through a second table that has to agree with this one.

 The idle case does NOT go through `desk-dispatch`, and that is load
 bearing rather than an optimisation. This loop polls continuously, and
 routing the idle iteration through a second function makes the recursion
 mutual instead of self, which stops being a tail call: the stack then
 grows once per poll and the desk double-faults (`!EXC=08`, CR2 beside
 RSP) within seconds of boot. Measured, by writing it the other way
 first. Only an actual keystroke or click may cross into the dispatch.

 A step answers 0 to close, 1 to stay open, a NEGATIVE number to stay open
 without the frame restore, and anything else as a scancode to dispatch after
 closing. The scancode arm is unambiguous because every dispatch scancode is
 at least 2.

 The negative answer exists for the one thing the frame bracket cannot serve:
 a step that GREW its own durable state this iteration. The bracket frees
 everything a step allocated, which is what keeps an idle desk flat, and a
 pane like Files that swapped its directory listing for a new one needs that
 listing to survive into the next poll. It cannot be moved below the frame
 mark after the fact, because a bump allocator has save and restore and
 nothing else. So the step keeps the frame instead, and pays for the
 iteration's transient allocations along with the state it meant to keep.
 That is a real cost and it is bounded by how often a human changes
 directory, not by the poll rate. Only a step that actually grew state may
 answer negative; answering it on a repaint would put the idle leak back.

 A CLICK ON THE CHROME WHILE A WINDOW IS FOCUSED GOES TO THE LAUNCHER, and
 without this branch the second window is unreachable. The focused pane steps
 and `desk-dispatch` is only reached when focus is NONE, so with the Clock open
 there was no road to the Calculator: the registry held two slots that nothing
 could fill, the painter drew a stack that could never be more than one deep,
 and every arm short of the pictures would still have passed.

 It goes through the existing dispatch table rather than teaching the window
 chrome which scancodes open which pane, which would put the launcher's
 knowledge in a third place. Opening the second window is then a click on the
 sidebar exactly as it is from the desktop, and the pane that is already open
 keeps its focus id until the new one takes it.

 The condition is deliberately narrow: a CLICK, on no window, while the
 focused pane IS a window. A keystroke is not included, because every
 scancode belongs to the focused pane and a key that opened a pane out from
 under the one reading it is how two panes start disagreeing about who owns
 the keyboard.

 A RESIZE BAND IS NOT THE DESKTOP, and without the last line the outer half of
 every band was unreachable. `desk-wnd-at` answers none for any point outside a
 window's rectangle, so a grab aimed at the OUTSIDE half of the straddle hit no
 window, came through here as a click on the desktop, and went to
 `desk-dispatch` -- the pane's chrome step, which is the only thing that can
 begin a resize, never saw it. Guarding the zone check inside the chrome step
 was not enough on its own because the click never arrived there at all.

  desk-wnd-to-desk : Integer, Integer, Integer, UsbMouse, GopFont, Boolean -> Boolean
  desk-wnd-to-desk (ds) (w) (h) (mouse) (tf) (clicked) =
    let ws = peek-32 ds dk-wr-cell
    in clicked
       & dk-wr-count ws > 0
       & dk-wr-find ws (peek-32 ds desk-focus-cell) 0 >= 0
       & desk-wnd-at w h (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) tf ds ws (dk-wr-count ws - 1) == desk-focus-none
       & dk-size-zone ds w h (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) == dk-size-none

  desk-loop : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-loop (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) = act
    let fmark = __heap-save
    in act
      sc <- kbd-take kbd
      ck <- if dk-drag-fid ds == desk-focus-none then desk-clock base w h stride font tf ds root else act 0 end
      let rr = dk-rate-tick ds
      in let rp = if rr == 1 then desk-rate-paint base w h stride font (ui-scale w) tf cs mouse ds else 0
      in let mv = mouse-pump mouse w h
      in let zs = if mv == 1 & dk-drag-fid ds == desk-focus-none & dk-size-active ds == dk-size-none then cursor-set-shape cs (dk-size-cursor (dk-size-zone ds w h (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse))) else 0
      in let cu = if mv == 1 then cursor-update cs base stride w h (ui-scale w) (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) else 0
      in let clicked = mouse-take-click mouse
      in act
        cl <- dk-snd-click ds clicked
        if desk-wnd-to-desk ds w h mouse tf clicked then
          let d = __heap-restore fmark
          in desk-dispatch base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root sc clicked
        else if peek-32 ds desk-focus-cell /= desk-focus-none then act
          alive <- desk-step-of base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root (peek-32 ds desk-focus-cell) sc clicked
          if alive == 0 then desk-app-close base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps
          else if alive == desk-step-hide then desk-app-hide base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps
          else if alive == 1 then
            let d = __heap-restore fmark
            in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
          else if alive < 0 then desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
          else desk-app-close-to base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps alive
        end
        else if clicked == False & sc /= desk-sc-shot then
          let d = __heap-restore fmark
          in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
        else
          let d = __heap-restore fmark
          in desk-dispatch base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root sc clicked
      end
    end
  end

 WHICH PILL WAS CLICKED, by asking the tree rather than by measuring the band.
 The pill is a button in the taskbar, so `ev-hit-widget` already knows where it
 is; walking the registry and asking for each minimised window's id costs at
 most `dk-wr-max` comparisons and cannot disagree with the paint.

  dk-pill-hit : WidgetNode, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-pill-hit (root) (mx) (my) (ws) (ds) (s) (i) =
    if i >= dk-wr-count ws then desk-focus-none
    else
      let fid = dk-wr-at ws i
      in if desk-widget-hit root mx my (dk-pill-id fid) s then fid
      else dk-pill-hit root mx my ws ds s (i + 1)

 EVERY PILL IS A HIT, not only a minimised one. Damian, 2026-08-26: clicking a
 taskbar pill should make that window active and bring it to the front. The
 filter here was the mirror of the one in `dk-pills`, which only BUILT a pill
 for a minimised window; now that every open window has one, a pill for a
 visible window was a control that drew and did nothing.

 `desk-pill-restore` needed no change for it. Clearing a normal window's state
 to normal is a no-op, and the raise and the focus were always the rest of
 what it does, which is exactly "make it active and bring it to the front".

 RESTORING IS CLEARING THE STATE AND RAISING, and it needs no kept rectangle
 because 6.3c made the normal rect a fact in the pane's own block. The window
 comes back exactly where it was, including a cascade offset or anything a
 later `dk-wnd-place` put there.

  desk-pill-restore : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode, Integer -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
 THE CURSOR IS HIDDEN FIRST, and leaving it out left a smear of the pill on
 the band. The arrow keeps the pixels it covered so it can put them back; a
 repaint underneath a shown cursor is undone by that restore the moment the
 pointer next moves, so the taskbar came back carrying a fragment of the pill
 that had just been removed. Every other repaint path here brackets the cursor
 for the same reason (`works-desk-contract.md` section 5).

  desk-pill-restore (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) (fid) = act
    hd <- act cursor-hide cs base stride end
    let ws = peek-32 ds dk-wr-cell
    in let st = poke-32 (desk-wnd-blk fid ds) dk-wnd-st-cell dk-wnd-st-normal
    in let t = dk-wr-raise ws fid
    in let sf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell fid
    in let root2 = desk-draw base w h stride font (ui-scale w) tf ds
    in act
      p <- desk-wnd-paint-all base w h stride font tf ds
      let c0 = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
      in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root2
    end
  end

  desk-dispatch : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode, Integer, Boolean -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-dispatch (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) (sc) (clicked) =
    let pill = if clicked then dk-pill-hit root (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) (peek-32 ds dk-wr-cell) ds (ui-wscale w) 0 else desk-focus-none
    in if pill /= desk-focus-none then desk-pill-restore base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root pill
    else desk-dispatch-app base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root sc clicked

 The pill is resolved BEFORE the launcher, because a pill sits in the taskbar
 beside the menu button and a restore is not an open. Everything below is the
 dispatch table as it was.

  desk-dispatch-app : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode, Integer, Boolean -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-dispatch-app (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) (sc) (clicked) =
    let files = if sc == 33 then True else clicked & desk-widget-hit root (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) "files" (ui-wscale w)
    in let scene = (if sc == 4 then True else clicked & desk-widget-hit root (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) "scene" (ui-wscale w))
    in let fish = (if sc == 16 then True else clicked & desk-widget-hit root (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) "fish" (ui-wscale w))
    in let mon = if sc == 50 then True else clicked & desk-widget-hit root (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) "monitor" (ui-wscale w)
    in let calc = if sc == 46 then True else clicked & desk-widget-hit root (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) "calc" (ui-wscale w)
    in let con = if sc == 20 then True else clicked & desk-widget-hit root (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) "console" (ui-wscale w)
    in let cal = if sc == 38 then True else clicked & desk-widget-hit root (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) "cal" (ui-wscale w)
    in let trk = if sc == 23 then True else clicked & desk-widget-hit root (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) "issues" (ui-wscale w)
    in let rev = if sc == 19 then True else clicked & desk-widget-hit root (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) "review" (ui-wscale w)
    in let dif = if sc == 32 then True else clicked & desk-widget-hit root (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) "diffusion" (ui-wscale w)
    in let bro = if sc == 48 then True else clicked & desk-widget-hit root (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) "browser" (ui-wscale w)
    in let edt = if sc == 18 then True else clicked & desk-widget-hit root (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) "edit" (ui-wscale w)
    in let sty = if sc == 30 then True else clicked & desk-widget-hit root (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) "style" (ui-wscale w)
    in let bye = sc == dk-sc-bye
    in let prg = if sc == 25 then True else clicked & desk-widget-hit root (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) "programs" (ui-wscale w)
    in let sysm = if sc == 41 then True else clicked & desk-widget-hit root (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) "menu" (ui-wscale w)
    in let clk = if sc == 37 then True else clicked & desk-widget-hit root (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) "clock" (ui-wscale w)
    in if sc == 88 then desk-snap base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
    else if sysm then desk-menu-open base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs (peek-32 ds desk-prog-cell) apps root
    else if prg then desk-programs-open base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs (peek-32 ds desk-prog-cell) apps root
    else if clk then desk-clock-open base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs (peek-32 ds desk-clock-cell) apps root
    else if files then desk-files-focus base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
    else if scene then desk-scene-focus base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root gsc-sky desk-focus-scene
    else if fish then desk-scene-focus base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root gf-sky desk-focus-fish
    else if mon then desk-mon-open base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
    else if calc then desk-calc-open base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs (peek-32 ds 24) apps root
    else if con then desk-console-open base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs (peek-32 ds desk-console-cell) apps root
    else if cal then desk-cal-open base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
    else if trk then desk-trk-open base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
    else if rev then desk-rev-open base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
    else if dif then desk-dif-open base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
    else if bro then desk-browser-focus base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
    else if edt then desk-edit-focus base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
    else if sty then desk-style-open base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
    else if bye then desk-shutdown base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
    else desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
  end

 The size of the desk's type, in DEVICE pixels, and the desk's own number.
 It was `comp-glyph-h * ui-wscale w`, which is 32 at 1600 and gives a 41 row
 cell with 22 pixel capitals against the 9 to 10 of a Windows UI font.
 `comp-glyph-h` could not simply be lowered because it is also the CBF
 fallback's cell height, hardwired to 16 rows in `gop-buf-cbf-rows-scaled`.

 16 and 14 are read off the sweep in `codex/test/apps/desk-leading-metrics`,
 which prints what the face answers at each ppem: 16 gives cap 11 and a 21 row
 cell, 14 gives cap 9 and 18 rows. Both sit in the range the reference does.
 The ppem is NOT multiplied by `ui-wscale`: it is already a device size, and
 doubling it at 1600 is exactly what made the type twice the size it should
 be. The larger of the two goes to the wider screen so the type does not
 shrink in proportion to the chrome around it.

  dk-ui-ppem : Integer -> Integer
  dk-ui-ppem (w) = if w >= 1600 then 16 else 14

 Loaded at the ppem the widget layer will draw it at, which is why this takes
 the display width rather than the ignored argument it used to. `gbf-put-text`
 paints glyph bitmaps at their rasterized size and takes no scale.

  desk-font : Integer -> [Device.Port] GopFont
  desk-font (w) = act
    m <- gfat-mount-esp
    if m.gm-ok == False then gfont-none
    else gfont-load (m.gm-vol) "CMUNSS.TTF" (dk-ui-ppem w)
  end

  desk-run : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
 The calculator's state block is allocated once here and its ADDRESS is
 kept in a `ds` cell, so no pane signature has to grow a parameter. The
 cell is 32-bit: there is no `poke-qword` builtin (only `peek-qword`), and
 a heap address fits because the bare-metal arena tops out below 4 GB --
 `peek-32` zero-extends, so it round-trips exactly rather than sign-
 extending into a negative pointer.

  desk-run (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (kbd) (mouse) = act
    tf <- desk-font w
    gp <- desk-gpu-present 0
    st <- gst-load (gst-store dk-settings-keys)
    let s = ui-scale w
    in let ds = alloc-zeroed 256 64
    in let hs = hpet-start
    in let cs = alloc-zeroed 2048 64
    in let ks = alloc-zeroed 64 64
    in let ns = alloc-zeroed 64 64
    in let kp = poke-32 ds 24 ks
    in let np = poke-32 ds desk-console-cell ns
    in let ep = poke-32 ds desk-edit-cell (alloc-zeroed 192 64)
    in let ci = gcon-init ns
    in let pp = poke-32 ds desk-prog-cell (alloc-zeroed 64 64)
    in let kp2 = poke-32 ds desk-clock-cell (alloc-zeroed 64 64)
    in let rvp = poke-32 ds desk-review-cell (alloc-zeroed 64 64)
    in let rvi = grv-init (peek-32 ds desk-review-cell)
    in let msp = poke-32 ds desk-marks-cell (alloc-zeroed 128 64)
    in let wrp = poke-32 ds dk-wr-cell (alloc-zeroed 64 64)
    in let calp = poke-32 ds dk-cal-cell (alloc-zeroed 64 64)
    in let difp = poke-32 ds dk-dif-cell (alloc-zeroed 64 64)
    in let styp = poke-32 ds dk-sty-cell (alloc-zeroed 64 64)
    in let trkp = poke-32 ds dk-trk-cell (alloc-zeroed 64 64)
    in let monp = poke-32 ds dk-mon-cell (alloc-zeroed 64 64)
    in let scnp = poke-32 ds dk-scene-cell (alloc-zeroed 64 64)
    in let fshp = poke-32 ds dk-fish-cell (alloc-zeroed 64 64)
    in let brop = poke-32 ds dk-bro-cell (alloc-zeroed 64 64)
    in let flsp = poke-32 ds dk-files-cell (alloc-zeroed 64 64)
    in let gpk = poke-32 ds desk-gpu-cell gp
    in let sdi = dk-snd-init ds
    in let ici = dk-icons-init ds tf w
      in let tki = dk-task-init ds tf w
    in let ap = dk-settings-into ds st
    in let wp = mouse-warp mouse (w / 2) (h / 2)
    in let apps = desk-apps-empty
    in let bm = poke-32 ds desk-mark-cell __heap-save
    in let root = desk-draw base w h stride font s tf ds
    in let c0 = cursor-update cs base stride w h s (w / 2) (h / 2)
    in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
  end
